// Auto-generated data for the Hawkeye Sterling advisor.
// REG_GROUPS: regulatory Q&A, each question carries its own cited answer {q, a, refs:[{ref,note}]}.
// SUPER_TOOLS: the Super Tools catalogue (id, label, group) + per-tool deterministic playbook (play:{intro,steps,refs}).
window.REG_GROUPS = [{"label":"UAE FDL & Cabinet Resolution","questions":[{"q":"What is the record-retention period under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.16?","a":"Financial institutions and DNFBPs must retain all records, documents, and data on transactions and customer due diligence for at least five years from the date the transaction is completed or the business relationship ends, whichever is later. Records must be sufficient to reconstruct individual transactions and be made available to the competent authorities promptly on request. The retention obligation also covers correspondence, account files, and the results of any analysis undertaken.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art. 16","note":"Requires retention of transaction and CDD records for a minimum of five years and provision to authorities on request."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 11","note":"Sets the international standard of at least five years record-keeping for transactions and CDD data."}]},{"q":"What are the STR reporting obligations under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"A reporting entity that has reasonable grounds to suspect a transaction, attempted transaction, or funds are linked to a crime, proceeds of crime, money laundering, or terrorist financing must file a Suspicious Transaction Report with the UAE Financial Intelligence Unit without delay. Reporting is required regardless of the amount and even where the transaction was not completed. The entity must also provide any additional information the FIU subsequently requests and must not proceed in a way that defeats the purpose of the report.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Obliges reporting entities to file STRs with the FIU on suspicion of ML, TF, or predicate offences, without a minimum threshold."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting to the FIU where there is suspicion of proceeds of crime or terrorist financing."}]},{"q":"What are the tipping-off prohibitions under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.25?","a":"Reporting entities, their managers, and employees are prohibited from disclosing, directly or indirectly, to the customer or any other person that a suspicious transaction report has been or will be filed, or that an investigation or analysis is underway. The prohibition is intended to prevent prejudicing the FIU's work and applies even after the relationship ends. Breach exposes the individual and entity to criminal and administrative penalties.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art. 25","note":"Prohibits tipping-off the customer or third parties about a filed STR or related investigation."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 21","note":"Requires that staff be protected for reporting and prohibited by law from disclosing (tipping off) that an STR was filed."}]},{"q":"What CDD measures does UAE Cabinet Resolution 134/2025 require?","a":"The Resolution requires identifying and verifying the customer and any beneficial owner using reliable independent source documents, understanding the purpose and intended nature of the business relationship, and conducting ongoing monitoring of transactions against the customer's risk profile. Measures must be applied on a risk-based approach, with enhanced due diligence for higher-risk customers such as PEPs and high-risk jurisdictions, and simplified measures permitted only for proven lower-risk situations. CDD must be undertaken before or during establishment of the relationship and refreshed periodically.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Resolution No. 134 of 2025","note":"Sets out the implementing CDD, beneficial ownership, ongoing monitoring, and risk-based approach requirements."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Defines the core CDD elements: identify and verify customer and beneficial owner, understand purpose, and monitor on a risk basis."}]}]},{"label":"MoE / DPMS","questions":[{"q":"What is the DPMS cash-transaction reporting threshold under MoE Circular 08/2021?","a":"Dealers in precious metals and stones must report to the UAE FIU any cash transaction, or series of linked cash transactions, equal to or above AED 55,000. The dealer files a Dealer in Precious Metals and Stones Report (DPMSR) through the goAML portal and must also apply CDD at that threshold. Linked or structured transactions designed to stay below the limit should still be reported.","refs":[{"ref":"MoE Circular No. 08 of 2021","note":"Establishes the AED 55,000 cash-transaction reporting obligation for DPMS to the FIU via goAML."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Brings dealers in precious metals and stones into CDD obligations for cash transactions at or above the designated threshold."}]},{"q":"What CDD applies to a UAE gold trader under MoE DPMS rules?","a":"A gold trader must identify and verify the customer and any beneficial owner, and apply full CDD whenever it engages in a cash transaction at or above AED 55,000, or whenever ML/TF is suspected or the integrity of identification data is doubted. This includes understanding the purpose of the dealing, screening against sanctions lists, and conducting ongoing monitoring on a risk-based basis with EDD for higher-risk customers and jurisdictions. Records of CDD must be retained for at least five years.","refs":[{"ref":"MoE DPMS AML/CFT Guidance and Circular No. 08 of 2021","note":"Requires CDD on DPMS cash transactions at or above AED 55,000 and on suspicion."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Applies CDD requirements (Rec 10) to dealers in precious metals and stones."}]},{"q":"Is goAML registration mandatory for MoE-supervised DPMS?","a":"Yes. MoE-supervised dealers in precious metals and stones must register on the UAE FIU's goAML platform, which is the mandatory channel for filing STRs, DPMSRs, and other reports. Failure to register is itself a compliance breach and exposes the dealer to administrative penalties. Registration must be completed even if the dealer has not yet had a reportable transaction.","refs":[{"ref":"MoE DPMS AML/CFT Guidance","note":"Requires DPMS to register on goAML as the mandatory reporting channel."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Underlies the obligation to file reports with the FIU through its designated system."}]},{"q":"What red flags must DPMS dealers monitor under MoE guidance?","a":"Dealers should monitor for customers paying large amounts in cash or structuring payments just below AED 55,000, reluctance to provide identification or beneficial owner details, purchases inconsistent with the customer's profile, and requests for unusual settlement such as third-party or cross-border transfers. Other indicators include nervousness about record-keeping, rapid buying and reselling of high-value items, dealings linked to high-risk jurisdictions or sanctioned parties, and unclear source of funds. Any such red flag should prompt EDD and, if suspicion remains, an STR.","refs":[{"ref":"MoE DPMS AML/CFT Guidance","note":"Lists indicators such as structuring, cash intensity, reluctance to identify, and inconsistent purchases that DPMS must monitor."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires filing an STR where monitoring identifies suspicion of ML/TF."}]}]},{"label":"UAE FIU / goAML","questions":[{"q":"What is the STR submission timeline to the UAE FIU via goAML?","a":"An STR must be filed without delay as soon as the reporting entity forms a reasonable suspicion; there is no fixed grace period and the expectation is immediate submission once suspicion crystallises. The report is lodged electronically through the goAML web portal. Delaying submission to gather further internal comfort is not a substitute for prompt filing, and the FIU may request additional information thereafter.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE FIU goAML reporting requirements","note":"STRs must be submitted promptly and electronically via goAML once suspicion arises."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting to the FIU promptly upon forming a suspicion."}]},{"q":"When must an Additional Information File (AIF) be filed via goAML?","a":"An Additional Information File is filed when the FIU issues a request for further information relating to a previously submitted report, or when the reporting entity itself obtains material new information that supplements an existing STR. The AIF links to the original report reference and must be submitted promptly within any deadline the FIU specifies. It is not used to open a new suspicion, which would require a fresh STR.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE FIU goAML reporting guidance","note":"The AIF is the goAML message type used to respond to FIU information requests or supplement an existing report."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Obliges entities to provide the FIU with additional information on request."}]},{"q":"What narrative elements must a goAML STR contain?","a":"The STR narrative should clearly state who is involved, what happened, when and where, how the transaction was conducted, and crucially why it is considered suspicious, linking the facts to specific red flags or typologies. It should describe the parties, accounts, amounts, dates, and source and destination of funds, and attach supporting documents. The grounds for suspicion must be articulated in the dealer's own analysis rather than left implicit.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE FIU goAML STR guidance","note":"Requires a clear narrative covering the parties, transaction details, and the specific reasons for suspicion with supporting documents."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Underpins the requirement that reports convey the basis of the suspicion to the FIU."}]},{"q":"Can a reporting entity disclose an STR to the subject?","a":"No. Disclosing to the customer or any third party that an STR has been or will be filed is prohibited tipping-off and is a criminal offence. The reporting entity, its directors, and employees must keep the existence and content of the report confidential. Internal sharing is limited to those who need to know for compliance purposes.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art. 25","note":"Prohibits disclosing to the subject or others that an STR has been filed (tipping-off)."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 21","note":"Requires a legal prohibition on tipping-off and protection for good-faith reporting."}]}]},{"label":"EOCN Sanctions","questions":[{"q":"How often must we re-screen against the UAE EOCN consolidated list?","a":"Entities must screen customers and transactions against the UAE Local Terrorist List and the UN Consolidated List at onboarding and continuously thereafter, and must re-screen the entire customer base without delay each time either list is updated. Because UN and UAE listings can change at any time, firms should subscribe to EOCN notifications and run screening on an ongoing, automated basis rather than on a fixed periodic cycle. New names must be checked against existing customers immediately upon publication.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE EOCN Guidance on Targeted Financial Sanctions","note":"Requires continuous screening and prompt re-screening of the customer base whenever the consolidated list is updated."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 6 and 7","note":"Require implementation of targeted financial sanctions without delay following UN designations."}]},{"q":"What is the freezing-action timeline under EOCN guidance?","a":"On a positive match against a designated person or entity, the firm must freeze the funds and assets without delay and without prior notice, in practice within hours and treated as an immediate obligation. The freeze must be reported to the Executive Office and the relevant supervisor, typically within the timeframe set in the guidance (generally within five business days of taking action), and no funds may be made available to the listed party. Asset freezing applies to all funds owned or controlled directly or indirectly by the designated party.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE EOCN Guidance on Targeted Financial Sanctions","note":"Requires freezing without delay and without prior notice, with prompt reporting of the freeze to the Executive Office and supervisor."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 6 and 7","note":"Mandate freezing of designated persons' assets without delay."}]},{"q":"What records must be kept on EOCN sanctions screening?","a":"Firms must retain records evidencing that screening was performed, including the lists and versions checked, dates and times of screening, the matching logic used, and the outcome of each check including discounted false positives with the reasoning. Records of any freeze, partial unfreeze, or report to the Executive Office must also be kept. These records must be retained for at least five years and made available to supervisors on request.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE EOCN Guidance on Targeted Financial Sanctions","note":"Requires documented evidence of screening, match decisions, and freezing actions."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art. 16","note":"Imposes the five-year record-retention requirement applicable to screening records."}]},{"q":"How do we handle a true positive against the EOCN list?","a":"Confirm the match is genuine, then immediately freeze all funds and assets of the designated party without tipping them off, and refrain from executing any pending transaction. Notify the Executive Office and your supervisor within the prescribed timeframe and file an STR with the FIU via goAML where appropriate. Continue the freeze until the Executive Office or competent authority directs otherwise, and document every step.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE EOCN Guidance on Targeted Financial Sanctions","note":"Sets the steps for confirmed matches: freeze without delay, no dealing, and report to the Executive Office."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 6 and 7","note":"Require freezing of confirmed designated parties' assets without delay."}]}]},{"label":"LBMA Responsible Gold","questions":[{"q":"What does the LBMA Responsible Gold Guidance require at Step 1?","a":"Step 1 requires the refiner to establish a strong company management system, including adopting and communicating a responsible sourcing policy consistent with the OECD Annex II model, assigning senior management responsibility, building internal traceability and record-keeping over the gold supply chain, strengthening engagement with suppliers, and establishing a grievance mechanism. It sets the governance foundation on which all later steps depend. Records supporting the management system must be maintained for at least five years.","refs":[{"ref":"LBMA Responsible Gold Guidance, Step 1","note":"Requires a company management system: policy, senior accountability, internal traceability, supplier KYC framework, and grievance mechanism."},{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance, Step 1","note":"LBMA Step 1 mirrors the OECD requirement to establish strong company management systems."}]},{"q":"What KYC standards apply under the LBMA RGG?","a":"Refiners must conduct KYC on all counterparties before transacting, identifying and verifying the counterparty's legal identity, ownership and beneficial owners, the nature of its business and source of gold, and screening against sanctions and watch lists. Higher-risk counterparties require enhanced due diligence and ongoing monitoring, and counterparties must be assessed against the refiner's gold supply chain policy. KYC records must be retained for at least five years.","refs":[{"ref":"LBMA Responsible Gold Guidance KYC requirements","note":"Requires identification, verification, beneficial ownership, sanctions screening, and risk-based EDD on counterparties."},{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance, Annex II","note":"Provides the model supply chain policy against which counterparty KYC is assessed."}]},{"q":"What is the LBMA Step 3 supplier audit obligation?","a":"Step 3 requires the refiner to design and implement a strategy to respond to identified risks, including a risk management plan reported to senior management. Where higher risks are found, the refiner must take measures including enhanced monitoring and, in some cases, supplier or supply-chain audits or on-the-ground assessment, and decide whether to mitigate while continuing, suspend, or disengage. The risk responses and outcomes must be documented.","refs":[{"ref":"LBMA Responsible Gold Guidance, Step 3","note":"Requires a risk management strategy with mitigation, monitoring, and where needed supplier audit or assessment of higher-risk supply."},{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance, Step 3","note":"Corresponds to designing and implementing a strategy to respond to identified supply chain risks."}]},{"q":"What public reporting does LBMA Step 5 require?","a":"Step 5 requires the refiner to report publicly on its supply chain due diligence each year, typically through a Compliance Report and the auditor's Assurance Report published on the LBMA Good Delivery framework. The report should describe the company's management systems, risk assessment, risk management, and the steps taken to implement the guidance, without disclosing commercially sensitive or security-sensitive information. This supports transparency and external verification.","refs":[{"ref":"LBMA Responsible Gold Guidance, Step 5","note":"Requires annual public reporting on supply chain due diligence via a Compliance Report and independent assurance."},{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance, Step 5","note":"Mirrors the requirement to report publicly on supply chain due diligence."}]}]},{"label":"OECD CAHRA Due Diligence","questions":[{"q":"What are the 5 steps of the OECD Due Diligence Guidance?","a":"The five steps are: Step 1 establish strong company management systems; Step 2 identify and assess risks in the supply chain; Step 3 design and implement a strategy to respond to identified risks; Step 4 carry out an independent third-party audit of supply chain due diligence at identified points; and Step 5 report publicly on supply chain due diligence. The framework is risk-based and applies to minerals from conflict-affected and high-risk areas (CAHRA).","refs":[{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible Supply Chains of Minerals","note":"Sets out the five-step risk-based due diligence framework for CAHRA minerals."}]},{"q":"What red flags trigger Step 3 enhanced due diligence under OECD guidance?","a":"Red flags include minerals originating from or transiting a conflict-affected or high-risk area, minerals from a country with limited known reserves or implausible production volumes, and suppliers or upstream parties known to have sourced from a red-flag location or linked to sanctions, armed groups, or serious human rights abuses. Such flags require the company to intensify due diligence, map the supply chain, and assess risks on the ground before deciding to mitigate, continue, suspend, or disengage. Findings feed the risk management plan reported to senior management.","refs":[{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance, Annex II and Step 3","note":"Defines red-flag locations, suppliers, and circumstances that trigger enhanced due diligence and risk response."}]},{"q":"What disengagement criteria does OECD guidance set for high-risk suppliers?","a":"A company must suspend or disengage from a supplier where there is a reasonable risk of contributing to the worst forms of abuse covered by Annex II, such as torture, forced or child labour, war crimes, or direct or indirect support to non-state armed groups or public or private security forces that illegally control or tax mine sites or transport routes. For other risks, the company may continue trade while implementing a measurable risk mitigation plan, but must disengage if mitigation fails after a reasonable period. Decisions must be documented and reviewed.","refs":[{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance, Annex II Model Policy","note":"Requires immediate disengagement for the worst abuses and disengagement where time-bound mitigation of other risks fails."}]},{"q":"What public reporting does OECD Step 5 require?","a":"Step 5 requires companies to report publicly on their supply chain due diligence policies and practices, with sufficient detail to allow stakeholders to evaluate the company's conduct, while protecting commercially confidential and personal information. Reporting should cover the management systems, risk assessment and findings, risk management measures taken, and the results of independent audits. It should be published at least annually.","refs":[{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance, Step 5","note":"Requires annual public reporting on supply chain due diligence covering management, risk assessment, response, and audit results."}]}]},{"label":"FATF Recommendations","questions":[{"q":"What does FATF Recommendation 16 require for wire transfers?","a":"Recommendation 16, the travel rule, requires that cross-border and certain domestic wire transfers carry accurate and complete originator and beneficiary information throughout the payment chain. Ordering, intermediary, and beneficiary institutions must obtain, transmit, and retain this information, screen against sanctions lists, and take risk-based action on transfers lacking required information. The standard applies to a de minimis threshold of around USD/EUR 1,000 for cross-border transfers, with reduced data below that level.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Requires originator and beneficiary information to accompany wire transfers and obliges institutions to monitor and screen them."}]},{"q":"What does FATF Recommendation 12 require for PEPs?","a":"Recommendation 12 requires institutions to put in place risk management systems to determine whether a customer or beneficial owner is a politically exposed person. For foreign PEPs they must obtain senior management approval to establish or continue the relationship, take reasonable measures to establish the source of wealth and funds, and conduct enhanced ongoing monitoring. For domestic PEPs and those entrusted by international organisations, enhanced measures apply where the relationship is higher risk, and the requirements extend to family members and close associates.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires PEP identification, senior management approval, source of wealth and funds checks, and enhanced ongoing monitoring."}]},{"q":"What does FATF Recommendation 15 require of VASPs?","a":"Recommendation 15 requires countries to identify and regulate virtual asset service providers, license or register them, and subject them to AML/CFT supervision. VASPs must apply CDD, record-keeping, suspicious transaction reporting, and the travel rule (originator and beneficiary information) to virtual asset transfers, and countries must assess and mitigate the ML/TF risks of new technologies. The 2019 interpretive note extended the full set of AML/CFT obligations to the virtual asset sector.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15 and its Interpretive Note","note":"Requires VASPs to be licensed or registered, supervised, and to apply CDD, recordkeeping, STR, and travel-rule obligations."}]},{"q":"What ownership threshold does FATF Recommendation 24 apply to UBOs?","a":"Recommendation 24 addresses transparency of beneficial ownership of legal persons; a beneficial owner is the natural person who ultimately owns or controls the entity. While the FATF standard is principles-based and does not fix a single universal figure, the commonly applied benchmark for a controlling ownership interest is a holding of more than 25 percent, after which control through other means is considered. Countries and obliged entities must maintain adequate, accurate, and up-to-date beneficial ownership information accessible to authorities.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires adequate, accurate, and timely beneficial ownership information; the 25 percent benchmark is the common controlling-interest threshold."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10 Interpretive Note","note":"Describes identifying beneficial owners through ownership (commonly above 25 percent) and otherwise through control."}]}]},{"label":"RMI Minerals","questions":[{"q":"What does the RMI Responsible Minerals Assurance Process (RMAP) require?","a":"RMAP is an independent third-party audit programme through which smelters and refiners demonstrate that their sourcing of minerals such as tin, tantalum, tungsten, gold, and cobalt is conducted responsibly and in line with OECD due diligence. It assesses the company's management systems, supply chain risk assessment, and risk mitigation, and confers conformant status when standards are met. Conformance must be maintained through periodic reassessment.","refs":[{"ref":"RMI Responsible Minerals Assurance Process (RMAP) standards","note":"Defines the independent audit by which smelters and refiners demonstrate OECD-aligned responsible sourcing."},{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance, Step 4","note":"RMAP operationalises the independent third-party audit step for upstream actors."}]},{"q":"What is the RMI Conflict Minerals Reporting Template (CMRT) used for?","a":"The CMRT is a standardised free reporting template used to collect information through the supply chain on the smelters and refiners used and the country of origin of tin, tantalum, tungsten, and gold (3TG). Companies use it to gather and pass sourcing data to customers, support OECD due diligence, and meet disclosure obligations such as those under conflict minerals regulations. A companion Extended Minerals Reporting Template covers additional minerals such as cobalt and mica.","refs":[{"ref":"RMI Conflict Minerals Reporting Template (CMRT)","note":"Standard template to collect and communicate 3TG smelter and country-of-origin data across the supply chain."},{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance, Step 2","note":"CMRT supports identifying and assessing supply chain risks through smelter and origin mapping."}]},{"q":"What is the RMI smelter audit cadence?","a":"Under RMAP, a conformant smelter or refiner is generally re-audited on a periodic cycle, with full reassessment typically every three years, subject to surveillance or earlier reassessment where risk indicators or material changes arise. New applicants undergo an initial audit before conformant status is granted. The cadence ensures sourcing controls remain effective over time.","refs":[{"ref":"RMI RMAP audit protocols","note":"Sets the recurring reassessment cycle (commonly three-yearly) for maintaining smelter conformant status."},{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance, Step 4","note":"Supports the principle of recurring independent audit of supply chain due diligence."}]},{"q":"How does RMI align with OECD 5-step due diligence?","a":"RMI tools and standards are explicitly built on the OECD Due Diligence Guidance: the RMAP standards reflect the management systems, risk assessment, and risk response of Steps 1 to 3, the RMAP audit itself delivers the Step 4 independent third-party audit, and conformance disclosure and the CMRT support Step 2 risk identification and Step 5 reporting. This alignment lets downstream companies rely on RMI conformance to evidence their own OECD-aligned due diligence.","refs":[{"ref":"RMI alignment with OECD Due Diligence Guidance","note":"RMI standards and RMAP are designed to implement the OECD five-step framework, especially the Step 4 audit."},{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible Supply Chains of Minerals","note":"Provides the five-step framework that RMI tools operationalise."}]}]},{"label":"ESG & Sustainability","questions":[{"q":"What does the EU Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence Directive (CSDDD) require?","a":"The CSDDD (Directive (EU) 2024/1760) requires in-scope large companies to identify, prevent, mitigate, bring to an end and account for actual and potential adverse human rights and environmental impacts across their own operations, subsidiaries and chains of activities. Companies must adopt a risk-based due diligence policy, integrate it into all corporate policies, take appropriate measures, provide remediation, and run complaints and monitoring mechanisms. In-scope firms must also adopt a climate transition plan aligned with limiting warming to 1.5 degrees Celsius.","refs":[{"ref":"Directive (EU) 2024/1760 (CSDDD), Arts. 5-16","note":"Core due diligence duty covering identification, prevention, remediation, complaints and climate transition plans across the chain of activities."},{"ref":"OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises (2023)","note":"Provides the risk-based due diligence framework the CSDDD draws on."}]},{"q":"What due diligence does the EU Conflict Minerals Regulation 2017/821 require?","a":"Regulation (EU) 2017/821 requires EU importers of tin, tantalum, tungsten and gold (3TG) above set volume thresholds to carry out supply chain due diligence aligned with the OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible Supply Chains of Minerals from Conflict-Affected and High-Risk Areas. Importers must establish management systems, identify and assess risk in their supply chain, design a strategy to respond to risks, carry out independent third-party audits, and publicly report annually on their due diligence.","refs":[{"ref":"Regulation (EU) 2017/821, Arts. 4-7","note":"Sets management-system, risk-assessment, third-party audit and disclosure obligations for 3TG importers."},{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible Supply Chains of Minerals (3rd ed.)","note":"The five-step framework the Regulation mandates importers to follow."}]},{"q":"What does CSRD / ESRS S1 require on workforce disclosures?","a":"Under the Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive, ESRS S1 (Own Workforce) requires companies to disclose how their own workforce is affected by material impacts, risks and opportunities, and the measures taken to address them. Disclosures cover working conditions, equal treatment and opportunities, including pay, collective bargaining coverage, health and safety, work-life balance, diversity, and other work-related rights, together with relevant policies, targets and metrics. Reporting follows the double materiality principle.","refs":[{"ref":"Directive (EU) 2022/2464 (CSRD)","note":"Establishes mandatory sustainability reporting under the European Sustainability Reporting Standards."},{"ref":"ESRS S1 (Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2023/2772)","note":"Specifies own-workforce disclosure requirements including working conditions, equal treatment and other work-related rights."}]},{"q":"How does OECD CAHRA guidance link to ESG due diligence?","a":"Conflict-Affected and High-Risk Areas (CAHRA) are identified by the presence of armed conflict, widespread violence or other risks of serious harm to people, and the OECD Due Diligence Guidance directs enterprises to apply heightened, risk-based due diligence when sourcing from them. This connects directly to ESG frameworks because EU instruments such as the Conflict Minerals Regulation and the CSDDD adopt the OECD approach, requiring enhanced scrutiny of supply chains touching CAHRA. For DPMS, gold sourced from CAHRA triggers the strongest red-flag review and enhanced supply chain checks.","refs":[{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible Supply Chains of Minerals (Annexes I-II)","note":"Defines CAHRA and sets the heightened five-step due diligence enterprises must apply when sourcing from them."},{"ref":"Regulation (EU) 2017/821, Art. 2","note":"Adopts the CAHRA concept and the EU CAHRA indicative list for mineral importers."}]}]},{"label":"Human Rights","questions":[{"q":"What do the UN Guiding Principles on Business & Human Rights (UNGPs) require?","a":"The UNGPs rest on three pillars: the State duty to protect human rights, the corporate responsibility to respect human rights, and access to remedy for victims. Businesses are expected to avoid causing or contributing to adverse human rights impacts, address impacts that occur, and seek to prevent or mitigate impacts directly linked to their operations, products or services through human rights due diligence. Companies should also have a policy commitment and processes to remediate harms they cause or contribute to.","refs":[{"ref":"UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights (2011), Principles 11-24","note":"Set out the corporate responsibility to respect human rights and the operational principles for meeting it."},{"ref":"UNGPs, Principles 1-10 and 25-31","note":"State duty to protect and access to remedy pillars."}]},{"q":"What are the steps of the UNGP human-rights due-diligence cycle?","a":"Human rights due diligence under the UNGPs is an ongoing cycle of four steps: identify and assess actual and potential human rights impacts; integrate the findings and take appropriate action to prevent and mitigate them; track the effectiveness of the response; and communicate externally how impacts are addressed. The process should cover impacts the company causes, contributes to, or is directly linked to, and should be repeated as risks evolve.","refs":[{"ref":"UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights (2011), Principles 17-21","note":"Define the four-step human rights due diligence process and its ongoing, risk-based nature."}]},{"q":"What do the OECD Guidelines for MNEs require on human rights?","a":"The OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises on Responsible Business Conduct require enterprises to respect internationally recognised human rights, avoid causing or contributing to adverse impacts, and address such impacts when they occur. Enterprises must carry out risk-based human rights due diligence, seek to prevent or mitigate impacts directly linked to their operations, products or services through business relationships, and provide for or cooperate in remediation where they cause or contribute to harm. The Guidelines are backed by National Contact Points handling specific instances.","refs":[{"ref":"OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises (2023), Chapter IV (Human Rights)","note":"Requires respect for human rights, due diligence, and remediation consistent with the UNGPs."},{"ref":"OECD Guidelines, Chapter II (General Policies)","note":"Establishes the overarching risk-based due diligence expectation and National Contact Point grievance mechanism."}]},{"q":"What modern-slavery red flags must DPMS supply chains monitor?","a":"DPMS businesses should monitor for indicators of forced labour and human trafficking in their gold and gemstone supply chains, including sourcing from conflict-affected or high-risk artisanal mining areas, unexplained or abnormally low labour costs, recruitment fees charged to workers, withheld identity documents, restricted worker movement, and the use of child labour. Opaque or rapidly changing intermediaries, refusal to disclose mine of origin, and inconsistent chain-of-custody documentation are also red flags warranting enhanced supply chain due diligence.","refs":[{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible Supply Chains of Minerals, Annex II","note":"Lists serious abuses including forced labour and the worst forms of child labour as zero-tolerance supply chain risks."},{"ref":"ILO Indicators of Forced Labour","note":"Provides the recognised red-flag indicators such as document retention, debt bondage and restriction of movement."}]}]},{"label":"UN Sanctions & Standards","questions":[{"q":"How is the UN Security Council Consolidated List implemented in the UAE?","a":"The UAE implements the UN Security Council Consolidated List through its Cabinet Decision on the Implementation of UN Security Council Resolutions on the suppression and combating of terrorism and financing of proliferation, administered via the Executive Office for Control and Non-Proliferation. Designations on the UN list are reflected on the UAE Local Terrorist List and Sanctions List, and all regulated entities must screen against them and freeze without delay any funds of designated persons. The framework requires action within prescribed short timelines and reporting to the supervisory authority and FIU.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 on UN Security Council Resolutions and Targeted Financial Sanctions","note":"Establishes the mechanism for adopting UN Consolidated List designations and the without-delay freeze and reporting duties."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Requires implementation of UN-mandated targeted financial sanctions related to terrorism and terrorist financing."}]},{"q":"What does UNSCR 1373 require on terrorist financing?","a":"UNSCR 1373 (2001) obliges all States to criminalise the wilful provision or collection of funds intended for terrorist acts, to freeze without delay the funds and assets of persons who commit or facilitate terrorist acts, and to prohibit making funds available to them. Unlike the 1267 regime, it requires States to maintain their own domestic designation mechanism rather than relying solely on a UN list. It also requires denial of safe haven and broad international cooperation and information exchange.","refs":[{"ref":"UNSCR 1373 (2001), paras. 1-2","note":"Mandates criminalisation, asset freezing, and prohibition on making funds available to terrorists, plus domestic designation."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Implements 1373-type domestic targeted financial sanctions for terrorism."}]},{"q":"What does UNSCR 1540 require on WMD non-proliferation financing?","a":"UNSCR 1540 (2004) requires all States to refrain from supporting non-State actors that seek to develop or acquire nuclear, chemical or biological weapons and their means of delivery, and to adopt and enforce laws prohibiting such activity. Relevant to financing, States must establish controls including measures to account for and secure such items and to prevent their financing, including export and trans-shipment controls and enforcement against illicit financing and brokering. It underpins the proliferation-financing controls applied to dual-use goods.","refs":[{"ref":"UNSCR 1540 (2004), Operative paras. 1-3","note":"Requires States to prohibit and prevent non-State actor access to WMD, including related financing and export controls."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7","note":"Targeted financial sanctions related to proliferation that complement the 1540 framework."}]},{"q":"What does the UN Global Compact require on anti-corruption (Principle 10)?","a":"Principle 10 of the UN Global Compact requires participating businesses to work against corruption in all its forms, including extortion and bribery. In practice this means companies should adopt internal anti-corruption policies and programmes, conduct risk assessments, implement controls and training, and collaborate on anti-corruption efforts within their industry and with stakeholders. It aligns with the UN Convention against Corruption as the underlying international standard.","refs":[{"ref":"UN Global Compact, Principle 10","note":"Commits signatories to work against corruption including extortion and bribery."},{"ref":"UN Convention against Corruption (UNCAC, 2003)","note":"International legal framework underpinning Principle 10's anti-bribery expectations."}]}]},{"label":"Terrorism Financing","questions":[{"q":"What are the FATF R.5 elements for the TF criminal offence?","a":"FATF Recommendation 5 requires countries to criminalise terrorist financing on the basis of the Terrorist Financing Convention, extending the offence to anyone who wilfully provides or collects funds or assets, by any means, directly or indirectly, with the unlawful intention or knowledge that they will be used to carry out terrorist acts, by a terrorist organisation, or by an individual terrorist, even absent a link to a specific act. The offence should extend to any funds whether from legitimate or illegitimate sources, and should not require that the funds were actually used. TF should be a predicate offence for money laundering and apply to natural and legal persons with proportionate, dissuasive sanctions.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 5 and Interpretive Note","note":"Defines the scope of the TF offence covering funding of acts, organisations and individual terrorists regardless of source or use."},{"ref":"International Convention for the Suppression of the Financing of Terrorism (1999)","note":"Treaty basis for the elements of the TF offence."}]},{"q":"What is the UAE CTF Law No. 7/2014 freeze obligation for UNSCR 1267 designations?","a":"Under Federal Law No. 7 of 2014 on Combating Terrorism Offences and its implementing Cabinet Decision, persons and entities on the UN 1267 Sanctions List are reflected on the UAE Sanctions List, and all natural and legal persons must freeze without delay, and without prior notice, all funds and assets owned or controlled by the designated party. No funds may be made available directly or indirectly to or for the benefit of such persons, and the freeze must be reported to the Executive Office and the FIU within the prescribed timeframe.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Law No. 7 of 2014 on Combating Terrorism Offences","note":"Establishes the legal basis for designating and freezing assets of terrorist persons and entities."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 (TFS implementation)","note":"Requires implementation of UN 1267 listings via without-delay freezing and reporting."}]},{"q":"What TF red flags must DPMS dealers monitor under FATF guidance?","a":"DPMS dealers should monitor for transactions that appear inconsistent with a customer's profile, purchases of high-value gold or stones funded by multiple small or third-party payments, reluctance to provide identification or beneficial ownership details, and links to jurisdictions or persons associated with terrorism. Other red flags include rapid buy-and-resell of easily portable high-value items, use of cash to convert value into transportable assets, and dealings connected to conflict zones or charities suspected of TF abuse. Any matched name against terrorist lists is an immediate trigger.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Risk-Based Approach Guidance for Dealers in Precious Metals and Stones (2008)","note":"Identifies sector-specific ML/TF red flags and risk indicators for DPMS."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Applies customer due diligence and record-keeping to DPMS, including for the high-value transactions used to spot red flags."}]},{"q":"How does hawala/IVTS create TF exposure under FATF R.14?","a":"Hawala and other informal value transfer systems move value outside the regulated banking channel, often with limited records and settlement through trust or trade, which can obscure the origin, destination and purpose of funds and facilitate terrorist financing. FATF Recommendation 14 therefore requires that money or value transfer service providers be licensed or registered, subject to AML/CFT obligations and monitoring, with sanctions for those operating illegally. Unregistered hawala operators are a key TF exposure point because they can bypass screening and reporting controls.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 14 and Interpretive Note","note":"Requires licensing or registration and AML/CFT supervision of money or value transfer services, including hawala."},{"ref":"UAE Registered Hawala Providers Regulation (CBUAE)","note":"Implements registration and reporting requirements for hawala providers operating in the UAE."}]},{"q":"When does a UNSCR 1267/1988 hit require immediate freeze without court order?","a":"Targeted financial sanctions under UNSCR 1267 and 1988 require an administrative freeze without delay and without prior notice to the designated party, and crucially without the need for any prior judicial decision. A confirmed match against the UN Sanctions List, reflected on the UAE list, obliges the entity to freeze all funds and assets immediately, typically within hours, and to report. The without-delay obligation exists precisely to prevent dissipation of assets before any court process.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6 and Interpretive Note","note":"Requires freezing without delay and without prior notice, not contingent on a judicial decision."},{"ref":"UNSCR 1267 (1999) and 1988 (2011)","note":"Establish the obligation to freeze assets of listed persons immediately upon designation."}]},{"q":"What does UNSCR 2178 require on foreign terrorist fighter financing?","a":"UNSCR 2178 (2014) requires States to prevent and suppress the recruiting, organising, transporting and equipping of foreign terrorist fighters and the financing of their travel and activities. States must criminalise the wilful provision or collection of funds to finance the travel of individuals to a State other than their residence or nationality for the purpose of perpetrating, planning, preparing or participating in terrorist acts or providing or receiving terrorist training. It builds on the 1373 financing obligations with a specific focus on travel financing.","refs":[{"ref":"UNSCR 2178 (2014), paras. 5-6","note":"Requires criminalisation of financing the travel of foreign terrorist fighters."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 5","note":"TF offence framework that countries extend to cover foreign terrorist fighter financing."}]}]},{"label":"Proliferation Financing","questions":[{"q":"What does FATF R.7 require on targeted financial sanctions for proliferation?","a":"FATF Recommendation 7 requires countries to implement targeted financial sanctions to comply with UN Security Council resolutions on the prevention, suppression and disruption of proliferation of weapons of mass destruction and its financing. This means freezing without delay the funds and assets of persons and entities designated under the relevant UN resolutions (notably the DPRK and Iran regimes) and prohibiting the making available of funds or assets to them, directly or indirectly. Implementation must be immediate and not require a prior court order.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7 and Interpretive Note","note":"Mandates without-delay targeted financial sanctions implementing UN proliferation-financing resolutions."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 (TFS implementation)","note":"UAE mechanism applying proliferation-financing TFS domestically."}]},{"q":"What DPRK transactions are prohibited under UNSCR 1718 and 2375?","a":"UNSCR 1718 (2006) and successor resolutions prohibit trade with the DPRK in arms and related materiel, nuclear and ballistic missile related items and dual-use goods, luxury goods, and the assets of designated persons and entities. UNSCR 2375 (2017) further bans textile exports from the DPRK, caps refined and crude petroleum supplies to the DPRK, prohibits new joint ventures and the provision of work authorisations for DPRK nationals abroad, and reinforces vessel inspection and bunkering prohibitions. Financial services and asset provision supporting any of these activities are prohibited.","refs":[{"ref":"UNSCR 1718 (2006)","note":"Establishes arms, WMD-related, dual-use and luxury goods embargoes and asset freezes on the DPRK."},{"ref":"UNSCR 2375 (2017)","note":"Adds textile export ban, petroleum caps, joint venture and labour prohibitions on the DPRK."}]},{"q":"What dual-use goods trigger UAE Cabinet Decision 57/2020 export controls?","a":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 57 of 2020 regulates the export, re-export, transit and trans-shipment of dual-use goods, being items that have both civilian and potential military or WMD applications, as set out in the UAE national control list aligned with international export control regimes. Triggered goods include controlled chemicals, nuclear and missile-related materials and technology, certain electronics, sensors, navigation and aerospace components, and related software and technology. Such transactions require a permit from the competent authority and screening against proliferation risk before export.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 57 of 2020 on Controlling the Export and Import of Dual-Use Items","note":"Establishes licensing and control over dual-use goods on the national control list."},{"ref":"UNSCR 1540 (2004)","note":"Underpins the requirement for export and trans-shipment controls on WMD-relevant dual-use items."}]},{"q":"How does UNSCR 2231 restrict Iran-related financial transactions?","a":"UNSCR 2231 (2015) endorsed the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action and replaced earlier Iran sanctions resolutions while retaining specific restrictions. It maintains asset freezes and a procurement channel requiring Security Council approval for transfers of nuclear-related and certain dual-use items, and it preserved restrictions on ballistic-missile-related and arms-related transfers for defined periods. Financial transactions linked to designated persons or to controlled proliferation-sensitive transfers remain prohibited or subject to prior approval.","refs":[{"ref":"UNSCR 2231 (2015) and its Annexes","note":"Sets the procurement channel, asset freezes and missile and arms-related restrictions for Iran."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7","note":"Requires implementation of the targeted financial sanctions retained under the resolution."}]},{"q":"What are the 50% rule implications for DPRK-owned entities?","a":"Under the ownership-and-control approach to sanctions, an entity that is owned or controlled by a designated person is itself treated as subject to the freeze even if not separately listed. Many regimes apply a 50 percent aggregation rule, so an entity owned 50 percent or more, directly or indirectly, by one or more designated DPRK persons is blocked, and funds must not be made available to it. Compliance therefore requires looking through ownership structures to identify control by designated DPRK parties, not just matching named entities.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7 Interpretive Note (owned or controlled)","note":"Extends the freeze to entities owned or controlled by designated persons."},{"ref":"UNSCR 2270 (2016) and 2321 (2016)","note":"Reinforce that DPRK entities acting on behalf of or controlled by designated parties are subject to asset freezes."}]},{"q":"What does FATF's 2018 Guidance on Proliferation Financing Risk Assessment require?","a":"The FATF guidance directs countries and private-sector entities to understand and assess their exposure to proliferation financing, focusing on the risk of breaching or evading the targeted financial sanctions under Recommendation 7. It emphasises identifying sanctions-evasion typologies such as front companies, complex ownership, trade-based concealment and misuse of the financial system, and applying mitigating controls including robust screening, beneficial ownership checks and trade-finance scrutiny. Following the 2020 revisions to Recommendations 1 and 2, entities are also expected to identify, assess and mitigate PF risk and ensure coordination at national level.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Guidance on Counter Proliferation Financing (2018)","note":"Sets expectations for assessing and mitigating sanctions-evasion-driven proliferation financing risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 1 and 7 (2020 amendments)","note":"Require identification, assessment and mitigation of proliferation financing risk and TFS implementation."}]}]},{"label":"Virtual Assets / VASP","questions":[{"q":"What AML/CFT obligations apply to UAE-licensed VASPs under CBUAE guidance?","a":"UAE-licensed virtual asset service providers must apply the full AML/CFT framework, including customer due diligence and beneficial ownership identification, ongoing monitoring, sanctions and TFS screening, suspicious transaction reporting to the FIU through the goAML system, record-keeping, and a risk-based programme with a compliance officer. They must implement the crypto travel rule for virtual asset transfers and apply enhanced due diligence to higher-risk customers, products and jurisdictions. Obligations flow from the federal AML law and decision and CBUAE and VARA guidance.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (AML/CFT Implementing Regulation)","note":"Applies CDD, monitoring, STR reporting and record-keeping to financial institutions and DNFBPs including VASPs."},{"ref":"CBUAE Guidance for Licensed Financial Institutions on Virtual Assets and VASPs","note":"Sets supervisory expectations for AML/CFT controls, including travel rule, for VASP activity."}]},{"q":"What does FATF R.15 require of virtual asset service providers?","a":"FATF Recommendation 15 requires countries to regulate VASPs for AML/CFT purposes, license or register them, and subject them to supervision or monitoring. VASPs must apply the preventive measures that apply to financial institutions, including customer due diligence, record-keeping, suspicious transaction reporting, and the travel rule for virtual asset transfers. Countries must also identify and mitigate the ML/TF risks of virtual assets and apply targeted financial sanctions.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15 and Interpretive Note","note":"Requires licensing/registration, supervision and full preventive measures for VASPs including the travel rule."}]},{"q":"What is the travel rule requirement for virtual asset transfers?","a":"The travel rule requires the originating VASP to obtain and hold required originator and beneficiary information and to transmit it securely to the beneficiary VASP immediately and with the transfer. Required data typically includes originator name, account or wallet identifier, and address or other identifier, plus beneficiary name and account or wallet identifier. FATF applies this to virtual asset transfers, with a de minimis threshold of USD/EUR 1,000 above which full information is required, and obliges VASPs to screen parties against sanctions lists.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16 and Interpretive Note to R.15","note":"Applies the travel rule to virtual asset transfers with the USD/EUR 1,000 de minimis threshold."}]},{"q":"How do we apply the FATF 'sunrise problem' guidance for crypto travel rule?","a":"The sunrise problem arises when the travel rule is in force in one jurisdiction but not yet in the counterparty VASP's jurisdiction, so the receiving VASP may be unable or unwilling to handle travel rule data. FATF guidance expects compliant VASPs to still collect and hold the required information, conduct counterparty VASP due diligence, and use a risk-based approach to decide whether to proceed, hold or reject the transfer rather than treating non-implementation elsewhere as an excuse to omit controls. Mitigations include using interoperable messaging solutions and documenting the rationale for proceeding.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Updated Guidance for a Risk-Based Approach to Virtual Assets and VASPs (2021)","note":"Addresses the sunrise issue and expects continued collection of data and counterparty VASP due diligence."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Underlying travel rule obligation that must still be met by the originating VASP."}]},{"q":"What red flags indicate crypto-TF or crypto-ML in gold/DPMS transactions?","a":"Red flags include payment for high-value gold via crypto from unhosted or newly created wallets, funds traced through mixers, tumblers or privacy coins, or from addresses linked to darknet markets, scams or sanctioned entities. Other indicators are structuring crypto payments below thresholds, rapid conversion of crypto into portable gold to obscure origin, reluctance to disclose the source of the virtual assets or wallet ownership, and counterparties in high-risk jurisdictions. Any sanctions or terrorist-list nexus to a wallet or counterparty is an immediate escalation trigger.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Virtual Assets Red Flag Indicators of ML and TF (2020)","note":"Catalogues transactional, anonymity and source-of-funds red flags for virtual asset activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22 (DPMS CDD)","note":"Requires DPMS to apply CDD and scrutiny that supports detecting these red flags in crypto-funded purchases."}]},{"q":"What VASP due diligence is required before accepting crypto payments for gold?","a":"Before accepting crypto for gold, a DPMS dealer should identify and verify the customer and beneficial owner, establish the source of the virtual assets, and conduct blockchain analytics to assess wallet exposure to illicit sources and sanctioned addresses. Where a counterparty VASP is involved, conduct VASP counterparty due diligence to confirm it is licensed and AML/CFT-compliant, apply sanctions screening, and document a risk assessment. Higher-risk indicators require enhanced due diligence or declining the transaction, and suspicious activity must be reported to the FIU.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Updated VA/VASP Guidance (2021)","note":"Sets expectations for counterparty VASP due diligence and source-of-funds checks on virtual asset payments."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, CDD provisions","note":"Requires customer and beneficial owner identification and source-of-funds checks for higher-risk dealings."}]}]},{"label":"PEP - Enhanced Scrutiny","questions":[{"q":"What is the FATF R.12 definition of a domestic PEP vs foreign PEP?","a":"Under FATF Recommendation 12, a foreign PEP is an individual entrusted with prominent public functions by a foreign country, such as a head of state or government, senior politician, senior government, judicial or military official, senior executive of a state-owned enterprise, or important political party official. A domestic PEP is a person entrusted with such prominent public functions domestically, and a third category covers persons entrusted with prominent functions by an international organisation. Foreign PEPs always require enhanced measures, while domestic and international-organisation PEPs require enhanced measures where the business relationship is assessed as higher risk.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12 and Glossary definition of PEP","note":"Defines foreign, domestic and international-organisation PEPs and the differing risk treatment."}]},{"q":"What enhanced measures are required for a Tier-1 PEP client under UAE rules?","a":"For a high-risk (Tier-1) PEP, UAE rules require obtaining senior management approval to establish or continue the relationship, taking reasonable measures to establish the source of wealth and source of funds, and conducting enhanced ongoing monitoring of the relationship. These enhanced due diligence steps mirror the FATF foreign-PEP standard and apply on a risk-sensitive basis, with documentation of the rationale and intensified scrutiny of transactions. The measures apply to the PEP and their family members and close associates.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 15 (PEPs)","note":"Requires senior management approval, source of wealth/funds checks and enhanced ongoing monitoring for PEPs."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Sets the enhanced due diligence measures for higher-risk PEP relationships."}]},{"q":"When can a PEP be stepped down from enhanced monitoring?","a":"A person who is no longer entrusted with a prominent public function may be stepped down only after a risk-based assessment, not automatically when they leave office. FATF guidance is that a person should continue to be treated as a PEP for as long as they may still pose a higher risk, considering the level of seniority and influence formerly held, whether they retain informal influence, and links to current PEPs. Many institutions apply a defined cooling-off period before reclassification.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12 Interpretive Note and FATF PEP Guidance (2013)","note":"Requires a risk-based, not automatic, approach to declassifying former PEPs."}]},{"q":"What family members and close associates must be screened under FATF R.12?","a":"FATF Recommendation 12 extends PEP requirements to family members and close associates of PEPs. Family members typically include spouses or partners, children and their spouses or partners, and parents; close associates include persons with close business or beneficial-ownership relationships with the PEP and those who are sole or beneficial owners of a legal entity set up for the PEP's benefit. These connected persons should be screened and subject to the same enhanced measures as the PEP on a risk basis.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12 Interpretive Note","note":"Applies PEP requirements to family members and close associates."},{"ref":"FATF Glossary (family members and close associates)","note":"Provides the descriptions of which connected persons fall in scope."}]},{"q":"What senior management approval is required before onboarding a foreign PEP?","a":"Before establishing a business relationship with a foreign PEP, the institution must obtain senior management approval, and senior management approval is also required to continue an existing relationship once a customer is found to be or to become a PEP. This is a mandatory enhanced measure for all foreign PEPs regardless of the risk rating, alongside source-of-wealth and source-of-funds checks and enhanced ongoing monitoring. The approval and its basis should be documented.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires senior management approval to establish or continue a relationship with a foreign PEP."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 15","note":"Implements the senior management approval requirement for PEPs in the UAE."}]},{"q":"How frequently must PEP clients be re-screened against sanctions lists?","a":"Sanctions and PEP screening should be ongoing rather than at onboarding only, with re-screening triggered whenever sanctions or PEP lists are updated and applied across the existing customer base. Given the UAE requirement to act without delay on sanctions designations, screening against updated lists should be effectively continuous or close to it, with PEP status reviewed at each periodic enhanced review. Many institutions screen the book against each list update and conduct full periodic reviews of high-risk PEPs at least annually.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6 (without delay obligation)","note":"Requires prompt action on sanctions designations, driving frequent or continuous re-screening."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (ongoing monitoring)","note":"Requires ongoing monitoring and keeping customer information current, supporting regular re-screening."}]}]},{"label":"Cash & Physical Currency","questions":[{"q":"What is the UAE cash declaration threshold at entry/exit points?","a":"Travellers entering or leaving the UAE must declare cash, negotiable instruments and precious metals or stones carried with them when the value reaches or exceeds AED 60,000 (or the equivalent in foreign currency). The declaration is made to UAE Customs at the border, and failure to declare or false declaration can result in seizure and penalties. This disclosure regime supports detection of cross-border movement of illicit value.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Customs cross-border cash declaration rules (Federal Customs Authority)","note":"Sets the AED 60,000 declaration threshold for cash and bearer instruments at entry and exit."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 32","note":"Requires a declaration or disclosure system for cross-border transportation of currency and bearer negotiable instruments."}]},{"q":"What reporting obligation applies to cash transactions above AED 55,000?","a":"DNFBPs, including dealers in precious metals and stones, must report to the UAE FIU any cash transaction equal to or above AED 55,000 with a customer, whether carried out as a single operation or several apparently linked operations. The report is filed through the goAML system, and the dealer must also conduct customer due diligence on such transactions. This is in addition to filing suspicious transaction reports whenever ML/TF suspicion arises.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (DNFBP cash transaction reporting)","note":"Requires DPMS to report and conduct CDD on cash transactions at or above AED 55,000."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Imposes CDD and record-keeping on DPMS for high-value cash transactions."}]},{"q":"What red flags indicate cash structuring below the AED 55,000 threshold?","a":"Structuring red flags include a customer breaking a large purchase into multiple cash payments each just under AED 55,000, repeated purchases below the threshold over a short period, or several apparently linked customers paying for one transaction to keep each below the limit. Other indicators are reluctance to proceed once told a report is required, requests to split invoices, and patterns inconsistent with the customer's profile. Such conduct should be treated as suspicious and reported regardless of the structuring.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF DPMS Red Flag and Typology Guidance","note":"Identifies structuring and splitting of cash payments to evade reporting thresholds as ML indicators."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (STR obligation)","note":"Requires suspicious transaction reporting even where amounts are kept below the cash reporting threshold."}]},{"q":"What records must be kept for cash transactions under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"Regulated persons must retain records of transactions, including cash transactions, together with customer due diligence information, account files and business correspondence, and the supporting documents sufficient to reconstruct the transaction. The standard retention period is at least five years from the end of the business relationship or the date of the occasional transaction, and records must be made available promptly to the competent authorities. This preserves the audit trail for cash dealings such as high-value gold purchases.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Establishes the record-keeping obligation for transactions and CDD records."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 11","note":"Requires retention of transaction and CDD records for at least five years."}]},{"q":"What due diligence applies when a DPMS client pays in physical gold rather than cash?","a":"Payment in physical gold is value equivalent to cash and should be subject to the same customer due diligence and reporting logic, including identifying the customer and beneficial owner, establishing the source of the gold, and assessing it against the high-value transaction threshold. The dealer should verify provenance and chain of custody to address sanctions, conflict-mineral and money-laundering risk, apply enhanced due diligence where origin is unclear or high-risk, and report linked dealings meeting the threshold or any suspicion. Trade-based and barter arrangements warrant heightened scrutiny.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22 (DPMS CDD)","note":"Applies CDD and record-keeping to high-value dealings including non-cash settlement in precious metals."},{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible Supply Chains of Minerals","note":"Requires provenance and chain-of-custody checks on gold to manage sourcing risk."}]},{"q":"How does FATF R.32 apply to cross-border transportation of cash and BNIs?","a":"FATF Recommendation 32 requires countries to have measures to detect the physical cross-border transportation of currency and bearer negotiable instruments, through a declaration or disclosure system. Authorities must be empowered to stop or restrain, and request information on the origin and intended use of, currency or BNIs that are suspected of being related to money laundering or terrorist financing or are falsely declared, and to apply proportionate sanctions. Information from declarations should be available to the FIU and support confiscation of illicit funds.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 32 and Interpretive Note","note":"Requires a declaration or disclosure system and powers to stop, restrain and sanction cross-border cash and BNI movements."},{"ref":"UAE Customs cross-border cash declaration rules","note":"UAE implementation of the R.32 declaration regime at entry and exit points."}]}]},{"label":"Correspondent Banking","questions":[{"q":"What FATF R.13 obligations apply before establishing a correspondent relationship?","a":"Before establishing a cross-border correspondent relationship, the respondent institution must be assessed: gather sufficient information to understand its business, reputation, and quality of supervision, including whether it has been subject to ML/TF investigation or regulatory action. The correspondent must assess the respondent's AML/CFT controls, obtain senior management approval before opening the account, and clearly document each institution's respective responsibilities. These obligations are part of enhanced due diligence and apply over and above standard CDD.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13 (Correspondent Banking)","note":"Requires gathering information on the respondent, assessing its AML/CFT controls, obtaining senior management approval, and documenting responsibilities."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 25","note":"Implements EDD for correspondent banking, requiring assessment of the respondent's controls and senior management sign-off."}]},{"q":"What nested correspondent account risks must DPMS dealers monitor?","a":"Nesting occurs when a respondent's correspondent account is used by third-party institutions or downstream customers the correspondent has not vetted, masking the true originator of value. Although DPMS dealers are not correspondent banks, where they receive bank transfers they should be alert to payments routed through layered or third-party institutional accounts that obscure the underlying party. The mitigation is to understand the payment chain, identify undisclosed third-party use of an account, and apply enhanced scrutiny or decline where the originator cannot be established.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13","note":"Prohibits permitting accounts to be used by downstream institutions without due diligence (payable-through / nested account risk)."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 4 and Art. 7","note":"Requires DPMS dealers to identify the source and parties to funds and apply ongoing monitoring of transactions."}]},{"q":"What is the UAE position on shell bank correspondent relationships?","a":"UAE law prohibits establishing or maintaining correspondent relationships with shell banks, meaning banks with no physical presence and no affiliation to a regulated financial group subject to effective supervision. Institutions must also satisfy themselves that respondent banks do not permit their accounts to be used by shell banks. This mirrors the FATF standard and is a hard prohibition rather than a risk-based option.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13","note":"Prohibits correspondent relationships with shell banks and use of accounts by shell banks."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 26","note":"Prohibits dealing with or maintaining accounts for shell banks."}]},{"q":"How does FATF R.13 apply to payable-through accounts in the UAE context?","a":"For payable-through accounts, where a respondent's customers gain direct access to the correspondent account, the correspondent must be satisfied that the respondent has performed CDD on those customers and can provide relevant identification data on request. The correspondent should not offer payable-through accounts unless these conditions are met. In the UAE this is implemented through the EDD framework for correspondent relationships, requiring documented assurance of the respondent's CDD before access is granted.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13","note":"Sets specific payable-through account conditions: respondent CDD performed and ID data available on request."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 25","note":"Requires assurance of the respondent's CDD before customers are given direct account access."}]}]},{"label":"Beneficial Ownership","questions":[{"q":"What is the UAE ultimate beneficial owner disclosure threshold under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"A beneficial owner is the natural person who ultimately owns or controls the customer, generally identified through ownership of 25 percent or more of the shares or voting rights, or who otherwise exercises ultimate effective control. Where no such person can be identified through ownership, control by other means is assessed, and failing that the senior managing official is treated as the beneficial owner. Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 maintains this UBO identification standard consistent with the UAE AML framework and FATF.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Governs the UAE AML/CFT regime and the obligation to identify and verify the ultimate beneficial owner."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24 and Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 1","note":"Define beneficial owner using the 25 percent ownership/control threshold with a cascade to control and senior official."}]},{"q":"What are the penalties for providing false UBO information under UAE law?","a":"Providing false, inaccurate, or misleading beneficial ownership information, or failing to disclose or update it, exposes the entity and responsible persons to administrative fines and, in serious cases, criminal liability. The UBO register regime provides for fines per violation and escalating sanctions including suspension and licence-related measures for repeated non-compliance. Penalties apply both to the legal person and to the natural persons responsible for the submission.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Establishes liability and sanctions for breaches of AML/CFT obligations including beneficial ownership duties."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 109 of 2023 (Beneficial Owner Procedures)","note":"Sets administrative penalties for failure to provide accurate UBO data and to maintain the register."}]},{"q":"How do we verify UBO claims for a BVI or Cayman holding structure?","a":"Treat offshore holding companies as higher risk and look through each layer to the natural person owners, obtaining certificates of incumbency, registers of members and directors, and where available beneficial ownership register extracts. Corroborate declared ownership with independent and reliable sources, identify nominee arrangements, and obtain a signed UBO declaration plus identification of each ultimate natural person. Where the structure cannot be unwrapped to a verified natural person, apply enhanced due diligence or decline the relationship.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24 and Recommendation 10","note":"Require identifying the UBO of legal persons and verifying using reliable independent source documents, with a look-through of layered structures."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 8","note":"Requires EDD and additional verification measures for high-risk situations including complex offshore structures."}]},{"q":"What does FATF R.24 require on legal persons' beneficial ownership transparency?","a":"Recommendation 24 requires countries to ensure adequate, accurate, and up-to-date beneficial ownership information on legal persons is obtained and held, and is accessible to competent authorities in a timely manner. Following the 2022 revision it mandates a multi-pronged approach, including a beneficial ownership register or equivalent mechanism, and that companies maintain their own BO records. It also requires controls on bearer shares and nominee arrangements to prevent their misuse.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24 (revised 2022)","note":"Requires adequate, accurate, up-to-date BO information held via a register or equivalent, with controls on bearer shares and nominees."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 109 of 2023","note":"Implements the UAE beneficial owner register obligations in line with R.24."}]},{"q":"When must UBO information be re-verified during the customer lifecycle?","a":"Beneficial ownership information must be kept current through ongoing due diligence and reviewed on a risk-sensitive basis, with more frequent review for higher-risk customers. Re-verification is triggered by trigger events such as a change in ownership or control, a material change in the customer's activity, doubts about previously obtained data, or a change in risk rating. Records and identification must be kept accurate and updated throughout the relationship.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing due diligence and keeping CDD and BO data up to date, especially for higher-risk customers."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 7","note":"Requires ongoing monitoring and updating of customer and beneficial owner information."}]}]},{"label":"Sanctions - Advanced","questions":[{"q":"What is the OFAC 50% rule and how does it apply to UAE-based entities?","a":"Under OFAC's 50 Percent Rule, any entity owned 50 percent or more, directly or indirectly, individually or in the aggregate, by one or more blocked persons is itself treated as blocked even if not separately listed. A UAE-based entity that is 50 percent or more owned by SDNs is therefore blocked for US-nexus purposes, and US persons or USD transactions cannot deal with it. UAE businesses should aggregate ownership across listed parties and screen ownership chains, not just the named entity.","refs":[{"ref":"OFAC Revised Guidance on Entities Owned by Blocked Persons (50% Rule, 2014)","note":"Deems entities 50 percent or more owned by blocked persons to be blocked, aggregating multiple owners."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Requires implementation of targeted financial sanctions, supporting screening of ownership chains."}]},{"q":"How does the EU 'ownership and control' test differ from the OFAC 50% rule?","a":"The EU test applies an ownership limb of more than 50 percent, but it also applies a separate control test: an entity may be subject to restrictions if a designated person controls it even without majority ownership, for example through voting rights, board appointment, or the ability to direct its affairs. This is broader and more qualitative than the OFAC bright-line 50 percent ownership rule. As a result an entity below 50 percent ownership can still be caught under EU measures if control is established.","refs":[{"ref":"EU Best Practices for the effective implementation of restrictive measures","note":"Sets the EU ownership (over 50 percent) and the separate qualitative control criteria for asset freezing."},{"ref":"OFAC 50% Rule (2014)","note":"By contrast applies a 50 percent or more ownership threshold without an independent control limb."}]},{"q":"What is the difference between primary and secondary sanctions exposure?","a":"Primary sanctions prohibit persons within a jurisdiction (for example US persons, or transactions touching the US financial system) from dealing with sanctioned targets. Secondary sanctions target non-US persons for engaging in defined transactions with sanctioned parties even without a US nexus, exposing them to being cut off from the US market or themselves being listed. For UAE entities, primary US sanctions bite on USD or US-touchpoint dealings, while secondary sanctions can attach to wholly non-US trade with certain designated regimes or sectors.","refs":[{"ref":"US sanctions framework (IEEPA and OFAC programs)","note":"Primary sanctions bind US persons and US-nexus transactions; secondary sanctions reach non-US persons by threat of designation or loss of US access."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Requires jurisdictions to give effect to targeted financial sanctions, informing how non-US entities manage exposure."}]},{"q":"When does a UAE entity face US secondary sanctions risk on gold transactions?","a":"A UAE entity faces secondary sanctions risk where it knowingly engages in or facilitates significant gold transactions with persons or jurisdictions targeted by US programs that designate gold as a sanctioned sector, notably certain dealings connected to Russia, Iran, or other listed regimes and SDNs. Risk increases when counterparties, refiners, or the source of the gold link to blocked persons or sanctioned origin. UAE dealers should screen counterparties and gold provenance and avoid significant transactions with sanctioned-linked parties even without a US nexus.","refs":[{"ref":"OFAC sanctions programs covering gold (e.g. Russia and Iran-related Executive Orders)","note":"Authorize secondary sanctions on non-US persons for significant transactions involving sanctioned gold trade."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6 and UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020","note":"Require implementation of targeted financial sanctions and screening of counterparties."}]},{"q":"What is OFAC's general licence framework and when does it apply to gold trade?","a":"A general licence is an authorization issued by OFAC that permits a category of transactions that would otherwise be prohibited, without the need to apply for a specific licence, subject to its stated conditions. General licences may carve out humanitarian trade, wind-down periods, or specified activities within a sanctions program. For gold trade, a general licence would only apply if a relevant OFAC program issues one covering that activity; absent an applicable general or specific licence, transactions with sanctioned parties remain prohibited.","refs":[{"ref":"OFAC general and specific licence framework (31 CFR Chapter V)","note":"General licences authorize categories of otherwise-prohibited transactions subject to conditions; specific licences require application."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Supports controlled, authorized exceptions within targeted financial sanctions regimes."}]},{"q":"What is the blocking statute implication of EU Regulation 2271/96 for UAE entities?","a":"EU Regulation 2271/96, the Blocking Statute, prohibits EU operators from complying with specified extraterritorial US sanctions and nullifies foreign judgments giving them effect within the EU. It binds EU persons rather than UAE entities directly, so a purely UAE entity is not subject to it; however a UAE business dealing with EU counterparties may find those counterparties caught between conflicting US and EU obligations. UAE entities should be aware of this conflict when structuring trade with EU parties but manage their own exposure under applicable US and UAE law.","refs":[{"ref":"EU Council Regulation (EC) No 2271/96 (Blocking Statute)","note":"Prohibits EU operators from complying with listed extraterritorial US sanctions and bars enforcement of related foreign judgments in the EU."},{"ref":"OFAC secondary sanctions framework","note":"Creates the conflicting US obligation that the Blocking Statute is designed to counter."}]}]},{"label":"goAML & FIU Reporting","questions":[{"q":"What is the difference between an STR and an SAR under UAE goAML rules?","a":"In the UAE goAML system a Suspicious Transaction Report (STR) is filed when there are reasonable grounds to suspect a specific transaction is linked to a crime or proceeds of crime, while a Suspicious Activity Report (SAR) covers suspicion arising from conduct, attempted transactions, or behaviour that is not tied to a completed transaction. Both are submitted to the UAE FIU through goAML without tipping off the customer. The distinction is the trigger: a transaction versus broader activity or attempted dealings.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 17","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious transactions to the FIU and submission through the goAML platform."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting of suspicion of proceeds of crime or terrorist financing to the FIU."}]},{"q":"What is an Additional Information File (AIF) and when must it be filed?","a":"An Additional Information File (AIF) is a goAML report type used to provide further information to the UAE FIU, typically in response to an FIU request or to supplement a previously filed report. It is filed when the FIU asks for more detail on a prior STR/SAR or when the reporting entity has new relevant information to add. It links to and supports earlier reports rather than standing alone as the original suspicion report.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE FIU goAML reporting framework","note":"Defines the AIF report type for supplementing prior reports or responding to FIU information requests."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 18","note":"Requires reporting entities to provide the FIU with additional information requested."}]},{"q":"What are the goAML XML schema mandatory fields for a gold-sector STR?","a":"A goAML STR XML must include the reporting entity identifiers, the report type and reason for suspicion, full subject details (parties, individuals and entities with identification data), and transaction details including amount, currency, date, and the accounts or instruments involved. For a gold-sector report this means capturing the customer and counterparty identification, the goods or precious metal description, value, payment method, and a clear narrative grounding the suspicion. Missing mandatory subject, transaction, or indicator fields will cause schema rejection.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE FIU goAML XML schema and reporting guidelines","note":"Specifies mandatory report, subject, transaction, and indicator fields required for valid submission."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 17","note":"Requires complete and accurate suspicious transaction reporting to the FIU."}]},{"q":"What is the UAE FIU feedback mechanism after an STR is filed?","a":"After an STR is filed, the UAE FIU may acknowledge receipt and, where appropriate, provide feedback or instructions through the goAML message board, including requests for additional information or directions such as to refrain from executing a transaction. The FIU does not routinely disclose investigative outcomes, but reporting entities should monitor the goAML inbox for FIU communications and act on any instructions. This feedback loop supports the quality and usefulness of future reporting.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE FIU goAML platform (message board / feedback)","note":"Provides the channel for FIU acknowledgements, requests, and instructions to reporting entities."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20 and Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 18","note":"Support FIU interaction with reporting entities and provision of further information."}]},{"q":"Can the UAE FIU request additional information after an STR is submitted?","a":"Yes. The UAE FIU has the power to request any additional information it deems necessary from reporting entities to perform its functions, and entities are obliged to provide it within the timeframe specified. Such requests are typically issued and answered through goAML, often via an Additional Information File. Failure to respond promptly and completely is itself a compliance breach.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 18","note":"Obliges reporting entities to provide the FIU with additional information on request within the specified time."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 29","note":"Empowers the FIU to obtain and request additional information from reporting entities."}]},{"q":"What is the goAML 'continuing suspicious activity' reporting obligation?","a":"Where suspicious activity continues after an initial report, the reporting entity must file further reports rather than relying on the original, capturing the ongoing conduct and any new transactions or parties. This ensures the FIU has current information on an evolving pattern and is generally done through follow-up STR/SAR or AIF submissions referencing the earlier report. The duty to report is ongoing and is not discharged by a single filing.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 15 and Art. 17","note":"Require ongoing monitoring and reporting of suspicious transactions as they arise."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of all suspicion promptly, including continuing or repeated suspicious activity."}]}]},{"label":"UAE Real Estate & DNFBPs","questions":[{"q":"What AML/CFT obligations apply to UAE real estate agents under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"Real estate agents and brokers are designated DNFBPs and must register with the supervisor (goAML and the relevant authority), conduct customer due diligence on buyers and sellers, identify beneficial owners, maintain records, perform ongoing monitoring, and file STRs to the FIU. They must apply a risk-based approach, screen against sanctions lists, and appoint a compliance officer. Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 maintains these core AML/CFT obligations for the real estate sector.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Sets the AML/CFT obligations applicable to DNFBPs including real estate brokers and agents."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 3 and Art. 4","note":"Define DNFBP scope and impose CDD, record keeping, and reporting duties."}]},{"q":"What is the cash payment reporting threshold for UAE real estate transactions?","a":"Real estate agents, brokers, dealers, and law firms must report to the FIU transactions in the property sector that involve cash payment at or above AED 55,000 (whether single or apparently linked transactions), as well as payments made using virtual assets or funds derived from virtual assets. The report captures the parties, the property, and the payment details. This is the Real Estate Activity Report obligation introduced under the UAE framework.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Ministry of Economy real estate sector reporting requirement (REAR)","note":"Requires reporting of single or linked cash payments at or above AED 55,000 and virtual asset payments in real estate deals."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Underpins DNFBP reporting obligations to the FIU through goAML."}]},{"q":"What CDD is required before completing a high-value real estate transaction in the UAE?","a":"Before completing a high-value transaction the agent must identify and verify the buyer and seller and their beneficial owners using reliable independent documents, understand the purpose of the transaction and the source of funds, and screen all parties against UAE and UN sanctions lists. Higher-risk situations such as offshore buyers, PEPs, or cash and virtual asset payments require enhanced due diligence and senior approval. CDD must be completed before the transaction is finalized, not afterwards.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 4 and Art. 8","note":"Require CDD, beneficial owner identification, and EDD for higher-risk transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Applies CDD obligations to real estate agents involved in transactions for clients."}]},{"q":"What red flags must UAE real estate agents monitor for property sector ML?","a":"Key red flags include payment in cash or virtual assets, purchases through complex or offshore structures or nominees, buyers unconcerned with price or rapid resale (flipping), values inconsistent with the buyer's profile, third-party payers with no clear connection, and reluctance to disclose the beneficial owner or source of funds. Under-valuation or over-valuation versus market price and unexplained early settlement of mortgages are also indicators. These should trigger enhanced scrutiny and, where suspicion remains, an STR.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Guidance on the Real Estate Sector and ML/TF risk","note":"Identifies cash, opaque ownership, mispricing, and third-party payment red flags for real estate."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 16 and Art. 17","note":"Require monitoring and reporting of suspicious transactions including these indicators."}]},{"q":"How does FATF Guidance on DNFBP risk apply to UAE real estate developers?","a":"FATF guidance requires DNFBPs, including those involved in real estate, to assess and document their ML/TF risks across customers, geographies, products, and delivery channels, and to apply mitigating controls proportionate to that risk. Real estate developers should risk-rate projects and buyers, apply CDD and EDD on a risk-sensitive basis, and integrate sanctions screening and source-of-funds checks. The risk-based approach drives the intensity of controls rather than a one-size-fits-all model.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Guidance for a Risk-Based Approach (DNFBPs / Real Estate)","note":"Requires risk assessment and proportionate, risk-based mitigation across the real estate sector."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 4","note":"Mandates a risk-based approach to CDD and controls by DNFBPs."}]},{"q":"What does UAE Cabinet Decision 57/2018 require from real estate sector DNFBPs?","a":"Cabinet Decision No. 57 of 2018 concerns the implementation of UN Security Council resolutions on targeted financial sanctions and the local terrorist lists. It requires all entities, including real estate DNFBPs, to screen customers against the sanctions lists, freeze without delay the funds of listed persons, refrain from dealing with them, and report any matches or attempted dealings to the relevant authority. This obligation operates alongside the general AML/CFT CDD and reporting duties.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 57 of 2018 (Terrorism Lists and TFS implementation)","note":"Requires sanctions screening, freezing without delay, and reporting of listed-person matches by all entities including DNFBPs."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 6 and 7","note":"Require implementation of targeted financial sanctions related to terrorism and proliferation."}]}]},{"label":"Legal Persons & Arrangements","questions":[{"q":"What is the UAE's beneficial ownership disclosure threshold under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"The beneficial owner is the natural person who ultimately owns or controls a legal person, identified primarily by ownership or control of 25 percent or more of the capital or voting rights, directly or indirectly. If no such person is identifiable, the test moves to whoever exercises control by other means, and finally to the senior managing official. Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 preserves this 25 percent threshold with the cascading control test.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Maintains the obligation to identify the ultimate beneficial owner of legal persons."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 109 of 2023 and Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 1","note":"Define the 25 percent ownership/control threshold and the cascade to control and senior official."}]},{"q":"How do we verify UBO claims for a trust structure under UAE law?","a":"For a trust you must identify and verify the settlor, the trustee, the protector if any, the beneficiaries or class of beneficiaries, and any other natural person exercising ultimate effective control over the trust. Obtain the trust deed and reliable identification for each relevant party, and understand the source of funds and purpose of the arrangement. All such parties are treated as beneficial owners for CDD purposes regardless of percentage interest.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 25 and Recommendation 10","note":"Require identification of settlor, trustee, protector, beneficiaries, and controllers of legal arrangements."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 1 and Art. 4","note":"Define beneficial owner for legal arrangements and require their identification and verification."}]},{"q":"What CDD applies to a shelf company with nominee directors under FATF R.24?","a":"A shelf company with nominee directors is a higher-risk indicator requiring enhanced due diligence: look through the nominees to the natural persons who actually own or control the company, obtain disclosure of nominee status and the identity of the nominator, and establish the real purpose and source of funds. R.24 requires that nominee directors and shareholders disclose their status and identify the person on whose behalf they act. Where the genuine controller cannot be established, the relationship should be declined.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires disclosure of nominee status and identification of the nominator to prevent misuse of legal persons."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 8","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence in higher-risk situations such as opaque or nominee structures."}]},{"q":"What does UAE Cabinet Resolution 109/2023 require on UBO registers?","a":"Cabinet Decision No. 109 of 2023 requires legal persons registered in the UAE (outside financial free zones) to create and maintain a register of beneficial owners and a register of partners or shareholders, and to file beneficial ownership data with the relevant registrar. Entities must keep this data accurate and up to date, notify changes within the prescribed period (generally 15 days), and appoint a person to provide information to authorities. Non-compliance attracts administrative penalties.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 109 of 2023 (Regulation of Beneficial Owner Procedures)","note":"Requires maintaining BO and shareholder registers, filing data, and updating changes within the prescribed time."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Underpins the register-based approach to beneficial ownership transparency."}]},{"q":"What are the red flags for complex layered ownership structures in the UAE?","a":"Red flags include ownership chains spanning multiple jurisdictions with no commercial rationale, use of offshore or secrecy jurisdictions, frequent changes in ownership, nominee shareholders and directors, structures designed to keep each owner just below the 25 percent threshold, and reluctance to disclose the ultimate natural person. Inconsistency between the structure and the stated business activity, or value flows that bypass the apparent owners, are further indicators. These warrant enhanced due diligence and, if unresolved, possible reporting.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24 and FATF Guidance on Transparency and Beneficial Ownership","note":"Identify layering, nominees, and secrecy jurisdictions as misuse indicators for legal persons."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 8","note":"Requires EDD where ownership structures are complex or opaque."}]},{"q":"What happens when a UAE entity cannot identify its UBO - what are the obligations?","a":"If the natural-person beneficial owner cannot be identified through ownership, the entity must apply the control test and, failing that, treat the senior managing official as the beneficial owner, while documenting the steps taken. From the reporting institution's perspective, inability to complete CDD including verifying the UBO means the institution must not establish or continue the relationship and must consider filing an STR. The matter cannot simply be left open.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 9","note":"Requires not establishing or terminating the relationship and considering an STR where CDD cannot be completed."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10 and Recommendation 24","note":"Set the cascade to control and senior official and the consequence of being unable to complete CDD."}]}]},{"label":"Non-Profit Organizations (NPOs)","questions":[{"q":"What FATF R.8 measures apply to non-profit organizations operating in the UAE?","a":"Recommendation 8 requires countries to identify the subset of NPOs at risk of terrorist financing abuse using a risk-based approach, rather than subjecting the whole sector to blanket controls. Targeted, proportionate measures include outreach, supervision or monitoring of at-risk NPOs, transparency over funding and beneficiaries, and effective investigation and information-sharing. The aim is to protect legitimate NPO activity while preventing misuse for TF.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 8 and its Interpretive Note","note":"Requires a risk-based, targeted, proportionate approach to protecting at-risk NPOs from TF abuse."},{"ref":"UAE NPO supervisory framework (Ministry of Community Empowerment / relevant authorities)","note":"Implements risk-based oversight of NPOs in the UAE."}]},{"q":"What due diligence is required before accepting donations from an NPO?","a":"Apply CDD to the NPO as a customer: verify its legal status and registration, identify its controllers and authorized signatories, understand its purpose, funding sources, and the destination of its funds. Higher risk arises where the NPO operates in or remits to higher-risk or conflict-affected jurisdictions, which calls for enhanced due diligence and source/destination of funds checks. Screen the NPO and its principals against sanctions lists before accepting funds.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10 and Recommendation 8","note":"Require CDD on the NPO customer and heightened attention to TF risk in the NPO sector."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 4 and Art. 8","note":"Require CDD and EDD where higher-risk factors are present."}]},{"q":"What red flags indicate NPO abuse for TF purposes under FATF guidance?","a":"Red flags include mismatch between funds raised and stated programs, transfers to or activity in conflict zones or high-risk jurisdictions, opaque governance or unverifiable beneficiaries, large cash movements, links between principals and designated persons, and use of informal value transfer channels. Sudden changes in funding patterns or diversion of funds away from declared charitable purposes are further indicators. These should prompt enhanced scrutiny and possible reporting.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Guidance on Recommendation 8 / NPO TF risk indicators","note":"Lists fund mismatches, conflict-zone transfers, opaque governance, and designated-person links as abuse indicators."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 16 and Art. 17","note":"Require monitoring and STR filing on suspicious indicators."}]},{"q":"What UAE regulatory body supervises NPOs for AML/CFT compliance?","a":"NPO supervision in the UAE is exercised by the authorities responsible for licensing and overseeing associations and charities, principally the Ministry of Community Empowerment (formerly Ministry of Community Development) together with the relevant emirate-level authorities and the regulators for charitable fundraising. These bodies apply risk-based monitoring of NPOs in coordination with the national AML/CFT framework. Charitable fundraising is also subject to authority approval and oversight.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE NPO supervisory framework (Ministry of Community Empowerment and emirate authorities)","note":"Licenses and supervises associations and charities including for TF-abuse risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 8","note":"Requires monitoring or supervision of at-risk NPOs by a competent authority."}]},{"q":"What does FATF Guidance on NPO Risk Assessment (2023) recommend for risk-based supervision?","a":"The guidance reinforces that supervision must be targeted at the identified at-risk subset of NPOs, based on a documented sectoral TF risk assessment, and proportionate so as not to disrupt or financially exclude legitimate charities. It recommends authorities understand the sector, engage in outreach and capacity building, and calibrate monitoring intensity to risk. Measures should not be applied as blanket controls across all NPOs.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Best Practices / Guidance on NPOs and Recommendation 8 (updated)","note":"Recommends targeted, proportionate, risk-based supervision grounded in a sector TF risk assessment with outreach."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 8 Interpretive Note","note":"Frames the proportionality and risk-based supervision expectations."}]},{"q":"What enhanced measures apply to NPOs transferring funds to conflict-affected zones?","a":"Transfers to conflict-affected or high-risk zones are a heightened TF risk and require enhanced due diligence: verify the end beneficiary and field partners, document the purpose and delivery of funds, use traceable banking channels rather than cash or informal transfer where possible, and apply robust sanctions screening of recipients and intermediaries. Ongoing monitoring and post-disbursement assurance over use of funds should be applied. Persistent inability to verify the destination warrants escalation and possible reporting.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 8 and Recommendation 16","note":"Require heightened controls over NPO fund flows and complete originator/beneficiary information on transfers."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 8","note":"Requires EDD for transactions linked to higher-risk geographies."}]}]},{"label":"Trade Finance & Letters of Credit","questions":[{"q":"What TBML red flags must banks monitor in documentary credit transactions?","a":"Red flags include prices that materially differ from market value (over or under-invoicing), goods inconsistent with the parties' usual business, vague or generic goods descriptions, mismatches between shipping documents and the underlying trade, circular or repeated shipments, and payments routed through unrelated third parties or high-risk jurisdictions. Unusual amendments to letters of credit and counterparties with no commercial logic are further indicators. These should trigger enhanced scrutiny and, where suspicion remains, an STR.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Guidance on Trade-Based Money Laundering (2020)","note":"Identifies mispricing, document mismatches, and third-party payment indicators in trade transactions."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 16","note":"Requires monitoring for suspicious indicators in transactions."}]},{"q":"What due diligence applies before issuing a letter of credit for CAHRA goods?","a":"For goods sourced from conflict-affected and high-risk areas (CAHRA), apply enhanced due diligence on the supply chain: verify the origin and provenance of the goods, identify all counterparties and their beneficial owners, screen against sanctions, and seek evidence of responsible sourcing or chain-of-custody documentation. For minerals and gold this aligns with OECD due diligence expectations to avoid financing conflict or sanctioned parties. The bank should be satisfied of the legitimacy of origin before issuing the credit.","refs":[{"ref":"OECD Due Diligence Guidance for Responsible Supply Chains from CAHRA","note":"Sets supply-chain due diligence expectations for minerals/gold from conflict-affected areas."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 8 (EDD) and UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 8","note":"Require enhanced due diligence for higher-risk geographies and counterparties."}]},{"q":"What does FATF Guidance on Trade-Based Money Laundering (2020) require?","a":"The guidance requires institutions to understand TBML typologies (over/under-invoicing, multiple invoicing, over/under-shipment, phantom shipments) and to embed trade-specific risk indicators into their monitoring. It calls for a risk-based approach, scrutiny of pricing and documentation against market and trade norms, data-sharing, and training so staff can detect manipulation of trade transactions. The objective is to identify value transfer disguised as legitimate trade.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Guidance on Trade-Based Money Laundering (2020)","note":"Sets out TBML typologies and risk indicators and a risk-based detection approach."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10 and 20","note":"Underpin CDD and suspicious transaction reporting applied to trade activity."}]},{"q":"How do we detect phantom shipments in a trade finance transaction?","a":"Phantom shipments involve documentation for goods that are never shipped or do not exist. Detect them by corroborating shipping documents against independent sources: verify the vessel and voyage (IMO number, AIS tracking), check that bills of lading and the carrier are genuine, confirm the goods, quantities, and routing are plausible, and look for inconsistencies between invoices, packing lists, and transport documents. Reused or recycled documents and counterparties with no verifiable trading presence are strong indicators.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Guidance on Trade-Based Money Laundering (2020)","note":"Identifies phantom/fictitious shipments and document verification as a key detection technique."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 16","note":"Requires scrutiny and monitoring of transactions for such anomalies."}]},{"q":"What over/under-invoicing indicators should trigger enhanced scrutiny under UAE AML rules?","a":"Enhanced scrutiny is warranted where the invoiced price deviates materially from prevailing market or fair value for the goods, where unit prices are inconsistent with similar transactions, where the goods description does not support the stated value, or where amounts are repeatedly amended. Over-invoicing transfers value to the exporter while under-invoicing transfers value to the importer, and both distort the trade-payment relationship. Such mispricing should be benchmarked against market data and, if unexplained, reported.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Guidance on Trade-Based Money Laundering (2020)","note":"Defines over and under-invoicing as core TBML techniques requiring price benchmarking."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art. 8 and Art. 16","note":"Require EDD and monitoring where transactions show mispricing anomalies."}]},{"q":"What does ICC Guidance on trade finance and AML require from issuing banks?","a":"ICC guidance expects issuing and confirming banks to apply CDD on their own customers, screen parties, goods, vessels, and ports against sanctions, and review trade documents for AML and sanctions red flags consistent with their financial-crime risk appetite. It recognizes that banks examine documents under UCP 600 for compliance but should overlay risk-based financial-crime checks rather than relying on documentary examination alone. The bank remains responsible for its own risk assessment of the transaction.","refs":[{"ref":"ICC Guidance / Wolfsberg-ICC-BAFT Trade Finance Principles","note":"Set expectations for sanctions screening, party and goods checks, and risk-based AML review by trade-finance banks."},{"ref":"ICC UCP 600","note":"Governs documentary examination, which AML checks supplement rather than replace."}]}]},{"label":"UAE Free Zones","questions":[{"q":"What AML/CFT obligations apply to entities registered in UAE free zones?","a":"Free zone entities that fall within the definition of a financial institution or DNFBP are subject to the same federal AML/CFT framework as mainland entities, including risk assessment, customer due diligence, transaction monitoring, suspicious transaction reporting via goAML, record keeping for at least five years, and appointment of a compliance officer. Financial free zones (DIFC and ADGM) apply their own regulator's AML rulebook, but these must be consistent with the federal regime and FATF standards. All free zone entities must register on the goAML and Automatic Reporting System for Sanctions Lists platforms.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Sets the federal AML/CFT obligations applicable to financial institutions and DNFBPs, including those in free zones."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Details CDD, STR, record keeping and compliance officer requirements applicable across the UAE including free zones."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 1, 10, 20","note":"Require risk-based approach, customer due diligence, and suspicious transaction reporting for all regulated entities."}]},{"q":"How does Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 apply to DIFC and ADGM-licensed entities?","a":"The federal AML/CFT law applies nationwide, so DIFC and ADGM entities remain within its scope as a matter of national policy, but day-to-day AML supervision of those entities is exercised by their own regulators, the DFSA and the FSRA, which issue AML rulebooks consistent with the federal regime. These financial free zone regulators enforce CDD, sanctions screening, STR filing to the UAE FIU, and record keeping standards aligned with the federal law and FATF. The federal law and the National Committee retain overarching coordination, and sanctions and STR reporting flow into national systems such as goAML.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Establishes a single national AML/CFT framework that extends to financial free zones."},{"ref":"DFSA AML Rulebook / FSRA AML Rulebook","note":"Implement AML/CFT obligations for DIFC and ADGM entities consistent with the federal law."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 26","note":"Requires effective AML supervision of financial institutions, including in free zones."}]},{"q":"What enhanced CDD applies to free zone companies with no UAE nexus business?","a":"A free zone company that conducts no genuine onshore activity is a higher-risk profile and warrants enhanced due diligence, including establishing the true purpose and economic rationale of the structure, identifying and verifying all beneficial owners, understanding the source of funds and wealth, and obtaining senior management approval to onboard or continue the relationship. The firm should scrutinise whether the entity is a shell or front used to obscure ownership and should apply intensified ongoing monitoring. Where the structure cannot be justified, the relationship should be declined or exited.","refs":[{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, EDD provisions","note":"Requires enhanced measures for higher-risk relationships including verifying beneficial ownership and source of funds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires identification and verification of beneficial owners and EDD for higher-risk situations."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Mandates a risk-based approach with enhanced measures for higher-risk customers."}]},{"q":"What red flags indicate free zone abuse for sanctions evasion?","a":"Warning signs include newly formed free zone entities with no real economic activity, complex layered ownership across multiple jurisdictions, nominee directors or shareholders, transactions routed through high-risk or sanctioned-adjacent jurisdictions, goods descriptions inconsistent with the entity's stated business, and use of the free zone solely as a transshipment or invoicing node. Frequent changes of beneficial owner, mismatch between trade documents and payment flows, and links to dual-use goods also signal evasion risk. Such indicators require sanctions screening, EDD, and consideration of an STR.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7","note":"Requires implementation of targeted financial sanctions related to proliferation, relevant to evasion typologies."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 (TFS)","note":"Imposes obligations to screen against and freeze assets of designated persons, including detection of evasion."},{"ref":"FATF Trade-Based Money Laundering Guidance","note":"Identifies misuse of trade and corporate structures, including free zones, for evasion."}]},{"q":"What are the AML supervisory responsibilities of UAE free zone authorities?","a":"Free zone authorities act as supervisory authorities for the entities they license, meaning they must understand the ML/TF risks in their zone, conduct risk-based supervision and inspections, ensure registrants comply with CDD, STR and record-keeping duties, maintain registration on goAML and the sanctions reporting system, and impose administrative penalties for breaches. They coordinate with the national supervisory framework and report systemic concerns. Financial free zone regulators (DFSA, FSRA) exercise these functions through their own rulebooks.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Defines supervisory authorities and their oversight duties over regulated entities."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, supervisory provisions","note":"Sets out inspection, monitoring and enforcement responsibilities of supervisors."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 26-28","note":"Require risk-based supervision and effective sanctions for financial institutions and DNFBPs."}]},{"q":"How does the DFSA AML Module differ from CBUAE AML requirements?","a":"The DFSA AML Module governs firms licensed in the DIFC and is set out in the DFSA Rulebook, applying English common law principles within the financial free zone, whereas CBUAE AML requirements apply to banks, exchange houses and other CBUAE-licensed financial institutions across the UAE under the federal civil law framework. Both are designed to meet FATF standards and the federal AML law, and both require RBA, CDD, EDD for PEPs, sanctions screening and STR filing to the UAE FIU. The principal differences are jurisdictional scope, the supervising regulator, and the specific rulebook drafting and enforcement mechanisms rather than the underlying standards.","refs":[{"ref":"DFSA Rulebook, AML Module (AML)","note":"Sets AML/CFT obligations for DIFC-licensed firms supervised by the DFSA."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance for Licensed Financial Institutions","note":"Sets AML obligations for CBUAE-supervised institutions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 26","note":"Both regimes implement supervisory standards consistent with FATF requirements."}]}]},{"label":"Insurance Sector","questions":[{"q":"What AML/CFT obligations apply to UAE life insurers under IA Circular 8/2022?","a":"Life insurers and intermediaries offering investment-linked or savings products are treated as financial institutions and must implement a full AML/CFT programme: enterprise risk assessment, risk-based CDD including beneficial owner and beneficiary identification, screening against UN and UAE sanctions lists, transaction and behaviour monitoring, STR filing through goAML, record keeping, and appointment of a compliance officer. Insurance Authority guidance requires governance, training and independent testing proportionate to the firm's risk. Pure non-life and term protection products carrying no investment value are generally lower risk.","refs":[{"ref":"IA Circular 8/2022 (insurance AML/CFT)","note":"Sets AML/CFT obligations for UAE life insurers and intermediaries."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Requires CDD, STR, screening and record keeping for financial institutions including life insurers."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires customer due diligence including identification of policy beneficiaries."}]},{"q":"What CDD is required before issuing a single-premium life insurance policy?","a":"Before issuing the policy the insurer must identify and verify the policyholder and any beneficial owner, understand the purpose and intended nature of the relationship, and establish the source of the single premium, which is a key concern given the lump-sum nature of the payment. The beneficiary or beneficiaries must be identified, and verification of the beneficiary's identity should occur at or before payout. Because a large single premium can be a laundering vehicle, EDD and source-of-funds evidence are expected for higher-value or higher-risk cases.","refs":[{"ref":"IA Circular 8/2022","note":"Sets CDD expectations for life insurance products including single-premium policies."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10, life insurance beneficiary measures","note":"Requires identification of beneficiaries of life insurance as a CDD component."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Requires verification of identity and source of funds before establishing the relationship."}]},{"q":"What red flags indicate ML through life insurance products in the UAE?","a":"Indicators include purchase of a high single-premium policy inconsistent with the customer's known means, early surrender or cancellation with acceptance of a loss penalty (a classic wash transaction), overpayment of premiums followed by refund requests to third parties, frequent changes of beneficiary, payment from or to high-risk third parties or jurisdictions, and reluctance to provide source-of-funds information. Assignment of policies to unrelated parties and use of cash for premiums are also red flags. Such patterns warrant EDD and consideration of an STR.","refs":[{"ref":"IA Circular 8/2022","note":"Identifies suspicious indicators and monitoring duties for life insurers."},{"ref":"FATF Guidance for the Life Insurance Sector","note":"Sets out recognised ML red flags and typologies for life products."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, STR provisions","note":"Requires reporting where transactions raise ML/TF suspicion."}]},{"q":"What does FATF Guidance on life insurance sector ML apply to UAE operators?","a":"FATF guidance directs life insurers to apply a risk-based approach, assess product, customer, distribution channel and geographic risk, and apply CDD that captures the policyholder, beneficial owner and ultimate beneficiary. UAE operators should incorporate these standards into their programmes alongside IA and federal requirements, applying simplified measures only to genuinely low-risk products and EDD to investment-type and PEP-related policies. The guidance emphasises beneficiary verification at payout and monitoring across the policy lifecycle.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Guidance for the Life Insurance Sector","note":"Provides the risk-based methodology and CDD expectations for life insurers."},{"ref":"IA Circular 8/2022","note":"Implements FATF-aligned obligations for UAE life insurers."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based approach across the sector."}]},{"q":"When must a UAE insurer file an STR for a suspicious insurance claim?","a":"An insurer must file a suspicious transaction report through goAML without delay, as soon as it has reasonable grounds to suspect that funds connected to a claim, premium or surrender relate to crime proceeds or terrorist financing, regardless of the amount. There is no monetary threshold for STRs, and the suspicion can arise from inflated or staged claims, payouts directed to unrelated third parties, or inconsistency with the customer profile. The insurer must not tip off the customer and must retain supporting records.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT), STR duty","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious transactions to the FIU without delay."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, reporting and tipping-off","note":"Sets the obligation to file STRs via goAML and the prohibition on tipping off."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting of suspicion of ML/TF irrespective of amount."}]},{"q":"What enhanced monitoring applies to insurance policies purchased by PEPs?","a":"PEP relationships require senior management approval to establish or continue, measures to establish the source of funds and source of wealth funding the policy, and enhanced ongoing monitoring throughout the relationship. These measures apply to foreign PEPs on a mandatory basis and to domestic PEPs and heads of international organisations on a risk-sensitive basis, and extend to family members and close associates. The insurer should also apply enhanced scrutiny where a PEP is identified as the policy beneficiary, applying EDD before payout.","refs":[{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, PEP provisions","note":"Requires senior approval, source of wealth and enhanced monitoring for PEPs."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 12 and 22","note":"Set EDD requirements for PEPs including life insurance beneficiaries."},{"ref":"IA Circular 8/2022","note":"Applies PEP EDD expectations to UAE insurers."}]}]},{"label":"Internal Audit & Compliance Testing","questions":[{"q":"What elements must a UAE AML internal audit programme cover under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"The independent audit function must test the adequacy and effectiveness of the entire AML/CFT framework: the enterprise risk assessment, policies and procedures, CDD and EDD application, sanctions and PEP screening, transaction monitoring and alert handling, STR timeliness and quality, record keeping, training, and the role and resourcing of the compliance officer. It must assess both design and operating effectiveness, report findings to senior management and the board, and track remediation. Scope and depth should be risk-based and proportionate to the entity's size and complexity.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Requires an independent audit function to test the AML/CFT programme."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, internal controls","note":"Requires independent audit and effective internal controls."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires an independent audit function to test the AML system."}]},{"q":"How frequently must AML controls be independently tested in a UAE DPMS entity?","a":"There is no single fixed statutory interval, but independent testing should be conducted on a risk-based and regular basis, with most DPMS firms performing it at least annually and more often where risk is elevated or material weaknesses are found. The frequency must be proportionate to the size, complexity and risk profile of the business, and significant changes in products, customers or regulation should trigger additional testing. Larger or higher-risk dealers are expected to maintain a more frequent and rigorous testing cycle.","refs":[{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, internal controls and audit","note":"Requires an independent audit function commensurate with size and risk."},{"ref":"Ministry of Economy DNFBP AML Guidance for DPMS","note":"Sets supervisory expectations for periodic independent testing of dealers in precious metals and stones."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires an independent audit function scaled to the nature of the business."}]},{"q":"What does FATF R.18 require on internal controls, audit, and compliance?","a":"FATF Recommendation 18 requires financial institutions and DNFBPs to implement internal programmes against ML/TF, including the appointment of a compliance officer at management level, employee screening procedures, an ongoing training programme, and an independent audit function to test the system. For groups, it requires group-wide programmes covering information sharing and ensuring foreign branches and subsidiaries apply consistent standards. The controls must be proportionate to the risks and the size of the business.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires internal controls, compliance officer, training and independent audit, including group-wide programmes."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Implements internal control and audit requirements in UAE law."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Mandates internal AML/CFT controls and an independent audit function."}]},{"q":"What are the key AML internal audit findings that must be escalated to the Board?","a":"Findings indicating systemic or material weaknesses must reach the board, including failures in beneficial ownership identification or sanctions screening, unreported or late STRs, ineffective transaction monitoring, breaches of record-keeping or training obligations, under-resourcing of the compliance function, and any conduct suggesting potential criminal exposure. Repeat or unremediated findings and matters affecting the firm's overall risk appetite or regulatory standing should be escalated. The board should receive remediation plans and track closure of high-rated issues.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Places ultimate accountability for the AML/CFT programme on senior management and the board."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires audit findings to feed governance and remediation."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, governance provisions","note":"Requires effective oversight of AML controls by senior management."}]},{"q":"What transaction monitoring effectiveness tests must an AML audit conduct?","a":"An audit should validate that monitoring rules and thresholds align with the firm's risk assessment, test scenario coverage against known typologies, sample alerts to assess investigation quality and timeliness, check for missed or improperly closed alerts, verify data completeness and feed accuracy, and confirm escalation to STRs where warranted. It should also assess model tuning, false positive and false negative rates, and whether system parameters are reviewed and approved. Findings should drive recalibration of the monitoring system.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires the audit function to test effectiveness of AML systems including monitoring."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, ongoing monitoring","note":"Requires ongoing transaction monitoring that audit must validate."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires monitoring to detect and escalate suspicious activity for reporting."}]},{"q":"What documentation must be retained from an AML compliance testing exercise?","a":"The firm should retain the audit scope and plan, methodology and sampling approach, working papers and evidence reviewed, findings with risk ratings, management responses, remediation plans with owners and deadlines, and the final report presented to senior management and the board. These records demonstrate that independent testing occurred and that issues were tracked to closure, and they must be kept for at least five years and made available to supervisors on request.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT), record keeping","note":"Requires retention of AML records for at least five years."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, record keeping","note":"Requires records to be retained and available to supervisors."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 11 and 18","note":"Require record keeping and documentation of the independent audit function."}]}]},{"label":"New Payment Methods","questions":[{"q":"What AML/CFT obligations apply to UAE-licensed e-wallet providers under CBUAE rules?","a":"Stored value facility and e-wallet providers licensed by the CBUAE are payment-token or retail payment service providers and must run a full AML/CFT programme: risk assessment, customer identification and verification proportionate to wallet limits, sanctions and PEP screening, transaction monitoring for structuring and velocity anomalies, STR filing via goAML, record keeping, and a compliance officer. Limits on balances, loading and transfers help manage risk, and higher-value or cross-border functionality triggers enhanced CDD. Anonymous or fully unverified wallets are not permitted beyond strictly limited low-value thresholds.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE Stored Value Facilities (SVF) Regulation / Retail Payment Services and Card Schemes (RPSCS) Regulation","note":"Sets licensing and AML obligations for e-wallet and payment providers."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Applies CDD, monitoring and STR duties to these financial institutions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15 and NPM Guidance","note":"Require AML controls for new payment products and providers."}]},{"q":"What red flags indicate ML through buy-now-pay-later (BNPL) products?","a":"Red flags include rapid use of multiple BNPL accounts or identities, purchases of high-value or easily resold goods followed by quick resale, early or overpayment of instalments from unrelated third-party sources, refunds directed to different payment instruments, and synthetic or stolen identity indicators at onboarding. Patterns suggesting the product is being used to layer or move value rather than to finance consumption are key concerns. Such activity should prompt EDD and consideration of an STR.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Guidance on New Payment Methods","note":"Identifies layering and misuse typologies relevant to deferred-payment products."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, monitoring and STR","note":"Requires monitoring and reporting of suspicious activity."},{"ref":"CBUAE RPSCS / consumer finance guidance","note":"Brings BNPL providers within AML monitoring expectations where licensed."}]},{"q":"What CDD applies to customers using mobile payment apps for high-value transactions?","a":"High-value mobile payment activity requires full CDD: verified identity, understanding of the purpose and expected pattern of use, sanctions and PEP screening, and source-of-funds enquiry where amounts or velocity are inconsistent with the customer profile. Simplified measures applied to low-value wallets are no longer appropriate once thresholds are exceeded, so the provider must step up to enhanced verification and monitoring. Aggregated or structured transactions designed to stay below limits should themselves trigger scrutiny.","refs":[{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, CDD and EDD","note":"Requires CDD scaled to risk and EDD for higher-risk or higher-value activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires CDD including for occasional and higher-value transactions."},{"ref":"CBUAE SVF / RPSCS Regulation","note":"Sets verification thresholds for mobile and stored value payments."}]},{"q":"How does FATF Guidance on New Payment Methods apply to prepaid cards in the UAE?","a":"The guidance treats prepaid cards as higher-risk where they allow anonymity, cash loading, high or unlimited balances, cross-border use, or reloadability, and it calls for risk-based limits and CDD calibrated to those features. UAE providers should impose load and balance caps, verify identity for reloadable and higher-value cards, monitor for structuring and overseas cash withdrawals, and apply EDD to cross-border or non-face-to-face issuance. Closed-loop low-value cards may attract simplified measures only where the risk is genuinely low.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Guidance on New Payment Methods / Prepaid Cards, Mobile Payments and Internet-Based Payment Services","note":"Sets the risk factors and CDD calibration for prepaid products."},{"ref":"CBUAE SVF Regulation","note":"Implements limits and AML controls for prepaid and stored value products in the UAE."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Requires identification and mitigation of risks from new technologies and products."}]},{"q":"What CBUAE supervision applies to payment service providers for AML purposes?","a":"The CBUAE licenses and supervises payment service providers as financial institutions and applies risk-based AML/CFT oversight, including off-site monitoring, on-site inspections, review of risk assessments and STR behaviour, and enforcement through fines, restrictions or licence action for breaches. Providers must register on goAML and the sanctions reporting system and meet CDD, monitoring and reporting standards. The CBUAE issues sector guidance and expects boards and compliance officers to be accountable for the programme.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE Retail Payment Services and Card Schemes (RPSCS) Regulation","note":"Establishes licensing and supervision of payment service providers."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Designates the CBUAE as supervisor for its licensees and sets sanctions for breaches."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 26-27","note":"Require risk-based supervision and enforcement powers."}]},{"q":"What does FATF R.15 require of payment system operators regarding new technologies?","a":"Recommendation 15 requires countries and institutions to identify and assess the ML/TF risks arising from new products, business practices, delivery mechanisms and technologies, both before launch and on an ongoing basis, and to take appropriate measures to manage and mitigate those risks. It also requires regulation, licensing or registration and supervision of virtual asset service providers and the application of preventive measures to them. Payment innovators must therefore conduct pre-launch risk assessments and build AML controls into new offerings.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Requires risk assessment of new technologies and regulation of VASPs."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Requires assessment and mitigation of risks from new products and technologies."},{"ref":"CBUAE / VARA technology risk guidance","note":"Applies new-technology risk assessment expectations to UAE providers."}]}]},{"label":"Money Service Businesses (MSBs)","questions":[{"q":"What licensing requirements apply to UAE money exchange houses under CBUAE rules?","a":"Money exchange and money transfer businesses must be licensed by the CBUAE, meeting capital, fit-and-proper ownership and management, governance, and AML/CFT compliance conditions before authorisation. Licensees must register on goAML and the sanctions reporting system, appoint a compliance officer, maintain transaction monitoring and record keeping, and submit to CBUAE supervision and reporting. Operating money services without a CBUAE licence is prohibited and exposes the operator to criminal and administrative sanctions.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE Regulations / Standards for Licensed Exchange Houses","note":"Set licensing, governance and AML conditions for money exchange businesses."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Requires AML compliance and supervision of licensed money services."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 14","note":"Requires money or value transfer services to be licensed or registered and supervised."}]},{"q":"What CDD applies when a customer exchanges more than AED 35,000 at a UAE exchange?","a":"For occasional transactions at or above the applicable threshold the exchange house must perform full CDD, identifying and verifying the customer and any beneficial owner, understanding the purpose of the transaction, screening against sanctions and PEP lists, and recording source of funds where the activity is inconsistent with the profile. Linked or structured transactions that together exceed the threshold must be aggregated and treated the same way. Higher amounts or higher-risk indicators trigger enhanced due diligence.","refs":[{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, occasional transaction CDD","note":"Requires CDD for occasional transactions at or above the prescribed threshold and for linked transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires CDD for occasional transactions above the designated threshold."},{"ref":"CBUAE Standards for Exchange Houses","note":"Sets CDD and aggregation expectations for money exchange transactions."}]},{"q":"What red flags must UAE hawala / IVTS operators monitor under FATF R.14?","a":"Registered hawala providers must monitor for transactions inconsistent with a customer's profile, structuring to avoid thresholds, transfers to or from high-risk jurisdictions, use of multiple senders or receivers linked to a single beneficiary, settlement patterns lacking economic rationale, and any indication of links to sanctioned parties or terrorist financing. They must maintain records of remitters and beneficiaries and apply CDD and reporting like other money transfer operators. Suspicious patterns require an STR.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 14","note":"Requires MVTS providers, including hawala, to be registered, supervised and to apply AML controls."},{"ref":"CBUAE Registered Hawala Providers Regulation","note":"Requires registration and AML monitoring by UAE hawala operators."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious transactions detected through monitoring."}]},{"q":"What reporting obligation applies to unregistered hawala operators discovered in the UAE?","a":"Hawala activity in the UAE must be conducted only by providers registered with the CBUAE, so an unregistered operator is operating illegally and the discovery should be reported to the CBUAE and, where suspicion of ML/TF exists, an STR filed via goAML. Financial institutions that detect customers using or operating unregistered hawala channels should treat this as a significant red flag, apply EDD, and consider exiting the relationship. Operating unregistered money services is itself a punishable offence.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE Registered Hawala Providers Regulation","note":"Requires hawala providers to register and makes unregistered operation unlawful."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT), STR duty","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious activity to the FIU."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 14","note":"Requires action against unregistered or unlicensed MVTS operators."}]},{"q":"What transaction monitoring systems must UAE exchange houses maintain?","a":"Exchange houses must operate systems capable of ongoing monitoring across the customer base, detecting structuring, high-velocity or high-volume activity, transfers to high-risk corridors, and behaviour inconsistent with the customer profile, with the ability to aggregate linked transactions and generate and investigate alerts. Sanctions screening must be applied to senders, receivers and beneficial owners at the point of transaction. Systems should be calibrated to the firm's risk assessment and feed timely STR filing.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE Standards for Licensed Exchange Houses","note":"Require ongoing transaction monitoring and sanctions screening."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, ongoing monitoring","note":"Requires continuous monitoring of business relationships and transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 10 and 20","note":"Require monitoring and reporting of suspicious transactions."}]},{"q":"What does UAE Cabinet Decision 57/2018 require from money transfer operators?","a":"Cabinet Decision No. 57 of 2018 concerns the implementation of UN Security Council resolutions and targeted financial sanctions, requiring money transfer operators and other regulated entities to screen customers and transactions against the UN consolidated and UAE local terrorist lists, freeze without delay the funds of designated persons, refrain from dealing with them, and report freezing actions and matches to the relevant authority. Operators must register on the sanctions reporting system and act immediately on new designations. Failure to freeze or report exposes the operator to penalties.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 57 of 2018 (TFS / UNSCR implementation)","note":"Requires screening, immediate freezing and reporting against UN and local sanctions lists."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 6 and 7","note":"Require implementation of targeted financial sanctions for terrorism and proliferation."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Underpins sanctions compliance obligations for regulated entities."}]}]},{"label":"Customer Risk Rating Models","questions":[{"q":"What risk factors must a UAE entity's customer risk rating model include under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"A compliant model must combine customer risk (type, ownership complexity, PEP status, adverse media), product and service risk, delivery channel risk (notably non-face-to-face onboarding), and geographic or jurisdiction risk. The model should reflect the entity's own risk assessment and the national risk assessment, support a risk-based application of CDD and EDD, and allow override and periodic review. Ratings must be documented, justifiable and capable of identifying high-risk relationships for enhanced measures.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Requires a risk-based approach and assessment of customer, product, channel and geographic risk."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, RBA provisions","note":"Requires identification and assessment of ML/TF risks across these factors."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires risk identification across customer, product, channel and geographic dimensions."}]},{"q":"What weighting should jurisdiction risk have in a UAE DPMS customer risk scoring model?","a":"There is no fixed regulatory percentage; jurisdiction risk should be weighted to reflect its materiality to the dealer's actual exposure, drawing on FATF high-risk and monitored jurisdiction lists, UAE national risk assessment findings, and sanctions risk. For a DPMS firm with significant cross-border or cash sourcing from higher-risk countries, jurisdiction risk should carry substantial weight and can elevate a customer to high risk on its own where the country is FATF-listed. The chosen weighting must be documented, evidence-based and reviewed as risk lists change.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 19 and High-Risk Jurisdictions statements","note":"Require EDD for higher-risk countries and inform jurisdiction weighting."},{"ref":"Ministry of Economy DNFBP AML Guidance for DPMS","note":"Sets risk-based expectations for dealers in precious metals and stones."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires geographic risk to be assessed and weighted in the risk model."}]},{"q":"When must a customer risk rating be reviewed outside the scheduled review cycle?","a":"An out-of-cycle review is triggered by material changes such as a new high-risk transaction or pattern, a change in beneficial ownership or control, a customer becoming a PEP, an adverse media or sanctions hit, a change in the customer's jurisdiction or business, or any event giving rise to suspicion. Filing an STR or detecting unusual activity should also prompt reassessment. The review must update the rating and apply corresponding CDD or EDD.","refs":[{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, ongoing monitoring","note":"Requires keeping CDD and risk ratings up to date and reviewing on trigger events."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing due diligence and updating of customer information."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Requires continuous risk-based monitoring of relationships."}]},{"q":"What does FATF R.10 require on ongoing monitoring and risk-based customer classification?","a":"Recommendation 10 requires CDD measures including identifying and verifying the customer and beneficial owner, understanding the purpose of the relationship, and conducting ongoing due diligence by scrutinising transactions to ensure they are consistent with the customer profile and keeping information current. It permits the extent of measures to vary with risk, allowing simplified measures for lower risk and requiring enhanced measures for higher risk. CDD must be applied at onboarding, on suspicion, and when doubts arise about previously obtained data.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Sets CDD, ongoing monitoring and risk-based application requirements."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Implements CDD and ongoing monitoring duties in UAE law."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Requires risk-based CDD and ongoing monitoring."}]},{"q":"What are the minimum risk factors for a PEP customer risk rating under UAE rules?","a":"A PEP rating must capture whether the person is a foreign PEP (always higher risk), a domestic PEP or head of an international organisation (risk-sensitive), the prominence and corruption exposure of the position and jurisdiction, source of wealth and funds, family members and close associates, and any adverse media. Foreign PEPs are treated as high risk by default and require senior approval, source-of-wealth verification and enhanced monitoring. The rating should drive mandatory EDD throughout the relationship.","refs":[{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, PEP provisions","note":"Defines PEP categories and required EDD measures."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 12 and 22","note":"Set EDD requirements and risk classification for PEPs."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Requires enhanced measures for PEP relationships."}]},{"q":"What documentation must support a downgrade of a customer from high to medium risk?","a":"A downgrade must be supported by documented evidence that the factors driving the high rating have materially reduced or been resolved, such as updated and verified CDD, satisfactory source-of-funds or source-of-wealth confirmation, resolution or clearance of adverse media or sanctions concerns, and a period of activity consistent with the customer profile. The rationale, supporting evidence, reviewer identity and approval (typically by compliance or senior management) must be recorded and retained. Unsupported or undocumented downgrades are a supervisory concern.","refs":[{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, record keeping and RBA","note":"Requires documented, evidence-based risk decisions and retention of records."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires risk classification to be based on current, verified information."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Requires documented and justifiable risk-based decisions."}]}]},{"label":"Virtual Assets & VASP Onboarding","questions":[{"q":"What licence must a UAE VASP obtain before offering virtual asset services under VARA?","a":"A virtual asset service provider operating in or from the Emirate of Dubai (excluding the DIFC) must obtain a VARA licence for the specific activities it conducts, such as exchange, broker-dealer, custody, management, advisory, lending or transfer and settlement services. Licensing requires meeting VARA's rulebooks on company structure, compliance, technology and market conduct, including an AML/CFT programme. Outside Dubai, VASPs are licensed by the SCA or the relevant financial free zone regulator, and operating without authorisation is prohibited.","refs":[{"ref":"Dubai Law No. 4 of 2022 and VARA Regulations / Rulebooks","note":"Require a VARA licence for virtual asset activities in Dubai with AML obligations."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 111 of 2022 (Virtual Assets)","note":"Sets the federal framework for regulating virtual assets and VASPs."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Requires VASPs to be licensed or registered and supervised for AML/CFT."}]},{"q":"What Travel Rule requirements apply to UAE virtual asset transfers under FATF R.16?","a":"VASPs must obtain, hold and transmit required originator and beneficiary information with virtual asset transfers, including names, account or wallet identifiers and, for the originator, address or identification details, and must make it available to authorities on request. The obligation applies to qualifying transfers and requires screening counterparties against sanctions lists and identifying the beneficiary institution. UAE VARA and SCA rules implement this Travel Rule consistent with FATF R.16.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16 (Travel Rule)","note":"Requires originator and beneficiary information to accompany virtual asset transfers."},{"ref":"VARA Compliance and Risk Management Rulebook","note":"Implements Travel Rule obligations for Dubai VASPs."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 111 of 2022 (Virtual Assets)","note":"Establishes the federal virtual asset regulatory basis for Travel Rule application."}]},{"q":"When must a UAE FI apply enhanced CDD to a VASP correspondent relationship?","a":"Enhanced due diligence is required when establishing or maintaining a correspondent-type relationship with a VASP, treating it like cross-border correspondent banking: assess the VASP's licensing status, AML/CFT controls, ownership and reputation, obtain senior management approval, document respective responsibilities, and ensure the VASP applies Travel Rule and CDD standards. EDD intensifies where the VASP is in a higher-risk jurisdiction, deals in privacy coins or unhosted wallets, or has weak controls. Relationships with unlicensed VASPs should generally be declined.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13 (correspondent relationships)","note":"Requires EDD for cross-border correspondent relationships, extended to VASPs."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, correspondent and EDD provisions","note":"Requires EDD and senior approval for higher-risk relationships."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15 / VA Guidance","note":"Applies AML standards and EDD to VASP relationships."}]},{"q":"What are the UAE CBUAE requirements for FIs dealing with unhosted wallet transactions?","a":"Financial institutions and VASPs must apply enhanced risk management to transfers involving unhosted (self-custodied) wallets, including taking reasonable measures to identify the counterparty, assessing the elevated ML/TF risk, applying blockchain analytics to trace exposure, and applying EDD or restricting or declining transactions where the counterparty cannot be adequately identified. Sanctions screening of wallet addresses and counterparties is required, and high-risk unhosted activity should feed monitoring and STR consideration. Controls should be proportionate to the assessed risk.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE / VARA guidance on virtual assets and unhosted wallets","note":"Requires enhanced risk management and counterparty identification for unhosted wallet transfers."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15 / Updated VA Guidance","note":"Addresses risks of unhosted wallets and recommends EDD and analytics."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 (TFS)","note":"Requires sanctions screening including of wallet counterparties."}]},{"q":"How does VARA's technology governance framework apply to VASP AML programmes?","a":"VARA's technology and information rulebook requires VASPs to maintain robust technology governance, cybersecurity, data integrity and resilience, which underpins AML effectiveness by ensuring reliable transaction data, secure custody, blockchain analytics integration and auditable records. AML systems for screening, monitoring and Travel Rule compliance must meet these governance and control standards, with board oversight and independent testing. Weak technology controls are treated as a deficiency affecting the firm's AML and overall compliance posture.","refs":[{"ref":"VARA Technology and Information Rulebook","note":"Sets technology governance and security standards supporting AML systems."},{"ref":"VARA Compliance and Risk Management Rulebook","note":"Requires AML controls integrated with technology governance."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Requires effective risk management of technology used by VASPs."}]},{"q":"What blockchain analytics tools are considered adequate for UAE VASP CDD?","a":"Regulators do not endorse specific vendors; adequacy is assessed by capability, meaning the tools must support wallet attribution, source and destination of funds tracing, sanctions and darknet exposure detection, risk scoring of counterparties, and Travel Rule data validation, with coverage of the asset types the VASP handles. The VASP must validate, calibrate and document the tool's effectiveness, integrate outputs into CDD, EDD and monitoring, and retain analytics records. The key test is whether the chosen solution lets the firm meet its CDD, screening and monitoring obligations.","refs":[{"ref":"VARA Compliance and Risk Management Rulebook","note":"Requires effective tools and controls for CDD, screening and monitoring rather than naming vendors."},{"ref":"FATF Updated Guidance for Virtual Assets and VASPs","note":"Recommends use of blockchain analytics for tracing and risk assessment."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, ongoing monitoring","note":"Requires monitoring capabilities sufficient to detect suspicious activity."}]}]},{"label":"Proliferation Financing (PF)","questions":[{"q":"What is the UAE's legal framework for countering proliferation financing under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"The federal AML/CFT law extends preventive obligations to the financing of proliferation of weapons of mass destruction, requiring regulated entities to assess and mitigate PF risk, implement targeted financial sanctions without delay, screen against UN and UAE lists, and report freezing actions. It is reinforced by the Cabinet Decision on targeted financial sanctions and the implementing regulation, and overseen by the Executive Office for Control and Non-Proliferation and the relevant supervisors. Entities must build PF into their enterprise risk assessment and sanctions controls.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT)","note":"Brings proliferation financing within the federal preventive and TFS framework."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 (TFS) and No. 57 of 2018","note":"Require immediate freezing and reporting under UNSCRs including for PF."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7","note":"Requires targeted financial sanctions to counter proliferation financing."}]},{"q":"Which UNSC resolutions impose immediate asset freeze obligations in UAE for PF?","a":"The principal PF-related resolutions are those imposing targeted financial sanctions on North Korea (the DPRK regime, including UNSCR 1718 and successors) and on Iran (UNSCR 2231 and predecessors), which require freezing without delay the funds and assets of designated persons and entities and prohibiting making funds available to them. The UAE implements these through its sanctions list mechanism, requiring immediate freezing on designation and prompt reporting. Entities must monitor the UN consolidated list and UAE local list for updates.","refs":[{"ref":"UNSCR 1718 (2006) and successor DPRK resolutions","note":"Impose asset freezes for proliferation related to North Korea."},{"ref":"UNSCR 2231 (2015) on Iran","note":"Imposes targeted financial restrictions implemented in the UAE."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 (TFS)","note":"Requires immediate freezing and reporting under these resolutions."}]},{"q":"What goods are considered dual-use under UAE Federal Decree-Law 26/2021 Strategic Goods Control?","a":"Dual-use goods are items, software and technology that have legitimate civilian applications but can also contribute to the development, production or delivery of weapons of mass destruction or conventional military capability, such as certain chemicals, nuclear-related materials, advanced electronics, machine tools and related technology. The strategic goods control regime requires licensing, authorisation and controls over the import, export, re-export and transit of such goods. Trade and finance involving these goods triggers enhanced scrutiny and end-user verification.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 26 of 2021 (Strategic Goods Control)","note":"Defines and regulates strategic and dual-use goods including licensing and controls."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7","note":"Links dual-use goods controls to proliferation financing sanctions."},{"ref":"UAE Executive Office for Control and Non-Proliferation guidance","note":"Provides controls and lists for strategic and dual-use goods."}]},{"q":"What is the UAE CBUAE PF Circular 2023 and what does it require from financial institutions?","a":"CBUAE guidance on proliferation financing directs licensed financial institutions to integrate PF into their enterprise-wide risk assessment, apply targeted financial sanctions without delay, screen customers, counterparties and transactions for PF and sanctions evasion indicators, scrutinise trade finance for dual-use goods and front companies, and report freezing actions and suspicions. Institutions must train staff on PF typologies and ensure governance and controls address evasion through complex ownership, transshipment and misdescribed goods. The guidance aligns UAE practice with FATF Recommendation 7.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE Guidance on Proliferation Financing for Licensed Financial Institutions","note":"Requires PF risk assessment, screening, TFS implementation and reporting."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7","note":"Requires targeted financial sanctions against proliferation financing."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 (TFS)","note":"Underpins the freezing and reporting obligations referenced in the guidance."}]},{"q":"How does FATF R.7 differ from FATF R.6 in terms of targeted financial sanctions?","a":"Recommendation 6 concerns targeted financial sanctions related to terrorism and terrorist financing (UNSCR 1267 and 1373 regimes), allowing both UN-listed and country-initiated designations. Recommendation 7 concerns targeted financial sanctions related to the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction (the DPRK and Iran UNSCR regimes), which are UN-driven and do not include an autonomous domestic designation mechanism of the 1373 type. Both require freezing without delay and prohibitions on making funds available, but they cover different threats and listing sources.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Requires TFS for terrorism and terrorist financing including UNSCR 1267 and 1373."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7","note":"Requires TFS for proliferation financing under UN resolutions."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 (TFS)","note":"Implements both terrorism and proliferation TFS in the UAE."}]},{"q":"What is the end-user certificate requirement for trade finance involving controlled goods?","a":"For trade in strategic or dual-use goods, an end-user certificate is documentation in which the stated end user confirms the identity, intended use and final destination of the goods and undertakes not to divert or re-export them in breach of controls, supporting licensing and non-proliferation checks. Financial institutions financing such trade should verify the certificate, screen the end user and parties against sanctions lists, and scrutinise for diversion or front-company red flags. Inability to obtain or verify a credible end-user certificate is a strong indicator to decline or report the transaction.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 26 of 2021 (Strategic Goods Control)","note":"Requires authorisation and end-user documentation for controlled goods trade."},{"ref":"CBUAE Guidance on Proliferation Financing / Trade Finance","note":"Expects verification of end users and screening in trade finance."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7","note":"Supports controls against diversion of goods to proliferation programmes."}]}]},{"label":"PEP Enhanced Due Diligence","questions":[{"q":"What is the definition of a Politically Exposed Person under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.14(2)?","a":"A PEP is a natural person who is or has been entrusted with prominent public functions, whether domestic, foreign, or in an international organisation, such as heads of state, senior politicians, senior government, judicial or military officials, senior executives of state-owned enterprises, and senior political party officials. The definition extends to their family members and known close associates, who must be subjected to the same enhanced measures. The categorisation is function-based, not name-list-based, so FIs must determine PEP status from the role rather than relying solely on screening tools.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.14(2)","note":"Defines PEPs to include domestic, foreign and international organisation PEPs plus their family members and close associates."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12 and Glossary","note":"Sets the international PEP definition and requires EDD covering family members and close associates."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation), Art.15","note":"Operationalises PEP identification and the related enhanced due diligence obligations."}]},{"q":"What senior management approval is required for a PEP relationship under UAE law?","a":"FIs must obtain senior management approval before establishing, or continuing where an existing customer is found to be a PEP, a business relationship with a PEP. This approval is a documented governance step and must be recorded with the rationale and the EDD evidence reviewed. The requirement applies to foreign PEPs in all cases and to domestic and international organisation PEPs assessed as higher risk.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.15","note":"Requires senior management approval to establish or continue a PEP relationship, with source of wealth and funds established."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires obtaining senior management approval for establishing or continuing PEP business relationships."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.14","note":"Anchors the enhanced measures applicable to PEP relationships in primary law."}]},{"q":"What is the difference between source of wealth and source of funds in PEP EDD?","a":"Source of wealth describes the origin of the customer's total accumulated assets and net worth, for example inheritance, business ownership, investments, or employment income over time. Source of funds describes the origin of the specific monies used in a particular transaction or placed into the account, for example a property sale or a salary payment. For PEPs, FIs must take reasonable measures to establish both, because wealth verifies overall legitimacy while funds verify the specific flows being moved.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.15","note":"Requires reasonable measures to establish both the source of wealth and source of funds of PEPs."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires establishing source of wealth and source of funds for PEP customers as part of EDD."}]},{"q":"How should a UAE FI handle a customer who becomes a PEP during an existing relationship?","a":"Once a customer is identified as having become a PEP, the FI must escalate the relationship to enhanced due diligence, obtain senior management approval to continue it, establish source of wealth and source of funds, and apply enhanced ongoing monitoring. The trigger event should be documented and the customer risk rating updated accordingly. Failure to re-classify on a change of status is a common supervisory finding.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.15","note":"Requires the PEP enhanced measures and senior management approval to apply when an existing customer becomes a PEP."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Extends PEP EDD obligations to customers who acquire PEP status during the relationship."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.14","note":"Provides the statutory basis for ongoing enhanced measures for PEP relationships."}]},{"q":"What ongoing monitoring frequency is required for foreign PEPs under UAE law?","a":"Foreign PEPs are always treated as high risk and must be subject to enhanced ongoing monitoring, which in practice means more frequent periodic reviews than standard customers, typically at least annually, plus event-driven reviews on material changes. UAE law does not fix a single numeric interval but requires the frequency to be commensurate with the high-risk rating and documented in the FI's policies. The monitoring must scrutinise transactions for consistency with the established source of wealth and expected activity.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.15 and Art.7","note":"Requires enhanced and ongoing monitoring of PEP relationships commensurate with high risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12 and Recommendation 10","note":"Require enhanced ongoing monitoring of PEP relationships and risk-based review frequency."}]},{"q":"Does the UAE define a time limit after which a former PEP ceases to be treated as a PEP?","a":"UAE law does not impose a fixed expiry period; whether a former PEP continues to warrant enhanced measures is determined on a risk-sensitive basis after the person leaves the prominent public function. FIs must assess the residual risk posed by the individual's former position and influence and may only step down enhanced measures once that risk is judged to have abated. International guidance commonly references a minimum of around 12 months as a reference point, but the decision must be risk-based and documented, not automatic.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12, Interpretive Note","note":"Requires a risk-based assessment of a former PEP rather than an automatic time cut-off."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.15","note":"Frames PEP measures as risk-based, requiring continued enhanced treatment while risk remains."}]}]},{"label":"Cross-Border Correspondent Banking","questions":[{"q":"What FATF R.13 requirements apply to UAE banks establishing correspondent relationships?","a":"FATF Recommendation 13 requires that, before entering a cross-border correspondent relationship, the bank gather sufficient information to understand the respondent's business and reputation, assess its AML/CFT controls, obtain senior management approval, and clearly document the respective AML/CFT responsibilities of each institution. It prohibits relationships with shell banks and requires assurance that the respondent does not permit its accounts to be used by shell banks. For payable-through accounts, the bank must be satisfied the respondent has performed CDD on its own customers with direct access.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13","note":"Sets the core cross-border correspondent banking due diligence, senior management approval and shell-bank prohibition requirements."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.16","note":"Transposes the correspondent banking due diligence obligations into UAE implementing regulation."}]},{"q":"What is a payable-through account and why is it prohibited without additional controls?","a":"A payable-through account is a correspondent account that the respondent bank's own customers can use directly to transact on their own behalf through the correspondent's books. It is high risk because the correspondent bank does not have a direct relationship with, or visibility over, those underlying customers. It is permitted only where the correspondent is satisfied the respondent has performed CDD on those customers and can provide relevant identification data on request.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13","note":"Permits payable-through accounts only where the respondent has conducted CDD on customers with direct access and can supply data on request."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.16","note":"Imposes the payable-through account control conditions under UAE law."}]},{"q":"What is the UAE CBUAE requirement for nested correspondent account relationships?","a":"Nested relationships, where a respondent provides correspondent services to its own downstream institutions through the UAE bank's account, must be identified and risk-assessed, and the UAE bank must understand the nature and extent of the downstream clearing. The CBUAE expects banks to obtain transparency over nested or downstream correspondents, set clear expectations in the relationship, and monitor for activity inconsistent with the respondent's stated profile. Unidentified nesting that masks high-risk underlying institutions is a key supervisory concern.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE Guidance for Licensed Financial Institutions on Correspondent Banking","note":"Expects identification, risk assessment and monitoring of nested or downstream correspondent activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13","note":"Underpins the requirement to understand the respondent's customer base, including downstream institutions."}]},{"q":"What due diligence must a UAE correspondent bank perform on a respondent bank's AML programme?","a":"The bank must gather enough information to fully understand the respondent's business, ownership and management, reputation, regulatory and supervisory status, and any history of AML/CFT investigations or enforcement actions. It must then assess the quality and adequacy of the respondent's AML/CFT controls before approving the relationship at senior management level. This assessment must be refreshed periodically and documented.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13","note":"Requires gathering information on the respondent and assessing its AML/CFT controls before establishing the relationship."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.16","note":"Requires assessment of the respondent's AML/CFT systems and senior management approval under UAE law."}]},{"q":"How should a UAE FI handle a correspondent bank in a FATF grey-list jurisdiction?","a":"A respondent in a jurisdiction under FATF increased monitoring (grey list) warrants enhanced due diligence proportionate to the heightened risk, including closer scrutiny of the respondent's controls, the country's strategic deficiencies, and enhanced transaction monitoring. The FI should apply countermeasures only where FATF or the UAE authorities specifically call for them, but must in all cases document a risk-based justification for continuing the relationship. Senior management should review and approve the elevated risk.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 19","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence and, where called for, countermeasures for higher-risk jurisdictions."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.4 and Art.16","note":"Require EDD for high-risk countries and correspondent relationships."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13","note":"Requires understanding and assessing the respondent's controls before and during the relationship."}]},{"q":"What is the 'shell bank' prohibition under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 and FATF R.13?","a":"A shell bank is a bank with no physical presence or meaningful mind and management in the country where it is incorporated and licensed, and which is not affiliated with a regulated financial group subject to consolidated supervision. UAE FIs are prohibited from establishing or maintaining correspondent or any other relationship with shell banks, and must satisfy themselves that respondent banks do not allow their accounts to be used by shell banks. This is an absolute prohibition rather than a risk-based control.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 and Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.16","note":"Prohibits dealing with shell banks and requires assurance respondents do not service shell banks."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13","note":"Prohibits correspondent relationships with shell banks and use of accounts by shell banks."}]}]},{"label":"High-Risk Customer Remediation","questions":[{"q":"What triggers a mandatory CDD refresh for a high-risk customer under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.15?","a":"A refresh is triggered by the high-risk classification itself, which requires more frequent periodic reviews, and by event-driven triggers such as material changes in ownership or control, unusual or unexpected transaction activity, adverse media or sanctions hits, a change to PEP status, or doubts about the veracity of previously obtained CDD. The FI must keep CDD information current and review it whenever a triggering event indicates the customer profile may have changed. High-risk customers must be reviewed on a shorter cycle than standard or low-risk customers.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.15","note":"Requires ongoing due diligence and keeping CDD records current, with enhanced measures for high-risk customers."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.7","note":"Requires updating CDD data on a risk basis and re-performing CDD on doubt or triggering events."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing due diligence and updating of CDD information, intensified for higher-risk customers."}]},{"q":"What account restrictions can a UAE FI impose pending completion of refresh CDD?","a":"Where CDD cannot be completed or updated, the FI may restrict or suspend the account, decline to process transactions, block outgoing transfers, or otherwise limit activity until the required information is obtained. These measures should be applied in a manner that avoids tipping off, and if CDD remains incomplete the FI must consider terminating the relationship and filing an STR. Any restriction should be documented and proportionate to the risk.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.7 and Art.9","note":"Requires that the FI not open or maintain the account and consider an STR where CDD cannot be completed."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.15","note":"Supports applying ongoing due diligence and restrictive measures where information is incomplete."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires that the FI not establish or continue the relationship where CDD cannot be performed and consider reporting."}]},{"q":"When must a UAE FI terminate a business relationship under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.15(3)?","a":"The FI must terminate, or not establish, the relationship where it cannot complete the required CDD or ongoing due diligence, where it cannot identify or verify the customer or beneficial owner, or where it cannot satisfy itself about the purpose and legitimacy of the activity. On termination in these circumstances the FI must assess whether a suspicious transaction report is required. Termination must avoid tipping off the customer about any related reporting.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.15(3)","note":"Requires terminating or refraining from the relationship where CDD cannot be completed and considering an STR."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.9","note":"Requires not proceeding with the relationship and considering an STR when CDD fails."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires terminating or declining the relationship where CDD cannot be completed."}]},{"q":"Should a UAE FI file an exit STR when terminating a high-risk relationship?","a":"If the reason for termination involves a suspicion of money laundering or terrorist financing, or doubts about the legitimacy of funds or the customer, the FI must file an STR via goAML regardless of whether the relationship is being exited. Termination does not discharge the reporting obligation, and exiting a customer without filing where suspicion exists is itself a breach. Where termination is purely commercial with no suspicion, no STR is required.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.26","note":"Requires reporting suspicious transactions to the FIU whenever suspicion arises, including on exit."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.17","note":"Sets the obligation to file STRs to the FIU when there are reasonable grounds to suspect ML or TF."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious transactions promptly to the FIU."}]},{"q":"What documentation is required to support a decision to continue a high-risk relationship?","a":"The FI must retain the EDD file showing identification and verification of the customer and beneficial owners, the established source of wealth and source of funds, the rationale for the high-risk rating, the enhanced monitoring plan, and the documented senior management approval to continue the relationship. The decision basis and supporting evidence must be auditable and retained for at least five years. This file should demonstrate that residual risk is understood and within the FI's risk appetite.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.4, Art.15 and Art.24","note":"Require EDD, senior management approval and retention of CDD and decision records for at least five years."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.16","note":"Requires record-keeping of CDD, transactions and supporting documentation for the prescribed period."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 10 and 11","note":"Require EDD for higher risk and record retention of CDD and supporting records."}]},{"q":"What is the remediation timeline for a customer classified as high-risk after post-onboarding monitoring?","a":"UAE law does not prescribe a single fixed number of days, but remediation must be prompt and risk-based, with the FI applying enhanced measures, obtaining missing CDD, and updating the risk profile without undue delay once high risk is identified. FIs typically set internal SLAs, commonly 30 to 90 days, in their policies, with interim restrictions applied if remediation cannot be completed. The key obligation is that CDD be kept current and that incomplete remediation leads to restriction, termination, and STR consideration.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.15","note":"Requires keeping due diligence current and applying enhanced measures to high-risk customers without undue delay."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.7","note":"Requires timely updating of CDD information on a risk basis and action where it cannot be completed."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires intensified ongoing due diligence and updated CDD for higher-risk relationships."}]}]},{"label":"Sanctions Compliance Programme","questions":[{"q":"What sanctions lists must a UAE financial institution screen against under UAE law?","a":"FIs must screen against the UN Security Council Consolidated List and the UAE Local Terrorist List (Local List) maintained under the targeted financial sanctions regime, with screening at onboarding and continuously against updates. Many FIs also screen relevant unilateral lists such as OFAC, EU and UK on a risk basis to manage exposure, but the mandatory UAE obligation centres on the UN and UAE lists. Screening must be conducted without delay following any list update.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020","note":"Requires implementation of UN Security Council and UAE Local List targeted financial sanctions without delay."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides the statutory framework for implementing targeted financial sanctions in the UAE."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 6 and 7","note":"Require implementation of targeted financial sanctions related to terrorism and proliferation financing."}]},{"q":"What is the UAE EOCN and what obligations does Cabinet Decision 74/2020 impose?","a":"The EOCN is the Executive Office for Control and Non-Proliferation, the UAE authority responsible for the targeted financial sanctions regime. Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 requires FIs to screen against the UN and Local lists, freeze without delay and without prior notice the funds and assets of designated persons, refrain from making funds available to them, and report freezing or attempted dealings to the EOCN and the supervisor within the prescribed timeframe. It also governs listing, de-listing and the handling of false positives.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020","note":"Establishes the EOCN-administered TFS regime, freeze-without-delay duties and reporting obligations."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 6 and 7","note":"Require freezing without delay of designated persons' assets under UN sanctions regimes."}]},{"q":"What is the timeline for reporting frozen assets to UAE EOCN after a designation hit?","a":"On a confirmed match, the FI must freeze the funds immediately and without prior notice and report the freezing measure to the EOCN and to its supervisory authority within the timeframe set by the regime, which is without delay and in practice within a small number of business days of taking the action. The FI must also report any attempted transactions involving the designated party. Speed is essential because the obligation is to act without delay rather than after internal review cycles.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020","note":"Requires freezing without delay and prompt reporting of the freeze and any attempted dealings to the EOCN and supervisor."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Requires freezing without delay and without prior notice of designated persons' funds."}]},{"q":"How does secondary sanctions risk from OFAC affect UAE financial institutions?","a":"OFAC secondary sanctions can target non-US persons, including UAE FIs, for knowingly facilitating significant transactions with certain sanctioned parties, exposing them to loss of US correspondent access or US-market restrictions even absent a US nexus. UAE FIs therefore commonly screen against OFAC SDN and sectoral lists and incorporate secondary-sanctions risk into their risk assessment, particularly given reliance on USD clearing. While not a direct UAE legal mandate, managing this exposure is a prudent and widely expected control.","refs":[{"ref":"OFAC Sanctions Programs and Secondary Sanctions Authorities","note":"Allow designation of non-US persons facilitating significant transactions with sanctioned parties."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020","note":"Requires a risk-based sanctions programme into which secondary-sanctions exposure is reasonably integrated."}]},{"q":"What is the tipping-off prohibition in the context of sanctions screening under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"The tipping-off prohibition forbids disclosing to a customer or third party that a report has been or will be made, or that an investigation is underway, where doing so could prejudice the matter. In a sanctions context, while the freeze itself must be implemented and the designated person will become aware funds are blocked, the FI must not disclose any related suspicious transaction reporting or investigative steps. Internal handling and communications should be designed to avoid prejudicing reporting and enforcement.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.26","note":"Prohibits tipping off about reporting or investigation of suspicious transactions."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.19","note":"Sets out the prohibition on disclosing reporting or investigation to customers or third parties."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 21","note":"Prohibits tipping off and protects reporting institutions and staff."}]},{"q":"What is the difference between a designated entity hit and a PEP hit in terms of legal obligations?","a":"A confirmed designated entity (sanctions) hit triggers a mandatory legal duty to freeze assets without delay, refrain from dealing, and report to the EOCN; it is a hard prohibition not subject to risk appetite. A PEP hit does not require freezing or blocking; it triggers enhanced due diligence, senior management approval, source of wealth and funds checks, and enhanced monitoring, and the relationship may lawfully continue on a risk-managed basis. Conflating the two, for example freezing a PEP or merely monitoring a sanctioned party, is a serious error.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020","note":"Requires freezing and reporting for designated persons under targeted financial sanctions."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.15","note":"Requires EDD and senior management approval for PEPs, not freezing."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 6 and 12","note":"Distinguish mandatory TFS freezing from risk-based PEP enhanced due diligence."}]}]},{"label":"Transaction Monitoring Calibration","questions":[{"q":"What is the CBUAE expectation for transaction monitoring system calibration documentation?","a":"The CBUAE expects FIs to maintain documented evidence of how monitoring scenarios, thresholds and parameters are designed, tested, tuned and approved, mapped to the FI's risk assessment and customer and product profile. This includes above-the-line and below-the-line testing, model validation, change-control records, and periodic review approvals by appropriate governance. Calibration must be demonstrably risk-based and revisited as the risk profile or typologies evolve.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidelines for Licensed Financial Institutions","note":"Expect documented, risk-based design, tuning and validation of transaction monitoring systems."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing monitoring of transactions consistent with the customer risk profile."}]},{"q":"What alert-to-SAR conversion rate benchmarks are considered adequate by UAE regulators?","a":"UAE regulators do not publish a fixed numeric alert-to-SAR conversion benchmark, and the metric must be interpreted in context rather than treated as a target. A very low conversion rate may indicate poorly calibrated or excessive rules, while an unusually high rate may suggest under-detection or thresholds set too tightly; the focus is on effectiveness and the quality of disposition, not hitting a ratio. FIs should monitor the metric as one of several indicators and explain movements rather than engineering a particular figure.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidelines for Licensed Financial Institutions","note":"Emphasise effectiveness and quality of monitoring outcomes rather than prescribing a conversion ratio."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of all genuinely suspicious transactions, which conversion analysis should support not constrain."}]},{"q":"How should a UAE FI document the rationale for transaction monitoring thresholds?","a":"Each scenario and threshold should be supported by a written rationale linking it to specific ML/TF risks, customer segments, product behaviours and relevant typologies, with the supporting data analysis, test results and the date and authority of approval. The documentation should record assumptions, any below-the-line testing justifying the setting, and the planned review cycle. This makes the calibration defensible to supervisors and reproducible by independent reviewers.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidelines for Licensed Financial Institutions","note":"Expect documented, risk-linked justification and governance approval for monitoring thresholds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires risk-based application of controls, supported by documented risk assessment."}]},{"q":"What is the risk of over-suppression of transaction monitoring alerts from a regulatory perspective?","a":"Over-suppression, whether by setting thresholds too high, disabling scenarios, or auto-closing alerts without genuine review, risks missing genuinely suspicious activity and failing to file required STRs, which is a reportable AML control failure. Supervisors view suppression aimed at reducing workload rather than at reducing false positives on a sound risk basis as a serious deficiency. Any suppression or tuning that reduces coverage must be risk-justified, tested below the line, documented and governance-approved.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.26","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious transactions, which over-suppression can cause an FI to miss."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidelines for Licensed Financial Institutions","note":"Expect tuning decisions to be risk-justified and tested, not driven by alert volume reduction."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires effective ongoing transaction monitoring proportionate to risk."}]},{"q":"How should typology changes identified by the UAE FIU be incorporated into TM rules?","a":"When the FIU issues new typologies, red-flag indicators or strategic analysis, the FI should assess their relevance to its business, translate applicable indicators into new or amended monitoring scenarios, and update its risk assessment and training accordingly. Changes should pass through the model change-control and testing process and be documented with the FIU source referenced. This closes the feedback loop between national intelligence and the FI's detection capability.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidelines for Licensed Financial Institutions","note":"Expect FIs to act on FIU typologies and guidance in their monitoring and risk assessment."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires keeping the risk assessment and controls current as new risks and typologies emerge."}]},{"q":"What does FATF R.10 require specifically for ongoing transaction monitoring?","a":"FATF Recommendation 10 requires FIs to conduct ongoing due diligence on the business relationship, including scrutinising transactions throughout the relationship to ensure they are consistent with the FI's knowledge of the customer, their business and risk profile, and where necessary their source of funds. It also requires keeping CDD documents, data and information up to date, particularly for higher-risk categories. Monitoring must be applied on a risk-sensitive basis across the customer life cycle.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing scrutiny of transactions against the customer profile and keeping CDD information current."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.7","note":"Transposes the ongoing monitoring and CDD-updating obligation into UAE regulation."}]}]},{"label":"Annual AML Programme Self-Assessment","questions":[{"q":"What are the required components of an annual AML/CFT programme review under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"The annual review should assess the adequacy and effectiveness of the FI's policies, controls and procedures, the enterprise-wide risk assessment, CDD and EDD, sanctions and PEP screening, transaction monitoring, STR reporting, record-keeping, training, and the independent audit function. It should identify gaps, document remediation actions with owners and timelines, and be approved by senior management and the Board. The review evidences that the risk-based programme remains commensurate with the FI's risks.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.19","note":"Requires FIs to maintain and review adequate AML/CFT policies, controls, internal audit and training."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.20 and Art.21","note":"Require risk-based internal policies, an independent audit function and ongoing programme assessment."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires internal controls and an independent audit function to test the AML/CFT programme."}]},{"q":"What must a UAE FI's annual enterprise-wide risk assessment (EWRA) cover?","a":"The EWRA must identify and assess ML/TF risks across customers, products and services, delivery channels, geographies and transactions, taking into account the national risk assessment and relevant FIU typologies. It must document risk ratings, the mitigating controls applied, and residual risk, and feed into the risk-based allocation of compliance resources. It should be updated at least annually and on material change to the business.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.4","note":"Requires identifying and assessing ML/TF risks across customers, products, channels and geographies."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires FIs to identify, assess and document their ML/TF risks and apply a risk-based approach."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.16 and Art.19","note":"Anchor the risk assessment and risk-based controls obligations in primary law."}]},{"q":"What is the CBUAE inspection framework for AML/CFT programmes and what are common findings?","a":"The CBUAE conducts risk-based onsite and offsite supervision assessing governance, the risk assessment, CDD and beneficial ownership, screening, transaction monitoring, STR quality and timeliness, training and independent audit. Common findings include weak or outdated risk assessments, incomplete beneficial ownership identification, poorly calibrated or under-tested monitoring systems, late or low-quality STRs, and insufficient board oversight and MIS. Deficiencies can lead to remediation directions and administrative penalties.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Examination and Supervision Framework","note":"Sets the risk-based onsite and offsite inspection scope for licensed financial institutions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 26 and 27","note":"Require risk-based supervision and adequate supervisory powers and sanctions."}]},{"q":"What governance documentation must a UAE MLRO maintain for the annual AML programme review?","a":"The MLRO should maintain the approved AML/CFT policies and procedures, the EWRA, the annual MLRO report to the Board, MIS and metrics, STR and internal report logs, training records and attendance, sanctions and PEP screening governance records, independent audit reports, and remediation tracking with sign-offs. These records evidence that the programme was reviewed, that deficiencies were escalated, and that the Board exercised oversight. They must be retained in line with the five-year record-keeping requirement.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.21 and Art.24","note":"Require the compliance officer function, reporting to senior management, and record retention for five years."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.16 and Art.19","note":"Require record-keeping and maintenance of AML/CFT controls and reporting."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires a compliance management arrangement and supporting documentation."}]},{"q":"What training requirements must be satisfied annually under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.19(3)?","a":"FIs must provide ongoing AML/CFT training to relevant employees so they understand the law, the FI's policies, how to identify and report suspicious activity, sanctions and PEP obligations, and emerging typologies. Training must be role-appropriate, refreshed regularly (at least annually), and its delivery and attendance documented. New joiners should be trained promptly and the programme should reflect the FI's risk profile.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.19(3)","note":"Requires ongoing AML/CFT training and awareness programmes for employees."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.20","note":"Requires continuous training and development programmes for staff on AML/CFT."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires ongoing employee training as part of internal controls."}]},{"q":"What are the penalties for an inadequate AML programme under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"Supervisors may impose a range of administrative penalties for AML/CFT failures, including warnings, significant monetary fines, restrictions on business, suspension or replacement of managers, and suspension or withdrawal of the licence. Serious or wilful violations, such as failing to report suspicious transactions or facilitating money laundering, can attract criminal liability, including imprisonment and substantial fines, and penalties may apply to both the institution and responsible individuals. The exact amounts are set out in the law and its implementing regulations.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (penalty provisions)","note":"Provides administrative and criminal penalties for AML/CFT violations and failures to report."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.14 and related","note":"Authorise supervisory administrative penalties for non-compliance."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 35","note":"Requires proportionate and dissuasive sanctions for AML/CFT failures."}]}]},{"label":"Board & Senior Management AML Oversight","questions":[{"q":"What are the AML/CFT governance obligations of the Board of Directors under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"The Board is ultimately responsible for ensuring the FI has an effective, risk-based AML/CFT framework, including approving policies and the risk appetite, ensuring adequate resources and an independent compliance function, and overseeing programme effectiveness through regular reporting. It must ensure senior management implements controls and that deficiencies are remediated. The Board cannot delegate ultimate accountability for AML/CFT compliance.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.19","note":"Requires governance arrangements ensuring adequate and effective AML/CFT controls."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.20 and Art.21","note":"Require senior management responsibility for policies, the compliance function and oversight."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires group and institutional governance over AML/CFT internal controls."}]},{"q":"What management information (MIS) must be reported to the Board on AML performance?","a":"Board MIS should include the AML/CFT risk profile and EWRA outcomes, STR volumes and quality and timeliness, alert and case backlogs, sanctions and PEP screening results, high-risk customer and PEP populations, training completion, audit and regulatory findings and remediation status, and any material incidents. The reporting should let the Board judge programme effectiveness, not just activity counts. It should be timely, accurate and clearly linked to risk and remediation.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.21","note":"Requires the compliance officer to report to senior management on AML/CFT matters."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires governance with adequate information flow to oversee AML/CFT effectiveness."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidelines for Licensed Financial Institutions","note":"Expect meaningful, effectiveness-focused MIS reporting to the Board and senior management."}]},{"q":"What is the personal liability of senior management for AML failures under UAE law?","a":"Senior managers and responsible officers can be held personally liable for AML/CFT failures, facing administrative penalties such as fines, suspension, dismissal or being barred from working in the sector, and criminal liability including imprisonment and fines where they knowingly fail to report or facilitate money laundering. Liability attaches to the responsible natural persons in addition to the institution. This underscores the need for documented oversight and timely escalation.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (penalty provisions)","note":"Provides for administrative and criminal liability of responsible individuals for AML/CFT violations."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.14","note":"Authorises administrative penalties applicable to responsible persons and managers."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 35","note":"Requires sanctions applicable to directors and senior management, not only the institution."}]},{"q":"How should a UAE FI document Board approval of the AML/CFT risk appetite statement?","a":"The risk appetite statement should be a written, Board-approved document setting the types and levels of ML/TF risk the FI will and will not accept, with the approval recorded in Board minutes including date, attendees and any conditions. It should link to the EWRA and to thresholds, prohibited customer types and escalation triggers, and be reviewed at least annually. The minutes and version-controlled statement together evidence the Board's decision.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.20","note":"Requires Board and senior management approval of risk-based AML/CFT policies and controls."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a documented risk-based approach reflecting the institution's risk tolerance."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.19","note":"Requires effective governance over the AML/CFT framework."}]},{"q":"What is the MLRO's reporting line and independence requirement under UAE AML rules?","a":"The FI must appoint a qualified compliance officer (MLRO) of appropriate seniority who is independent, has direct access to senior management and the Board, and has sufficient authority, resources and access to all relevant customer and transaction information. The role must be free from conflicts that could compromise the objective handling of internal reports and STRs. The MLRO is the focal point for suspicious transaction reporting to the FIU.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.21","note":"Requires appointment of an independent, suitably senior compliance officer with access to information and authority."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires a compliance officer at management level as part of internal controls."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.26","note":"Establishes the reporting channel to the FIU through which the MLRO files STRs."}]},{"q":"What is the three lines of defence model in the context of UAE AML/CFT governance?","a":"The first line is the business and operations that own and manage AML/CFT risk day to day, applying CDD, screening and monitoring at the customer interface. The second line is the independent compliance and AML function, led by the MLRO, that sets policy, advises, monitors and challenges the first line. The third line is independent internal audit, which periodically and objectively tests the effectiveness of the whole framework and reports to the Board or audit committee.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.20 and Art.21","note":"Require business controls, an independent compliance function and an independent audit function."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires internal controls including a compliance function and independent audit to test the system."}]}]},{"label":"goAML Filing Quality & FIU Engagement","questions":[{"q":"What are the mandatory fields in a UAE FIU goAML STR submission?","a":"A goAML STR must identify the reporting entity and MLRO, the subject persons or entities with full identification details, the accounts and transactions involved with amounts, dates and counterparties, and a clear narrative grounds-for-suspicion explaining why the activity is suspicious and the supporting indicators. Supporting documents should be attached. Incomplete identification or a weak narrative is the most common quality failing and undermines the report's value.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE FIU goAML Reporting Guidance","note":"Specifies the mandatory subject, account, transaction and reason-for-suspicion fields for STR submissions."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.17","note":"Requires complete reporting of suspicious transactions to the FIU with supporting information."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt and complete reporting of suspicious transactions to the FIU."}]},{"q":"What is the 2-business-day STR filing deadline and when does it start running under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.26?","a":"The obligation is to report suspicious transactions to the FIU without delay once reasonable grounds for suspicion are formed. Where an internal time standard such as two business days is applied, the clock starts when the MLRO concludes there are reasonable grounds to suspect ML, TF or proceeds of crime, not when the alert first generated. The overarching legal standard is promptness, and any internal SLA must serve that without-delay requirement.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.26","note":"Requires reporting suspicious transactions to the FIU without delay upon reasonable grounds for suspicion."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.17","note":"Requires filing the STR to the FIU promptly once suspicion is established."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting promptly to the FIU when suspicion of ML or TF arises."}]},{"q":"What is the tipping-off prohibition and how does it interact with the STR filing process?","a":"The tipping-off prohibition bars the FI and its staff from disclosing to the customer or any third party that an STR has been or will be filed, or that an investigation is underway, where this could prejudice the matter. It does not prevent the FI from declining transactions or exiting a relationship, but communications and reasons given must be handled so they do not reveal the report. Breaching the prohibition is itself an offence.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.26","note":"Prohibits disclosing the existence or content of an STR or related investigation."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.19","note":"Sets the prohibition on tipping off customers or third parties about reporting."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 21","note":"Prohibits tipping off and protects FIs reporting in good faith."}]},{"q":"What voluntary disclosure obligations exist under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 beyond STRs?","a":"Beyond standard STRs, FIs must respond to FIU requests for additional information, and the goAML system supports other report types such as suspicious activity reports, attempted transaction reports, and additional information files (AIF) to supplement prior reports. FIs should proactively file follow-up or additional information when new facts emerge on a previously reported subject. Cooperation with FIU requests is mandatory and must also avoid tipping off.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.26","note":"Requires reporting and provision of additional information to the FIU on request."},{"ref":"UAE FIU goAML Reporting Guidance","note":"Provides for additional information and supplementary report types beyond the base STR."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious transactions, including attempted transactions."}]},{"q":"How should a UAE MLRO handle a goAML STR that was filed in error?","a":"The MLRO should not silently delete or ignore the report; instead, the error should be documented internally and corrected through goAML by submitting an additional information or correction filing that clarifies or withdraws the erroneous content, in line with FIU procedures. The FI should retain a record of the original filing, the error, and the corrective action for audit purposes. If in doubt about how to amend, the MLRO should consult the FIU rather than leave inaccurate intelligence in the system.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE FIU goAML Reporting Guidance","note":"Provides correction and additional information mechanisms for amending submitted reports."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019, Art.24","note":"Requires retention of reporting records, including corrections, for at least five years."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.26","note":"Requires accurate reporting and cooperation with the FIU."}]},{"q":"What feedback does the UAE FIU provide to reporting entities after an STR is filed?","a":"The FIU may acknowledge receipt and, where appropriate, issue requests for additional information, dissemination notices, or instructions such as a freeze or hold on funds, but it generally does not provide case-by-case outcome feedback for confidentiality and operational reasons. The FIU also publishes typologies, trends, and strategic feedback to help reporting entities improve report quality. FIs should treat any FIU instructions as actionable and confidential, avoiding tipping off.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE FIU goAML Reporting Guidance","note":"Describes acknowledgement, additional information requests and instructions issued to reporting entities."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 34","note":"Requires authorities to provide feedback and guidance to reporting entities."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art.26","note":"Establishes the FIU reporting relationship and duty to act on instructions."}]}]},{"label":"Layering Detection & Asset Tracing","questions":[{"q":"What are the three ML stages (placement, layering, integration) and how does the UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 address each?","a":"Placement introduces illicit cash into the financial system, layering moves and disguises it through complex transactions, and integration returns it to the economy as apparently legitimate wealth. UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 criminalises the laundering of proceeds across all three stages by penalising the conversion, transfer, concealment, acquisition, possession, and use of property known to be criminal proceeds, regardless of where in the cycle the conduct occurs. The offence is autonomous, so a predicate conviction is not required to prosecute laundering at any stage.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Defines and criminalises money laundering covering conversion, transfer, concealment, and use of criminal proceeds across all laundering phases."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 3","note":"Requires countries to criminalise money laundering on the Vienna and Palermo Convention basis, covering all stages of the laundering process."}]},{"q":"What structuring patterns indicate placement-stage ML under UAE CTR threshold rules?","a":"Classic placement-stage structuring (smurfing) involves breaking large cash sums into multiple deposits kept just below mandatory cash reporting thresholds, using several individuals, accounts, or branches on the same or consecutive days. UAE indicators include repeated round-amount cash deposits below the AED 55,000 designated reporting level, rapid deposit-then-withdrawal cycles, and third parties making deposits into an account they do not control. Such patterns must be assessed for a suspicious transaction report irrespective of whether any single transaction breaches a numeric threshold.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious transactions even where structured below numeric thresholds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting to the FIU of funds suspected to be proceeds of crime, including structured placement activity."}]},{"q":"What round-trip transaction indicators indicate layering per FATF typologies?","a":"Round-tripping indicators include funds sent out and returned to the originator through a chain of intermediaries, shell companies, or related-party entities with no clear economic purpose, often via offshore or secrecy jurisdictions. Other markers are mirror trades, back-to-back loans where the borrower's own funds serve as collateral, circular invoicing between connected parties, and rapid pass-through of funds that leaves little or no balance. These patterns disguise the audit trail and origin of the funds, the hallmark of the layering stage.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Money Laundering Typologies","note":"Identify round-tripping, mirror trades, and back-to-back loans as layering techniques to obscure the source of funds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires scrutiny of complex, unusual transactions with no apparent economic or lawful purpose."}]},{"q":"How does the UAE confiscation framework under Federal Law 4/2002 apply to traced criminal proceeds?","a":"UAE Federal Law No. 4 of 2002 established the original AML confiscation regime, allowing courts to confiscate funds, proceeds, and instrumentalities of laundering offences, including property of equivalent value where the proceeds themselves cannot be located. Traced proceeds can be subject to provisional freezing and seizure pending trial, with confiscation ordered on conviction. This framework has been superseded and expanded by later AML laws but the confiscation and value-based recovery principles remain central to UAE asset recovery.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Law No. 4 of 2002","note":"Provides for freezing, seizure, and confiscation of laundering proceeds and instrumentalities, including equivalent-value confiscation."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 4","note":"Requires confiscation regimes covering laundered property, proceeds, instrumentalities, and property of corresponding value."}]},{"q":"What MLAT frameworks are available for UAE asset tracing to offshore jurisdictions?","a":"The UAE conducts mutual legal assistance through bilateral MLAT and extradition treaties, the UN Convention against Corruption and the Palermo Convention as multilateral bases, and GCC and Arab League judicial cooperation agreements. Requests are channelled through the competent UAE judicial authority and the Ministry of Justice as central authority, and the FIU exchanges financial intelligence directly through the Egmont Group. These channels support tracing, freezing, and repatriation of assets located in offshore jurisdictions.","refs":[{"ref":"UNCAC and UN Palermo Convention","note":"Provide multilateral bases for mutual legal assistance, asset tracing, and recovery between states parties."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 37 and 38","note":"Require countries to provide the widest mutual legal assistance and to act on foreign requests to identify, freeze, and confiscate assets."}]},{"q":"What evidence standard is required for asset restraint under UAE Federal Law 35/1992?","a":"UAE Federal Law No. 35 of 1992, the Criminal Procedure Law, allows the Public Prosecution to order provisional seizure or freezing of assets connected to a crime on the basis of reasonable grounds or sufficient evidence linking the property to the offence under investigation, rather than the higher trial standard of proof. The restraint is a precautionary measure preserving assets pending investigation and trial, and is subject to judicial oversight. Final confiscation requires conviction to the criminal evidentiary standard.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Law No. 35 of 1992 (Criminal Procedure Law)","note":"Empowers the Public Prosecution to order provisional seizure and freezing of crime-related assets during investigation."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 4","note":"Requires authorities to be able to freeze or seize property subject to confiscation expeditiously."}]}]},{"label":"Real Estate Money Laundering","questions":[{"q":"What off-plan property red flags must UAE DNFBP real estate agents report under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"Red flags include purchase of multiple off-plan units by one buyer or connected parties, rapid assignment or flipping of contracts before completion, payments from unrelated third parties or unknown offshore sources, and reluctance to disclose beneficial ownership. Sudden large cash or crypto settlements, prices inconsistent with market value, and buyers indifferent to location or specifications are also reportable indicators. UAE real estate brokers are designated DNFBPs and must file a suspicious transaction report with the FIU through the goAML system when such indicators arise.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Treats real estate brokers as DNFBPs obliged to apply CDD and report suspicious transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Guidance on ML/TF Risks in the Real Estate Sector (2022)","note":"Sets out off-plan and contract-flipping red flags requiring scrutiny by real estate professionals."}]},{"q":"When is a UAE real estate transaction classified as all-cash and what EDD applies?","a":"A transaction is treated as all-cash when full or partial settlement is made through physical cash, virtual assets, or single or linked cash payments at or above AED 55,000, which triggers a mandatory Real Estate Activity Report to the FIU. The agent must apply enhanced due diligence by verifying the buyer's and beneficial owner's identity, establishing and documenting source of funds and source of wealth, and obtaining senior management approval where risk is high. The supporting records and rationale must be retained.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Requires real estate dealers to report single or linked cash and virtual-asset transactions at or above AED 55,000."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires enhanced CDD including source of funds verification for higher-risk transactions."}]},{"q":"What third-party payment red flags trigger STR obligations for UAE real estate agents?","a":"Payments funded by a person with no apparent relationship to the buyer, settlements split across multiple unconnected payers, funds routed through lawyer or company escrow accounts to obscure origin, and payments from high-risk or sanctioned jurisdictions are key red flags. Inability or refusal to explain why a third party is paying, or last-minute substitution of the payer, heightens suspicion. These indicators require the agent to investigate and, where suspicion is not dispelled, file a suspicious transaction report.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires DNFBPs to identify beneficial owners and report suspicious third-party funding arrangements."},{"ref":"FATF Guidance on ML/TF Risks in the Real Estate Sector (2022)","note":"Flags unexplained third-party payments as a core real estate laundering indicator."}]},{"q":"How does rapid DLD re-registration within 6 months indicate ML layering?","a":"Quick resale and Dubai Land Department re-registration of a property within a short period, especially at a markedly different price and with no renovation or genuine economic purpose, is a recognised layering technique used to inject and distance illicit funds through successive title transfers. Sequential transfers between connected parties or shell companies further obscure the true owner and the source of funds. Such patterns warrant enhanced due diligence and a suspicious transaction report where they cannot be commercially justified.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Guidance on ML/TF Risks in the Real Estate Sector (2022)","note":"Identifies rapid successive resales without economic rationale as a real estate layering red flag."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Obliges DNFBPs to scrutinise and report transactions lacking apparent lawful purpose."}]},{"q":"What DLD and RERA records must be preserved for AML investigations in UAE?","a":"Real estate professionals must retain title deeds, sale and purchase agreements, DLD and RERA registration and transfer records, escrow and trust account statements, payment and source-of-funds evidence, and CDD and beneficial ownership records. Under UAE AML law these must be kept for at least five years after the transaction or end of the business relationship and made available to the FIU and supervisors on request. Records must be sufficient to reconstruct the transaction.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires retention of transaction and CDD records for at least five years and availability to authorities."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 11","note":"Requires record-keeping of transactions and CDD information for at least five years."}]},{"q":"What does the FATF 2022 Guidance on ML through Real Estate require from DNFBPs?","a":"The 2022 risk-based guidance requires real estate professionals to conduct a sector and customer risk assessment, perform CDD including beneficial ownership identification and source of funds checks, apply enhanced measures to higher-risk clients such as PEPs and complex ownership structures, and monitor for and report suspicious activity. It also stresses training, recognising sector-specific red flags such as undervaluation, rapid resale, and third-party or cash settlement. The approach must be proportionate to assessed risk.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Guidance on a Risk-Based Approach for the Real Estate Sector (2022)","note":"Requires risk assessment, CDD, beneficial ownership checks, monitoring, and STR reporting by real estate professionals."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 22 and 23","note":"Extend CDD, record-keeping, and reporting obligations to DNFBPs including real estate agents."}]}]},{"label":"Source of Wealth & Illicit Enrichment","questions":[{"q":"What is UNCAC Article 20 illicit enrichment and how does it apply to UAE public officials?","a":"UNCAC Article 20 invites states parties to consider criminalising illicit enrichment, defined as a significant increase in a public official's assets that he or she cannot reasonably explain in relation to lawful income. It places an evidential burden on the official to justify wealth disproportionate to known earnings. The UAE is a party to UNCAC, and although illicit enrichment is framed as a non-mandatory offence, UAE financial institutions must still treat unexplained disproportionate wealth of a public official as a strong corruption and laundering red flag requiring enhanced due diligence.","refs":[{"ref":"UNCAC Article 20","note":"Invites criminalisation of unexplained significant increases in a public official's assets beyond lawful income."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence and source of wealth measures for politically exposed persons."}]},{"q":"What documentation must a UAE FI obtain to verify PEP source of wealth under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"For a PEP the institution must take reasonable measures to establish source of wealth and source of funds with supporting documentation such as employment and salary records, business ownership and audited financials, dividend and sale-of-asset evidence, inheritance or gift documentation, tax filings, and bank statements. Senior management approval is required to open or continue the relationship, and the wealth profile must be corroborated against reliable independent sources. The basis for accepting the explanation must be documented and kept under ongoing review.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires enhanced measures for PEPs including establishing source of wealth and funds and senior management approval."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires source of wealth and source of funds establishment and senior management sign-off for PEP relationships."}]},{"q":"What is the difference between source of wealth and source of funds in UAE EDD?","a":"Source of wealth describes the origin of a customer's total net worth and how it was accumulated over time, for example a business sale, inheritance, or career earnings. Source of funds describes the specific origin of the money used in a particular transaction or relationship, for example a named bank account or property sale proceeds. Enhanced due diligence on higher-risk customers and PEPs requires establishing both, because legitimate overall wealth does not by itself explain the provenance of a specific transaction.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires establishing both source of wealth and source of funds in enhanced due diligence."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10 and Recommendation 12","note":"Distinguish source of funds for transactions from source of wealth for the customer's overall assets."}]},{"q":"What unexplained wealth gap percentage triggers an STR filing obligation for a UAE MLRO?","a":"UAE AML law sets no fixed numeric percentage; the STR obligation is triggered by reasonable grounds to suspect that funds are proceeds of crime, not by a mechanical threshold. Any material and unexplained gap between a customer's documented lawful income or wealth and their actual financial activity that cannot be resolved through enquiry should be treated as suspicious and reported. The test is qualitative and risk-based, requiring filing whenever suspicion arises regardless of the size of the discrepancy.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires STR filing on reasonable grounds of suspicion, with no minimum threshold."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of any funds suspected to be proceeds of crime regardless of amount."}]},{"q":"How should a UAE FI handle an inheritance claim that cannot be independently verified?","a":"The institution should seek corroborating evidence such as a will, succession or probate certificate, court order, estate distribution documents, or bank records evidencing the transfer. Where the claim cannot be independently verified and the funds remain inconsistent with the known profile, the institution should treat the unexplained source as a red flag, apply enhanced due diligence, and decline or exit the relationship rather than rely on an unverified assertion. If suspicion of illicit origin persists, an STR must be filed.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires verified source of funds and STR filing where suspicion of criminal proceeds cannot be dispelled."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires reliable, independent verification of CDD information including source of funds."}]},{"q":"What lifestyle indicators are red flags for illicit enrichment in UAE compliance screening?","a":"Red flags include spending, luxury assets, property, or transaction volumes plainly inconsistent with the customer's declared income or occupation, sudden unexplained increases in wealth, ownership through nominees or shell companies, and reluctance to explain how assets were acquired. For public officials, acquisition of high-value real estate or vehicles disproportionate to salary is particularly significant. Such indicators should prompt enhanced due diligence, source of wealth enquiry, and an STR if the disparity cannot be lawfully explained.","refs":[{"ref":"UNCAC Article 20","note":"Frames a significant unexplained increase in assets beyond lawful income as illicit enrichment."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires enhanced scrutiny and source of wealth checks where a PEP's wealth is disproportionate."}]}]},{"label":"Insider Threat & Employee Conduct","questions":[{"q":"What constitutes tipping off under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.20 and what are the criminal penalties?","a":"Tipping off is the unlawful disclosure, directly or indirectly, to a customer or any other person that a suspicious transaction report has been or will be filed, or that an AML investigation is underway, in a way that could prejudice that report or investigation. UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 prohibits such disclosure and exposes the offender to imprisonment and substantial fines as set out in the law's penal provisions. The prohibition binds the MLRO, staff, and management alike.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art. 20","note":"Prohibits tipping off and disclosure that an STR has been filed or an investigation is ongoing, with criminal penalties."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 21","note":"Requires prohibition of tipping off and protection from disclosure for reporting institutions and staff."}]},{"q":"What controls must a UAE FI have to detect insider facilitation of financial crime per FATF R.18?","a":"FATF Recommendation 18 requires institutions to implement internal controls including employee screening and fit-and-proper checks at hiring and on an ongoing basis, segregation of duties, an independent audit function, and ongoing training. Detection of insider facilitation also relies on transaction and access monitoring, dual authorisation for sensitive actions, conflict-of-interest declarations, and confidential whistleblowing channels. The MLRO and senior management must ensure these controls are tested and escalated where anomalies appear.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires internal AML controls, employee screening, independent audit, and ongoing training."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires institutions to maintain internal policies, controls, and an independent audit function."}]},{"q":"What lifestyle checks can a UAE FI conduct on employees in sensitive AML roles?","a":"Subject to UAE labour and data-protection rules and employee consent, institutions may apply enhanced pre-employment and periodic screening for staff in sensitive roles, including criminal record and sanctions checks, financial integrity or credit checks where lawful, conflict-of-interest and outside-business declarations, and review for unexplained changes in lifestyle relative to remuneration. Checks must be proportionate, role-based, documented, and conducted under a clear policy. The aim is to assess continued fitness and integrity, not unlawful surveillance.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires screening procedures to ensure high standards when hiring and retaining employees."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires fit-and-proper standards for AML compliance staff within internal controls."}]},{"q":"When must a UAE MLRO notify regulators of a confirmed insider tipping-off incident?","a":"A confirmed insider tipping-off incident is itself a criminal offence and a serious control breach, so the MLRO should report it to the FIU and to the relevant supervisor such as the CBUAE without undue delay, alongside any related suspicious activity. Many UAE supervisory frameworks require prompt notification of significant compliance breaches and fraud or misconduct affecting the AML programme. The institution should also preserve evidence and consider a referral to law enforcement.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Criminalises tipping off and requires reporting of suspicious activity and serious compliance breaches to authorities."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 21","note":"Requires legal protection and enforcement around the prohibition on tipping off."}]},{"q":"What HR disciplinary steps must align with UAE Labour Law before an employee is suspended for AML breaches?","a":"Under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 33 of 2021 on the Regulation of Labour Relations, disciplinary action must follow a fair process: notifying the employee in writing of the allegation, allowing them to respond and present a defence, conducting an investigation, and documenting the findings before imposing a penalty. Precautionary suspension during investigation of serious misconduct is permitted within the law's limits, including time and pay rules. Penalties must be proportionate and recorded, and dismissal for gross misconduct must meet the statutory grounds.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 33 of 2021 (Labour Law)","note":"Sets disciplinary procedure, investigation, employee defence rights, and rules on suspension and dismissal."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Supports disciplinary controls for AML breaches within an institution's internal control framework."}]},{"q":"What system access monitoring is required to detect after-hours MLRO case system access?","a":"Institutions should log and monitor access to AML case and STR systems with immutable audit trails capturing user identity, timestamp, and actions, and configure alerts for unusual patterns such as after-hours access, bulk record viewing, or access to cases outside an analyst's assignment. Role-based access controls, periodic access reviews, and segregation of duties limit exposure, while exception reports are reviewed by an independent function. This both deters insider misuse and protects the confidentiality of suspicious activity reporting.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires internal controls and independent audit, supporting access logging and monitoring of sensitive systems."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires confidentiality of STR information and protection of AML records and systems."}]}]},{"label":"Board AML Governance & Reporting","questions":[{"q":"What quarterly AML MIS must a UAE Board receive under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.5(2)?","a":"The Board should receive regular management information covering the volume and outcomes of STRs filed, alert and case backlogs, high-risk and PEP customer exposures, sanctions screening and any breaches, results of independent audit and testing, training completion, and the status of remediation of regulatory findings. This enables the Board to oversee the effectiveness of the AML/CFT programme and the adequacy of resourcing. UAE AML law and CBUAE expectations require senior management and the Board to exercise active oversight of the compliance function.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art. 5(2)","note":"Requires governance arrangements ensuring senior management and Board oversight of the AML/CFT programme."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires institutions to have programmes with senior-level oversight of AML/CFT controls."}]},{"q":"What personal liability does a UAE Board member face for AML programme failures?","a":"UAE AML law and CBUAE rules allow administrative penalties, fines, and removal or suspension to be imposed on directors and senior managers personally where AML programme failures result from their negligence or failure to oversee compliance. Where there is knowing involvement or wilful blindness, criminal liability can attach to individuals. Board members are expected to ensure adequate systems, resourcing, and a culture of compliance, and cannot delegate away their accountability.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides for administrative and criminal sanctions against responsible managers and directors for AML failures."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 35","note":"Requires proportionate and dissuasive sanctions applicable to directors and senior management."}]},{"q":"What KPIs must a UAE Board monitor for AML effectiveness under CBUAE guidelines?","a":"Relevant effectiveness KPIs include STR quality and timeliness, alert clearance times and case backlogs, CDD and EDD completion and remediation rates, sanctions screening hit-clearance accuracy, training coverage, audit and regulatory finding closure rates, and trends in high-risk customer onboarding and exits. CBUAE guidance emphasises measuring whether controls actually mitigate risk, not just activity counts. The Board should track these against the institution's risk appetite and risk assessment.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance for Licensed Financial Institutions","note":"Expects Boards to monitor the effectiveness and adequacy of the AML/CFT programme through meaningful metrics."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based approach where controls are monitored for effectiveness against assessed risk."}]},{"q":"When must a UAE MLRO escalate a compliance matter directly to the Board?","a":"The MLRO should escalate directly to the Board or its risk or audit committee when a matter is material or systemic, such as a significant control failure, a major regulatory enforcement action or inspection finding, an exposure to sanctions or high-profile PEP or financial-crime risk, or where senior management has failed to act. The MLRO must have direct and independent access to the Board to discharge this duty. Escalation should be documented with the issue, impact, and recommended action.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires an empowered compliance officer with authority and access to report on AML matters."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires a compliance officer at management level with the standing to escalate AML concerns."}]},{"q":"What does a UAE Board AML risk appetite statement need to contain under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"A risk appetite statement should define the types and levels of money laundering, terrorist financing, and sanctions risk the institution is willing to accept, set out prohibited or restricted customers, products, channels, and geographies, and link to risk-based limits, controls, and escalation triggers. It must be approved by the Board, aligned to the enterprise-wide risk assessment, and reviewed periodically. It guides decisions on onboarding higher-risk relationships such as PEPs and high-risk jurisdictions.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires a documented risk-based approach and governance approved at senior level."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires institutions to assess risk and apply Board-endorsed risk-based policies and controls."}]},{"q":"What is the three lines of defence model in UAE AML/CFT governance?","a":"The first line is the business and customer-facing units that own and manage AML risk day to day by applying CDD, monitoring, and reporting. The second line is the independent compliance and MLRO function that sets policy, oversees controls, and monitors risk. The third line is internal audit, which independently tests and assures the effectiveness of the first two lines and reports to the Board. CBUAE expects clear separation and adequate resourcing across all three lines.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance for Licensed Financial Institutions","note":"Endorses the three lines of defence model for AML governance and independent assurance."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires a compliance function and independent audit to test the AML/CFT system."}]}]},{"label":"Enforcement & Regulatory Penalties","questions":[{"q":"What are the maximum administrative penalties under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 for AML violations?","a":"UAE AML law empowers supervisors such as the CBUAE to impose a range of administrative penalties for violations, including warnings, financial fines that can reach into the millions of dirhams per violation, restrictions on business, suspension or removal of managers, and ultimately withdrawal of licence. Fines escalate with the gravity, recurrence, and systemic nature of the breach. The exact monetary ceilings are set by the law and its implementing regulation and cabinet decisions.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Authorises a graduated range of administrative sanctions up to fines, business restrictions, and licence withdrawal."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 35","note":"Requires proportionate, dissuasive, and effective sanctions for AML/CFT breaches."}]},{"q":"When does an AML violation result in criminal prosecution of the MLRO personally under UAE law?","a":"Criminal liability attaches to the MLRO personally where there is intent or knowledge, for example knowingly failing to file an STR on a suspicious transaction, tipping off, deliberately concealing information, or actively assisting laundering. Mere administrative or negligence failures generally lead to administrative sanctions rather than prosecution. Where the conduct is wilful or amounts to complicity, imprisonment and fines under the AML law's penal provisions can apply.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Imposes criminal penalties for knowing failure to report, tipping off, and complicity in laundering."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 35","note":"Requires criminal, civil, or administrative sanctions for natural persons including compliance staff."}]},{"q":"What self-reporting discount is available under CBUAE enforcement policy for voluntary disclosure?","a":"The CBUAE treats prompt voluntary self-disclosure, full cooperation, and timely remediation as mitigating factors that can reduce the level of administrative penalty, though no fixed statutory percentage discount is published. The benefit depends on the timing, completeness, and credibility of the disclosure and the steps taken to fix the underlying failing. Self-reporting does not eliminate liability, particularly where the conduct is serious or harm has occurred.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE Enforcement and Sanctions Framework","note":"Recognises voluntary disclosure, cooperation, and remediation as mitigating factors in setting penalties."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 35","note":"Supports proportionate sanctions where cooperation and remediation reduce severity."}]},{"q":"What precedent CBUAE enforcement actions have been taken for STR filing failures?","a":"The CBUAE has publicly imposed substantial financial penalties on banks, exchange houses, and other licensed institutions for AML failings that include inadequate transaction monitoring and failures to identify and report suspicious activity. Sanctions in recent years have run into millions of dirhams per institution and have been accompanied by remediation requirements. Specific case names and amounts are published in CBUAE enforcement notices, and an MLRO should consult the latest CBUAE announcements for current precedents.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE Enforcement Notices and Public Statements","note":"Record financial penalties against licensed institutions for AML and STR reporting deficiencies."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 35","note":"Requires dissuasive sanctions for failures including inadequate suspicious transaction reporting."}]},{"q":"What mitigating factors reduce AML penalties in UAE regulatory proceedings?","a":"Mitigating factors include prompt self-reporting, full cooperation with the regulator, swift and effective remediation, the absence of actual harm or laundering loss, a previously clean compliance record, and the involvement of only isolated rather than systemic failings. Strong governance and prompt management action also weigh in favour. Aggravating factors, such as recurrence, concealment, or senior management involvement, work in the opposite direction.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE Enforcement and Sanctions Framework","note":"Lists cooperation, remediation, and absence of harm among factors that mitigate penalties."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 35","note":"Requires sanctions to be proportionate, allowing mitigation based on conduct and cooperation."}]},{"q":"What is the MLRO's personal criminal exposure for knowingly failing to file an STR in UAE?","a":"Knowingly failing to report a suspicious transaction, or deliberately omitting or concealing information from the FIU, is a criminal offence under UAE AML law and exposes the MLRO personally to imprisonment and fines, in addition to any administrative sanction or disqualification. Good-faith reporting is protected, but wilful non-reporting or complicity removes that protection. The exposure reflects the law's treatment of the reporting duty as a core obligation.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Criminalises knowing failure to report and concealment of information from the FIU, with imprisonment and fines."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20 and 21","note":"Require mandatory reporting of suspicions and protection only for good-faith reporting."}]}]},{"label":"Inter-Agency Coordination & Referrals","questions":[{"q":"What are the legal pathways for a UAE FI to refer a case to law enforcement under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"The primary statutory pathway is filing a suspicious transaction report with the UAE FIU through the goAML system; the FIU then analyses and disseminates intelligence to law enforcement and the Public Prosecution. Institutions do not generally report directly to police but route financial-crime suspicions through the FIU, while responding to lawful production orders and requests from competent authorities. Internal escalation, evidence preservation, and confidentiality must be maintained throughout.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Channels suspicious transaction reporting through the FIU, which disseminates to law enforcement."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 29","note":"Requires a central FIU to receive, analyse, and disseminate suspicious transaction reports."}]},{"q":"What information must a UAE MLRO provide to the UAE FIU in an inter-agency referral?","a":"An STR or referral must contain full identification of the customer and any beneficial owners, account and transaction details including dates, amounts, and counterparties, a clear narrative of the grounds for suspicion, supporting documentation, and the reporting institution's contact details. The report should be complete, accurate, and timely so the FIU can analyse and act on it. Institutions must also respond to FIU follow-up requests for additional information.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires complete and accurate STRs with customer, transaction, and suspicion details filed to the FIU."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt and complete reporting of suspicions to the FIU."}]},{"q":"When must a UAE MLRO refer a matter to the UAE Attorney General vs filing an STR?","a":"The MLRO's routine statutory duty is to file an STR with the FIU, which is responsible for onward dissemination to the Public Prosecution and law enforcement; the MLRO does not normally refer directly to the Attorney General. Direct contact with prosecutorial authorities arises mainly when responding to a lawful production order, summons, or court request, or when the FIU or prosecution directs cooperation on a live matter. The FIU remains the central conduit for financial intelligence.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Designates the FIU as recipient of STRs and conduit to the Public Prosecution and law enforcement."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 29","note":"Establishes the FIU as the central agency for receiving and disseminating financial intelligence."}]},{"q":"What Egmont Group channels are available for UAE cross-border intelligence sharing?","a":"The UAE FIU is a member of the Egmont Group and uses the secure Egmont Secure Web to exchange financial intelligence spontaneously and on request with foreign FIUs. This channel supports cross-border tracing of funds and beneficial owners subject to the principles of reciprocity, confidentiality, and use limited to the agreed purpose. It complements formal mutual legal assistance, which is needed where evidence is to be used in court.","refs":[{"ref":"Egmont Group Principles for Information Exchange","note":"Provide the framework and Egmont Secure Web for FIU-to-FIU intelligence sharing."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 40","note":"Requires international cooperation and exchange of information between FIUs and competent authorities."}]},{"q":"What tipping-off restrictions apply when coordinating with UAE law enforcement on a live case?","a":"Even while cooperating with authorities, staff must not disclose to the customer or any unauthorised third party that an STR has been filed or that an investigation is underway, as this constitutes the criminal offence of tipping off. Information sharing must be confined to the FIU, supervisors, and lawful requests from competent authorities, with access restricted internally on a need-to-know basis. The confidentiality of the report and the investigation must be preserved.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025, Art. 20","note":"Prohibits disclosure that an STR was filed or an investigation is ongoing during cooperation."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 21","note":"Requires prohibition of tipping off while protecting good-faith reporting."}]},{"q":"How does the UAE NAMLCFTC coordinate between CBUAE, MOF, and law enforcement?","a":"The National Anti-Money Laundering and Combating the Financing of Terrorism and Financing of Illegal Organisations Committee, NAMLCFTC, is the UAE's national coordinating body. It brings together the CBUAE, the Ministry of Finance, the FIU, supervisors, and law enforcement to set national AML/CFT policy, oversee the national risk assessment, ensure alignment with FATF standards, and coordinate inter-agency cooperation and information exchange. It drives the UAE's national strategy and reporting.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Establishes the national committee to coordinate AML/CFT policy among UAE authorities."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendations 1 and 2","note":"Require national AML/CFT policies and domestic inter-agency coordination mechanisms."}]}]},{"label":"AML Policy & Programme Governance","questions":[{"q":"What elements must a UAE AML/CFT policy contain to comply with Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 and CBUAE guidelines?","a":"A compliant policy must cover an enterprise-wide risk assessment and risk-based approach, customer due diligence and enhanced due diligence including beneficial ownership and PEPs, ongoing transaction monitoring, sanctions and targeted financial sanctions screening, suspicious transaction reporting through the FIU, record-keeping for at least five years, appointment and authority of an MLRO, employee screening and training, and independent audit. It must be approved by senior management and kept current with the law. The policy should reflect the institution's specific products, customers, and geographies.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires documented risk-based AML/CFT policies covering CDD, reporting, record-keeping, and governance."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires institutions to develop AML/CFT programmes with policies, controls, audit, and training."}]},{"q":"How often must a UAE AML policy be formally reviewed and board-approved?","a":"The AML/CFT policy should be reviewed and approved at least annually, and additionally whenever there is a material change such as new products, new regulation, or revised risk assessment findings. CBUAE expectations and good practice require senior management or Board approval of the policy and demonstrable evidence of periodic review. The review should test whether controls remain effective against current risks.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance for Licensed Financial Institutions","note":"Expects periodic, at least annual, review and senior approval of AML policies."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires keeping the risk-based approach and policies current as risks evolve."}]},{"q":"What policy changes are mandatory following UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 coming into force?","a":"Institutions must update policies to reflect the new law's definitions, obligations, and penalty regime, refresh references and procedures aligned to its implementing regulations, and confirm that CDD, reporting, sanctions, record-keeping, and governance provisions match the current requirements. Staff training and the compliance manual should be revised, and any transitional or new supervisory expectations incorporated. A gap analysis against the new law should be documented and remediation tracked.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires institutions to align internal policies and procedures with its current obligations."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires AML programmes to remain consistent with applicable legal requirements."}]},{"q":"What does FATF R.18 require regarding documented AML policies and procedures?","a":"FATF Recommendation 18 requires financial institutions to implement programmes against money laundering and terrorist financing, including documented internal policies, procedures, and controls, the appointment of a compliance officer at management level, employee screening, ongoing training, and an independent audit function to test the system. Group-wide programmes must apply to branches and majority-owned subsidiaries, including information sharing within the group. The measures must be proportionate to the institution's risks.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires documented internal AML/CFT policies, a compliance officer, screening, training, and independent audit."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Implements equivalent internal control and compliance officer requirements in UAE law."}]},{"q":"How should a UAE AML policy address the risk-based approach to CDD and monitoring?","a":"The policy should require an institution-wide risk assessment, customer risk rating, and the application of simplified, standard, or enhanced due diligence proportionate to assessed risk, with EDD mandatory for PEPs, high-risk jurisdictions, and complex structures. Transaction monitoring intensity, review frequency, and senior approval should scale with risk, and higher-risk relationships should be reviewed more often. The methodology and decisions must be documented and periodically validated.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 and Recommendation 10","note":"Require a risk-based approach to CDD with EDD for higher-risk and simplified measures for lower-risk situations."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires a risk-based application of CDD and ongoing monitoring proportionate to risk."}]},{"q":"What policy documentation must be available during a CBUAE AML/CFT inspection?","a":"Institutions should make available the AML/CFT policies and procedures, the enterprise-wide risk assessment, the MLRO appointment and reporting lines, CDD and EDD records, transaction monitoring and sanctions screening evidence, STR registers and FIU filings, training records, independent audit reports, and minutes evidencing Board and senior management oversight. Records must be complete, current, and retained for at least five years. The documentation should demonstrate both design and operating effectiveness of the programme.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires maintenance and availability of AML records and documentation to supervisors on request."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 11 and Recommendation 27","note":"Require five-year record-keeping and supervisory power to inspect and obtain AML records."}]}]},{"label":"SWIFT, Letters of Credit & Trade Finance AML","questions":[{"q":"What SWIFT MT103 fields must a compliance officer review for TBML red flags?","a":"Review field 50 (ordering customer), field 59 (beneficiary), and fields 52/56/57 (ordering, intermediary and account-with institutions) to detect missing or incomplete originator/beneficiary information, opaque intermediaries, and high-risk jurisdictions. Field 70 (remittance information) and field 72 (sender-to-receiver information) should be checked for vague trade descriptions, mismatched goods, or pass-through structuring. Field 32A (value date, currency and amount) is compared against the underlying trade documents to spot over- or under-invoicing and amounts inconsistent with the stated commercial purpose.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16 (wire transfers)","note":"Requires complete and accurate originator and beneficiary information to accompany wire transfers and be screened."},{"ref":"FATF Trade-Based Money Laundering: Trends and Developments (2020)","note":"Identifies invoice/value manipulation and intermediary layering as core TBML indicators."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance for Licensed Financial Institutions (correspondent and wire transfers)","note":"Sets expectations for monitoring payment message data for missing or anomalous originator information."}]},{"q":"What LC documentary credit conditions indicate over-invoicing ML risk?","a":"Watch for unit prices or total values materially above prevailing market rates for the described goods, vague or generic goods descriptions, and amendments that increase value without commercial justification. Other indicators include payment terms inconsistent with the trade relationship, beneficiaries or applicants in high-risk or unrelated jurisdictions, and documents (invoice, bill of lading, packing list) that do not reconcile on quantity, weight, or value. Repeated transactions with the same counterparties at inflated prices suggest value transfer rather than genuine trade.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Trade-Based Money Laundering: Trends and Developments (2020)","note":"Lists over-invoicing as a primary TBML technique to move value across borders."},{"ref":"ICC UCP 600 (documentary credit examination)","note":"Requires examination of documents for consistency, supporting reconciliation against stated values."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance on Trade Finance","note":"Expects banks to assess pricing reasonableness and document consistency for ML risk."}]},{"q":"What does FATF TBML guidance require for correspondent bank screening of trade finance?","a":"Correspondent banks must apply risk-based due diligence on respondent institutions, understand the nature of their business and AML controls, and screen underlying trade parties and payment data where visible. They should monitor for nested/downstream relationships, shell respondents, and trade flows inconsistent with the respondent's stated customer base. Enhanced measures and information requests are expected where trade finance involves high-risk jurisdictions or goods.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13 (correspondent banking)","note":"Requires gathering information on respondents, assessing controls, and obtaining senior management approval before establishing relationships."},{"ref":"FATF Trade-Based Money Laundering: Trends and Developments (2020)","note":"Highlights correspondent and nested relationships as a channel for disguising TBML flows."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16 (wire transfers)","note":"Requires intermediary institutions to retain and screen originator/beneficiary data in payment chains."}]},{"q":"When must a UAE bank reject an LC application on AML grounds?","a":"A UAE bank must decline or refuse to proceed where it cannot verify the customer or beneficial owner, where a party is subject to UN or UAE targeted financial sanctions, or where it forms a suspicion of money laundering or terrorist financing that CDD cannot resolve. It must also reject where the trade lacks economic or lawful purpose, documents are inconsistent or fraudulent, or proceeding would breach UAE export-control or sanctions obligations. In such cases the bank must not execute the transaction and must file an STR with the FIU.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.7","note":"Requires refusal to establish or continue a relationship where CDD cannot be completed, and filing of an STR."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 20 of 2018 (as amended) reporting obligation","note":"Obliges FIs to report suspicious transactions and refrain from completing them where required."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10 (customer due diligence)","note":"Prohibits proceeding where CDD cannot be performed and requires consideration of an STR."}]},{"q":"What dual-use goods require enhanced scrutiny in trade finance under UAE export controls?","a":"Goods on the UAE controlled-goods/dual-use lists that have both civilian and potential military, nuclear, chemical, biological, or missile applications require enhanced scrutiny, including electronics, precision machinery, certain chemicals, navigation and telecom equipment, and items with proliferation sensitivity. Banks should verify end-use and end-user, confirm the necessary export permits or licences, and screen for diversion routes through transshipment hubs. Transactions touching sanctioned destinations or proliferation-financing red flags warrant enhanced due diligence and possible reporting.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 13 of 2007 on Commodities Subject to Import and Export Control (and Executive Regulations)","note":"Establishes UAE dual-use control lists and licensing for controlled commodities."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7 (proliferation financing) and Recommendation 2","note":"Requires implementation of PF-related targeted financial sanctions and inter-agency coordination."},{"ref":"FATF Guidance on Counter Proliferation Financing","note":"Identifies dual-use goods diversion as a key proliferation-financing red flag for trade finance."}]},{"q":"How does phantom shipment TBML typology manifest in SWIFT message fields?","a":"Phantom (fictitious) shipments show payment messages settling against goods that were never shipped, so MT103/MT700 references point to bills of lading or invoices that cannot be reconciled with carrier or customs data. Indicators in the fields include field 70/72 narratives citing vessels, ports, or goods that do not match shipping records, repeated round-sum amounts in field 32A, and beneficiary/ordering parties (fields 50/59) that are related or shell entities. Settlement velocity inconsistent with normal trade cycles and absence of supporting logistics documentation reinforce the typology.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Trade-Based Money Laundering: Trends and Developments (2020)","note":"Describes fictitious/phantom shipments where no goods move but value is transferred via trade documents."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16 (wire transfers)","note":"Requires payment message data to be complete and verifiable, enabling detection of inconsistent references."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance on Trade Finance","note":"Expects reconciliation of payment data with shipping and customs documentation."}]}]},{"label":"Regulatory Calendar & Compliance Deadlines","questions":[{"q":"What is the STR filing deadline under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 and when does the clock start?","a":"A suspicious transaction report must be filed with the UAE Financial Intelligence Unit without delay (immediately) once a suspicion of money laundering, terrorist financing, or related predicate offence is formed. The clock starts at the point reasonable grounds for suspicion arise, not when the analysis is fully complete, and the reporting entity must not tip off the customer. Filing is made electronically through the goAML platform.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT framework)","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious transactions to the FIU without delay and prohibits tipping off."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.17","note":"Sets the obligation to notify the FIU of suspicious transactions promptly via goAML."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20 (reporting of suspicious transactions)","note":"Requires prompt reporting to the FIU when ML/TF suspicion arises."}]},{"q":"What is the CTR filing deadline for cash transactions above AED 55,000?","a":"The AED 55,000 threshold is the cash reporting trigger applicable to Designated Non-Financial Businesses and Professions (notably dealers in precious metals and stones), who must file a Dealer Cash Transaction Report through goAML for cash transactions at or above that amount. The report should be submitted promptly, generally without undue delay after the qualifying transaction. Financial institutions report large or unusual cash activity through their transaction-monitoring and reporting obligations rather than a separate fixed CTR threshold.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (DNFBP obligations)","note":"Imposes reporting for DPMS cash transactions at or above AED 55,000 via goAML."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22 and 23 (DNFBPs)","note":"Extends CDD and reporting obligations to dealers in precious metals and stones above designated cash thresholds."},{"ref":"Ministry of Economy / goAML DCTR guidance","note":"Specifies submission of the Dealer Cash Transaction Report for qualifying cash dealings."}]},{"q":"When must a UAE FI file its annual AML self-assessment with CBUAE?","a":"UAE financial institutions complete the CBUAE Annual AML/CFT Risk Assessment (self-assessment) survey on the dates set by the CBUAE each cycle, typically a defined window communicated by notice through the supervisory portal. The submission captures the institution's inherent risk, controls, and residual risk profile for the period. Institutions should monitor CBUAE notices for the exact opening and closing dates as they are set annually rather than by a fixed statutory calendar date.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT supervisory reporting requirements","note":"Requires periodic completion of the AML/CFT risk assessment survey within CBUAE-defined deadlines."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.4 and Art.20","note":"Requires risk assessment and supervisory cooperation/reporting by FIs."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 (risk-based approach)","note":"Requires institutions to assess and document ML/TF risks on an ongoing basis."}]},{"q":"What is the goAML registration renewal deadline for UAE reporting entities?","a":"goAML registration is a one-time enrolment that must be maintained and kept current rather than renewed on a fixed annual date; entities must register before commencing reporting and update their profile promptly whenever details such as the compliance officer, legal name, or contact information change. Failure to register or to keep the registration active exposes the entity to supervisory penalties. Entities should confirm any specific re-validation notices issued by their supervisor or the FIU.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE FIU goAML registration requirements","note":"Requires reporting entities to register on goAML and maintain accurate, up-to-date registration details."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.17","note":"Requires FIs and DNFBPs to be able to report to the FIU, which is enabled by goAML registration."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 29 (financial intelligence units)","note":"Supports establishment of an FIU and effective reporting channels for entities."}]},{"q":"What quarterly reporting obligations do UAE FIs have under CBUAE AML guidelines?","a":"CBUAE may require periodic, including quarterly, returns covering matters such as STR/SAR volumes, sanctions screening hits, high-risk customer counts, and transaction-monitoring statistics through its supervisory reporting framework. These returns supplement the annual risk-assessment survey and event-driven reporting and must be accurate and submitted within the deadlines set in each return's instructions. Institutions should follow the specific schedules and templates issued by CBUAE for their sector.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT supervisory reporting and returns framework","note":"Authorises CBUAE to require periodic returns and statistical reporting from licensed FIs."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.20","note":"Requires FIs to provide information to supervisory authorities on request."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 26 (supervision)","note":"Requires risk-based supervision supported by data collection from regulated entities."}]},{"q":"What is the EWRA annual review deadline and board approval requirement?","a":"The enterprise-wide risk assessment must be reviewed and updated at least annually and additionally whenever a material change occurs, with the senior management and board taking ownership of and approving the assessment and resulting risk-based controls. There is no single fixed statutory calendar date; the institution sets a review cycle that ensures at least yearly refresh and documents board sign-off. The dated, board-approved EWRA should be available for supervisory inspection.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.4","note":"Requires FIs to identify, assess and keep the ML/TF risk assessment up to date."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 (risk-based approach)","note":"Requires risk assessments to be documented, kept current, and approved by senior management."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance (governance)","note":"Expects board and senior management ownership and approval of the EWRA and risk appetite."}]}]},{"label":"Enterprise-Wide Risk Assessment (EWRA)","questions":[{"q":"What risk categories must a UAE FI's EWRA cover under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"The EWRA must assess risk across the core dimensions of customers (including PEPs, legal-person structures, and beneficial ownership), products and services, delivery and distribution channels (including non-face-to-face and agents), and geographic exposure (customer, transaction, and counterparty jurisdictions). It should also factor in proliferation-financing and sanctions risk and any new technologies. Each category is assessed for inherent risk, control effectiveness, and residual risk.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (AML/CFT framework, risk assessment)","note":"Requires identification and assessment of ML/TF risks across business lines and exposures."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.4","note":"Lists customer, product, channel, and geographic risk factors for the institutional assessment."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 (risk-based approach)","note":"Requires assessment of risk across customers, products, geographies, and delivery channels."}]},{"q":"How should a UAE DPMS entity weight real estate and gold sector risks in its EWRA?","a":"A dealer in precious metals and stones should treat gold as an inherently high-risk product given its high value, liquidity, and use in TBML and integration, and weight cash-intensive and cross-border gold flows accordingly. Where the entity also touches real estate (for example through related parties or settlement), it must reflect the high ML risk of high-value property as flagged in the UAE National Risk Assessment. Weightings should be evidence-based, documented, and drive enhanced controls such as lower cash thresholds, source-of-funds checks, and counterparty screening.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (DNFBP risk and DPMS obligations)","note":"Subjects DPMS dealers to risk assessment and enhanced cash-transaction controls."},{"ref":"UAE National Risk Assessment (gold and real estate sectors)","note":"Identifies gold trading and real estate as high ML/TF risk sectors requiring elevated weighting."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 and Recommendation 22","note":"Requires risk-based weighting and DNFBP coverage of higher-risk products such as precious metals."}]},{"q":"What methodology does FATF recommend for institutional ML/TF risk assessment?","a":"FATF recommends identifying inherent risks across customers, products, channels, and geographies, assessing the effectiveness of mitigating controls, and deriving a residual risk rating that drives the allocation of resources and enhanced measures. The process should be documented, proportionate, informed by national and sectoral risk assessments, kept current, and approved by senior management. Higher residual risk requires enhanced due diligence and stronger controls, while lower risk may permit simplified measures.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 (risk-based approach)","note":"Sets the core requirement to identify, assess, and mitigate ML/TF risk and apply resources accordingly."},{"ref":"FATF Guidance on the Risk-Based Approach","note":"Describes the inherent-risk, control-effectiveness, and residual-risk methodology for institutions."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.4","note":"Aligns national rules with the FATF risk-based assessment methodology."}]},{"q":"When must a UAE FI update its EWRA outside the annual review cycle?","a":"An out-of-cycle update is required whenever a material change alters the institution's risk profile, such as launching a new product or channel, entering a new market or jurisdiction, onboarding a new high-risk customer segment, undergoing a merger or acquisition, or in response to new typologies, sanctions developments, or supervisory findings. The trigger-based refresh ensures the EWRA and controls remain current between annual reviews. Each ad hoc update should be documented and, where material, re-approved by senior management.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.4","note":"Requires the risk assessment to be kept up to date as risks change."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 (risk-based approach)","note":"Requires risk assessments to remain current and responsive to new risks."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance (risk assessment)","note":"Expects event-driven updates to the EWRA on material change."}]},{"q":"What residual risk rating triggers mandatory enhanced controls under CBUAE expectations?","a":"Where residual risk is assessed as high after applying controls, CBUAE expects mandatory enhanced due diligence and stronger mitigating measures, including senior management approval, enhanced ongoing monitoring, and source-of-funds and source-of-wealth verification. High residual ratings for customers such as PEPs, complex structures, or high-risk jurisdictions automatically attract EDD. The institution must document why residual risk remains high and how the enhanced controls bring it within risk appetite.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.4 and Art.15 (EDD)","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence and controls proportionate to higher assessed risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10 and Recommendation 1","note":"Requires EDD for higher-risk situations identified through the risk-based approach."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance (high-risk and EDD)","note":"Sets supervisory expectation that high residual risk triggers enhanced controls."}]},{"q":"How should a UAE FI document the link between EWRA outcomes and its AML programme design?","a":"The institution should maintain a clear, traceable record showing how each identified high or elevated residual risk maps to a specific control, policy, monitoring rule, threshold, or resourcing decision in the AML programme. This is typically captured in a risk register or control matrix cross-referencing the EWRA findings to the policies, CDD/EDD procedures, transaction-monitoring scenarios, and training that mitigate them. The documentation should be board-approved and available to demonstrate to supervisors that the programme is genuinely risk-based.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 (risk-based approach)","note":"Requires policies and controls to be informed by and proportionate to the assessed risks."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.4 and Art.20","note":"Requires documented risk assessment and supporting policies and procedures available to supervisors."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance (programme governance)","note":"Expects demonstrable linkage between EWRA outcomes and programme design."}]}]},{"label":"AML Programme Gap Analysis","questions":[{"q":"What are the mandatory components of a UAE AML/CFT programme under Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.18?","a":"A compliant programme must include internal policies, procedures, and controls proportionate to assessed risk; CDD and ongoing monitoring; appointment of a qualified compliance officer/MLRO with sufficient authority and independence; suspicious-transaction reporting through goAML; record-keeping; staff screening and ongoing AML training; and an independent audit function to test the programme. It must also cover targeted financial sanctions and proliferation-financing controls. Senior management and the board must own and approve the framework.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.18","note":"Sets the mandatory elements of the AML/CFT compliance programme for regulated entities."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.20-21","note":"Detail compliance officer appointment, internal controls, audit, and training requirements."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18 (internal controls)","note":"Requires internal policies, compliance management, screening, training, and independent audit."}]},{"q":"What CBUAE inspection findings most commonly indicate AML programme gaps?","a":"Common findings include weak or stale EWRA, incomplete CDD and beneficial-ownership identification, ineffective or poorly calibrated transaction monitoring with high false positives and missed typologies, delayed or under-reporting of STRs, inadequate sanctions screening, insufficient MLRO authority or resourcing, and gaps in record-keeping and training. CBUAE also frequently cites weak governance and failure to remediate prior findings. These gaps point to a programme that is not effectively risk-based or operationally tested.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT inspection and enforcement findings","note":"Identifies recurring deficiencies in CDD, monitoring, reporting, and governance."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.20-21","note":"Establishes the control, reporting, and governance obligations whose breach constitutes gaps."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18 and Recommendation 20","note":"Set internal-control and reporting standards against which gaps are assessed."}]},{"q":"What does FATF R.18 require for internal AML audit independence and frequency?","a":"FATF Recommendation 18 requires regulated entities to maintain an independent audit function to test the effectiveness of the AML/CFT programme, with independence meaning the audit is performed by persons separate from those who design or operate the controls. The audit must be adequately resourced and have sufficient scope to cover policies, CDD, monitoring, reporting, and training. While FATF does not fix a single interval, the testing must be conducted on a risk-based and regular basis, commonly at least annually for higher-risk institutions.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18 (internal controls and audit)","note":"Requires an independently resourced audit function to test the AML/CFT programme."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.21","note":"Requires an independent audit function to assess the adequacy of AML controls."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance (independent testing)","note":"Expects regular, risk-based independent testing of the programme."}]},{"q":"What gaps in a UAE TM programme are considered systemic failures by CBUAE?","a":"Systemic failures include monitoring scenarios that are not aligned to the EWRA or known typologies, untuned thresholds producing pervasive false positives or missing genuine activity, large backlogs of unreviewed alerts, lack of coverage for product or channel risks, and failure to escalate or report despite clear indicators. Absence of model validation, governance, and audit trails over the TM system is also treated as systemic. Such failures indicate the institution cannot reliably detect and report suspicious activity.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance on Transaction Monitoring","note":"Sets expectations for risk-aligned scenarios, tuning, alert handling, and governance."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.16 (ongoing monitoring)","note":"Requires continuous monitoring of transactions to detect suspicious activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20 (suspicious transaction reporting)","note":"Requires effective detection and prompt reporting, undermined by systemic TM gaps."}]},{"q":"What remediation timeline is considered adequate for a critical AML programme gap?","a":"There is no fixed statutory period; adequacy is judged against the severity and risk of the gap, with critical deficiencies expected to be addressed urgently through immediate interim risk-mitigation and a credible, dated remediation plan agreed with CBUAE. Supervisors typically require prompt containment of the risk while structural fixes follow on a realistic but accelerated schedule, with milestones and evidence of completion. The institution should track progress and report status to CBUAE as directed in any enforcement notice.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT enforcement and remediation framework","note":"Requires risk-prioritised remediation plans with agreed milestones and supervisory reporting."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.20","note":"Requires FIs to maintain and correct controls to remain compliant."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 (risk-based approach)","note":"Requires prioritisation of mitigation based on the severity of identified risks."}]},{"q":"What documentation must a UAE MLRO maintain to evidence programme effectiveness?","a":"The MLRO should maintain the board-approved EWRA and policies, CDD/EDD records and beneficial-ownership files, transaction-monitoring rules and alert/case dispositions, STR records and FIU acknowledgements, sanctions-screening logs, training records and attendance, independent-audit reports, and MLRO reports to senior management and the board. Records must be retained for at least five years and be readily retrievable for supervisors. Together these evidence that the programme is implemented, tested, and risk-based in practice.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.24 (record-keeping)","note":"Requires retention of CDD, transaction, and reporting records for at least five years."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 11 (record-keeping) and Recommendation 18","note":"Require maintenance of records and evidence of internal controls and testing."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.18","note":"Requires compliance management, reporting, and record evidence of the programme."}]}]},{"label":"Trade Invoice & TBML Analysis","questions":[{"q":"What HS code categories carry heightened TBML risk under UAE AML guidelines?","a":"Higher TBML risk attaches to goods that are high-value, easily mispriced, or hard to value, including gold and precious metals and stones, electronics and high-tech components, vehicles and parts, textiles, and certain commodities and scrap. Dual-use and proliferation-sensitive items also warrant elevated scrutiny. Risk is heightened where the HS code on documents does not match the goods described or the customs declaration.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Trade-Based Money Laundering: Trends and Developments (2020)","note":"Identifies high-value and hard-to-value goods as preferred for invoice manipulation."},{"ref":"UAE National Risk Assessment (gold and precious commodities)","note":"Flags precious metals and stones as elevated ML/TF risk goods."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance on Trade Finance","note":"Expects scrutiny of goods classification and pricing against market norms."}]},{"q":"How does over-invoicing by 10% or more trigger UAE TBML reporting obligations?","a":"There is no fixed statutory 10% bright line; rather, a material discrepancy between invoiced value and the fair market or customs-declared value is a red flag that, where it gives rise to a suspicion of money laundering, triggers the duty to file an STR. A 10% or greater variance that cannot be justified commercially should prompt enhanced review of source of funds, counterparties, and documents. Reporting is driven by suspicion, not by the size of the percentage alone.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Trade-Based Money Laundering: Trends and Developments (2020)","note":"Treats value discrepancies versus market price as a core TBML indicator."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.17","note":"Requires STR filing whenever suspicion of ML/TF arises, including from value anomalies."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20 (reporting of suspicious transactions)","note":"Requires reporting on suspicion regardless of a fixed quantitative threshold."}]},{"q":"What does FATF TBML Guidance 2020 require for port-of-entry customs coordination?","a":"The guidance promotes information sharing and cooperation between financial institutions, customs, FIUs, and law-enforcement so that trade, payment, and shipment data can be reconciled to detect mispricing, mis-description, and phantom shipments. It encourages customs data exchange, joint typology work, and feedback loops to improve detection at points of entry and exit. For banks, this means leveraging available customs and shipping references when assessing trade transactions.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Trade-Based Money Laundering: Trends and Developments (2020)","note":"Recommends customs and inter-agency data sharing to detect trade value and quantity anomalies."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 2 (national cooperation and coordination)","note":"Requires coordination among competent authorities including customs."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 40 (international cooperation)","note":"Supports cross-border exchange of trade and financial intelligence."}]},{"q":"When does a discrepancy between invoice value and customs declaration require STR filing?","a":"An STR is required when the discrepancy, after reasonable enquiry, gives rise to a suspicion or reasonable grounds to suspect money laundering, terrorist financing, or a predicate offence and cannot be explained by a legitimate commercial reason. Unexplained material gaps, especially combined with other red flags such as high-risk parties or unusual payment flows, should be reported promptly via goAML. The trigger is suspicion, not the mere existence of a discrepancy.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.17","note":"Requires prompt STR filing once suspicion of ML/TF arises."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20 (suspicious transaction reporting)","note":"Requires reporting when there is suspicion or reasonable grounds to suspect ML/TF."},{"ref":"FATF Trade-Based Money Laundering Guidance (2020)","note":"Treats invoice-versus-customs value mismatches as a reportable red flag."}]},{"q":"What TBML red flags arise when a UAE free zone entity is the importer?","a":"Red flags include free-zone companies with minimal substance acting as conduits, rapid re-export or transshipment inconsistent with stated business, goods or values not matching the entity's licensed activity, and opaque beneficial ownership. Use of multiple free-zone entities sharing addresses or directors, and payments routed through unrelated third parties, also raise concern. The combination of free-zone status, cross-border flows, and weak commercial rationale warrants enhanced due diligence.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Trade-Based Money Laundering: Trends and Developments (2020)","note":"Identifies free-trade zones and transshipment as channels exploited for TBML."},{"ref":"UAE National Risk Assessment (free zones and trade)","note":"Notes free-zone and trade-corridor exposure as ML/TF risk factors."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10 (CDD and beneficial ownership)","note":"Requires verification of identity, ownership, and business purpose for higher-risk customers."}]},{"q":"How do phantom shipments in gold trading create integration-stage ML risk?","a":"Phantom gold shipments allow criminals to justify large inbound or outbound payments against goods that never moved, giving illicit funds the appearance of legitimate trade proceeds and thereby integrating them into the financial system. Gold's high value, liquidity, and difficulty of tracing make fictitious or inflated trades an effective layering and integration vehicle. The resulting funds enter accounts as apparently clean sales revenue, completing the laundering cycle.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Trade-Based Money Laundering: Trends and Developments (2020)","note":"Describes fictitious shipments and gold as tools for layering and integration of illicit value."},{"ref":"UAE National Risk Assessment (gold sector)","note":"Identifies gold trading as a high ML risk for value transfer and integration."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22 (DNFBPs - precious metals dealers)","note":"Extends AML obligations to gold dealers given integration-stage risk."}]}]},{"label":"Entity Network & Beneficial Ownership Mapping","questions":[{"q":"What relationship indicators suggest a nominee director arrangement under UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025?","a":"Indicators include a director who holds positions across many unrelated companies, lacks knowledge of or involvement in the business, takes instructions from an undisclosed party, or is a professional service provider acting for multiple clients at the same address. Frequent director changes, divergence between the registered director and the person controlling bank accounts, and ownership chains designed to obscure the natural-person controller also signal nominee use. The institution must look through to the true beneficial owner exercising control.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (beneficial ownership and control)","note":"Requires identification of the natural persons who ultimately own or control a legal person."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 58 of 2020 (Real Beneficiary Procedures)","note":"Requires disclosure of UBO and treats nominee arrangements as requiring look-through."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24 and 10 (beneficial ownership)","note":"Require identifying beneficial owners and addressing the use of nominees."}]},{"q":"How does a shared registered address across multiple companies indicate a shell network?","a":"A single address used by numerous otherwise unrelated companies, especially a service-provider or virtual office, can indicate shell entities created for layering rather than genuine operations. Combined with common directors or shareholders, minimal substance, and circular or pass-through transactions, it suggests a coordinated network used to obscure ownership and fund flows. Such clustering warrants enhanced due diligence and network mapping to identify the controlling persons.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24 (transparency of legal persons)","note":"Addresses misuse of legal persons and the need to identify beneficial owners behind structures."},{"ref":"FATF Money Laundering Using Trust and Company Service Providers / shell company typologies","note":"Identifies shared addresses and mass incorporation as shell-network indicators."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 58 of 2020 (Real Beneficiary Procedures)","note":"Requires accurate registered details and UBO disclosure to deter shell misuse."}]},{"q":"What is the UAE UBO disclosure threshold and how is it applied to layered holding structures?","a":"Under the UAE beneficial-ownership regime, a beneficial owner is a natural person who ultimately owns or controls 25% or more of the shares or voting rights, directly or indirectly, or who otherwise exercises control by other means. In layered holding structures the 25% test is applied on a look-through, cumulative basis through each tier to the ultimate natural person. Where no person meets the ownership test, the senior managing official is identified as the beneficial owner.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 58 of 2020 (Real Beneficiary Procedures)","note":"Sets the 25% ownership/control threshold and the fallback to senior managing official."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24 and 10 (beneficial ownership)","note":"Require look-through identification of natural persons controlling legal persons."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.9","note":"Requires identification and verification of beneficial owners as part of CDD."}]},{"q":"When do transaction links between entities indicate conduit ML activity per FATF typologies?","a":"Conduit (pass-through) activity is indicated when funds move quickly in and out of an account with little retained balance, transactions lack apparent commercial purpose, and value flows in circular or layered patterns among related or shell entities. Round-sum transfers, mismatches between business profile and turnover, and rapid distribution to multiple jurisdictions reinforce the pattern. Such links suggest the entity is being used to layer and disguise the origin of funds rather than to conduct genuine business.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF typologies on layering and the use of legal persons","note":"Describe pass-through accounts and circular flows as layering indicators."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20 (suspicious transaction reporting)","note":"Requires reporting where transaction patterns indicate suspicion of ML."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.16","note":"Requires ongoing monitoring to detect transactions inconsistent with the customer profile."}]},{"q":"What corporate registry searches must a UAE FI conduct for network mapping under FATF R.24?","a":"The institution should search the relevant corporate and beneficial-ownership registries (including mainland and free-zone registries and the UBO register) to obtain shareholders, directors, registered address, and UBO data, then cross-reference shared parties, addresses, and ownership chains across entities. It should corroborate registry data with independent sources and identify the natural persons exercising ultimate control. R.24 supports access to adequate, accurate, and timely beneficial-ownership information for this purpose.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24 (transparency and beneficial ownership of legal persons)","note":"Requires adequate, accurate, and timely access to beneficial-ownership information."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 58 of 2020 (Real Beneficiary Procedures)","note":"Establishes the UAE UBO register and disclosure obligations supporting registry checks."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.9","note":"Requires verification of legal-person ownership and control during CDD."}]},{"q":"How should a UAE MLRO document a complex ML network for a goAML STR narrative?","a":"The narrative should clearly identify each entity and natural person, their roles and relationships, and the suspicious fund flows in chronological order, stating the grounds for suspicion and the typology observed. It should reference the underlying transactions, accounts, dates, amounts, and supporting documents, and attach or list evidence in the goAML report. The account should be factual, concise, and self-explanatory so the FIU can follow the network and the basis for the report without external context.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE FIU goAML STR reporting guidance","note":"Requires a clear factual narrative, parties, and grounds for suspicion in the report."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.17","note":"Requires submission of complete suspicious-transaction reports to the FIU."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20 (suspicious transaction reporting)","note":"Requires reports that enable the FIU to analyse and act on the suspicion."}]}]},{"label":"Risk Appetite & Governance Frameworks","questions":[{"q":"What must a UAE FI's AML risk appetite statement prohibit as zero-tolerance activities?","a":"The statement must set zero tolerance for dealings that breach UN or UAE targeted financial sanctions, for relationships with sanctioned persons, shell banks, and unlicensed money or value transfer services, and for knowingly facilitating money laundering, terrorist financing, or proliferation financing. It should also prohibit onboarding customers whose identity or beneficial ownership cannot be verified, anonymous or fictitious-name accounts, and business that breaches UAE law. These prohibitions reflect non-negotiable legal limits rather than risk-weighted choices.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 (targeted financial sanctions)","note":"Prohibits dealing with listed persons and requires immediate freezing measures."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13 and 10","note":"Prohibit shell-bank relationships and anonymous/unverified accounts."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 (prohibited conduct)","note":"Criminalises facilitation of ML/TF, framing zero-tolerance limits."}]},{"q":"How should Board risk appetite thresholds translate into operational TM alert parameters?","a":"Board-set appetite (for example limits on high-risk customers, cash intensity, or exposure to certain jurisdictions) should be operationalised into monitoring scenarios, thresholds, and alert rules calibrated to detect activity that approaches or breaches those limits. The translation should be documented so each appetite statement maps to specific TM coverage, with tuning evidence and periodic validation. Breaches or trends near appetite limits should be escalated to the MLRO and reported to the board.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 (risk-based approach)","note":"Requires controls and monitoring proportionate to the institution's risk and appetite."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.16","note":"Requires ongoing transaction monitoring aligned to risk."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance (governance and monitoring)","note":"Expects board risk appetite to drive operational controls and escalation."}]},{"q":"What does UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.5 require of the Board on AML risk appetite?","a":"The board and senior management are required to take ownership of the AML/CFT framework, including approving the risk appetite, policies, and the risk-based programme, and ensuring adequate resources and an independent compliance function. They must oversee implementation, receive regular reporting on AML risk and performance, and hold accountability for effectiveness. This embeds risk appetite as a board-level governance responsibility rather than a purely operational matter.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.5","note":"Establishes board and senior-management responsibility for the AML/CFT framework and risk governance."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.20-21","note":"Require governance, compliance-officer appointment, and senior-management oversight."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18 (internal controls)","note":"Requires senior-management commitment and governance over AML controls."}]},{"q":"How often must a UAE AML risk appetite statement be reviewed and re-approved by the Board?","a":"The risk appetite statement should be reviewed and re-approved by the board at least annually, and additionally whenever there is a material change in the business, risk profile, or regulatory environment. There is no single fixed calendar date, but the review must be regular, documented, and dated to evidence current board ownership. Out-of-cycle revisions are expected following significant events or supervisory findings.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.5","note":"Places ongoing ownership of risk appetite with the board, implying periodic re-approval."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 (risk-based approach)","note":"Requires risk frameworks to be kept current as risks change."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance (governance)","note":"Expects at least annual board review and approval of AML governance documents."}]},{"q":"What risk appetite metrics must be reported to the MLRO and Board regularly?","a":"Reporting should include STR/SAR volumes and trends, sanctions-screening alerts and true matches, counts of high-risk customers and PEPs, EDD and onboarding rejections, transaction-monitoring alert and backlog statistics, breaches of appetite limits, and training and audit-finding status. These key risk and performance indicators let the MLRO and board judge whether actual exposure remains within appetite. Material breaches and emerging risks should be escalated promptly rather than only in periodic packs.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18 (internal controls and reporting)","note":"Requires compliance management information to support governance and oversight."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.21","note":"Requires the compliance officer to report to senior management on AML matters."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance (MI and reporting)","note":"Expects regular risk metrics to be reported to the MLRO and board."}]},{"q":"What is the difference between risk tolerance and risk appetite in UAE AML governance?","a":"Risk appetite is the board-set, forward-looking statement of the amount and type of ML/TF risk the institution is willing to accept in pursuit of its objectives, while risk tolerance is the acceptable variation or operational limit around that appetite before action is triggered. In practice appetite is strategic and qualitative, and tolerance is the quantitative boundary that, when approached or breached, forces escalation or remediation. Both must stay within the non-negotiable legal and zero-tolerance limits.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 (risk-based approach)","note":"Frames acceptance and mitigation of risk that underpin appetite and tolerance."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance (risk governance)","note":"Distinguishes board risk appetite from operational tolerance limits."},{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025 Art.5","note":"Vests risk-appetite ownership in the board within legal limits."}]}]},{"label":"Regulatory Examination Readiness","questions":[{"q":"What are CBUAE examiners' most common AML findings in UAE bank inspections?","a":"Frequent findings include incomplete CDD and beneficial-ownership verification, weak or outdated EWRA, ineffective transaction monitoring and STR under-reporting or delay, gaps in sanctions and PEP screening, insufficient MLRO authority and resourcing, inadequate training, and failure to remediate prior issues. Governance weaknesses and poor record-keeping also recur. These reflect programmes that are documented but not effectively operating on a risk basis.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT inspection and enforcement findings","note":"Catalogues recurring deficiencies across CDD, monitoring, reporting, and governance."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.20-21","note":"Sets the obligations whose breach drives common findings."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18 and 20","note":"Provide the internal-control and reporting benchmarks examiners apply."}]},{"q":"What CDD file evidence must be prepared for a CBUAE AML inspection of a DPMS entity?","a":"A DPMS entity should produce identification and verification documents for customers and their beneficial owners, evidence of purpose and nature of the business relationship, source-of-funds checks for high-value or cash dealings, records of Dealer Cash Transaction Reports for cash at or above AED 55,000, screening results, and risk ratings. Ongoing-monitoring notes and any EDD for high-risk customers should also be available. All records must be complete, dated, and retained for at least five years.","refs":[{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.8-9 and Art.24","note":"Require CDD, beneficial-ownership verification, and five-year record retention."},{"ref":"Ministry of Economy DNFBP / DPMS guidance (AED 55,000 cash threshold)","note":"Requires DCTR filing and supporting records for qualifying cash transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22 (DNFBPs)","note":"Extends CDD and record-keeping obligations to precious-metals dealers."}]},{"q":"How should a UAE MLRO prepare for a FATF mutual evaluation onsite visit?","a":"The MLRO should ensure the institution can demonstrate effectiveness, not just technical compliance, by preparing evidence across the relevant immediate outcomes: a current EWRA, functioning CDD and monitoring, timely STR filing, sanctions implementation, and governance and training records. Staff should be able to articulate how risks are identified and mitigated in practice, with metrics and case examples ready. Coordination with the supervisor and consistency between policy and operational evidence are key.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Methodology for Assessing Technical Compliance and Effectiveness","note":"Assesses immediate outcomes and effectiveness, not only laws on the books."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 (risk-based approach)","note":"Requires demonstrable, risk-based implementation of AML controls."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (supervisory cooperation)","note":"Requires institutions to cooperate with and provide information to authorities."}]},{"q":"What TM effectiveness metrics do CBUAE examiners request in AML inspections?","a":"Examiners typically request alert volumes and trends, alert-to-case and case-to-STR conversion rates, false-positive ratios, alert and case backlogs and ageing, scenario coverage versus the EWRA and typologies, threshold-tuning and model-validation evidence, and timeliness of investigation and reporting. They also look at quality assurance over alert disposition. These metrics show whether the monitoring system actually detects and escalates suspicious activity effectively.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance on Transaction Monitoring","note":"Sets expectations for measuring monitoring coverage, tuning, and effectiveness."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.16","note":"Requires effective ongoing monitoring of transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20 (suspicious transaction reporting)","note":"Requires effective detection and timely reporting that metrics evidence."}]},{"q":"What remediation documentation satisfies a CBUAE enforcement notice for AML deficiencies?","a":"An adequate response includes a root-cause analysis, a dated and prioritised remediation plan with owners and milestones, evidence of interim risk mitigation, and proof of completed actions such as updated policies, retuned monitoring, cleared backlogs, and retrained staff. It should include validation or independent testing confirming the fixes are effective, and progress reports to CBUAE as required by the notice. The documentation must demonstrate the deficiency is resolved, not merely actioned.","refs":[{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT enforcement and remediation framework","note":"Requires root-cause analysis, remediation plans, and evidence of effective completion."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.20","note":"Requires correction and maintenance of compliant controls."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1 (risk-based approach)","note":"Requires risk-prioritised mitigation of identified deficiencies."}]},{"q":"How does the CBUAE risk-based inspection approach determine examination depth and scope?","a":"CBUAE allocates supervisory intensity according to the assessed ML/TF risk of the institution, considering its size, business mix, customer and geographic exposure, prior findings, and inherent-risk profile. Higher-risk institutions face more frequent and deeper onsite and offsite reviews, while lower-risk entities receive proportionately lighter scrutiny. Findings, self-assessment returns, and event-driven intelligence feed continuously into the scoping of subsequent inspections.","refs":[{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 26 (supervision of FIs)","note":"Requires risk-based supervision with intensity proportionate to assessed risk."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT risk-based supervision framework","note":"Scopes inspections by institutional risk, history, and exposure."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 Art.20","note":"Establishes supervisory powers and information access underpinning risk-based reviews."}]}]}];
window.SUPER_TOOLS = [{"id":"escalation","label":"⚡ Escalation Decision Engine","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Determines whether an alert or internal report should be escalated to the MLRO and onward to the FIU, applying deterministic escalation thresholds and decision logic. The MLRO uses it to convert raw alerts into a defensible escalate-or-close decision.","steps":["Pull the triggering alert, customer risk rating, and any prior internal reports for the same subject before scoring escalation.","Apply the escalation matrix: match the alert against suspicion indicators, sanctions hits, and threshold breaches to set a tier.","Confirm whether knowledge or suspicion of ML, TF, or proliferation financing exists, which mandates onward reporting irrespective of amount.","Record the decision rationale, the responsible officer, and the timestamp for the audit trail.","Route escalated items to STR drafting and goAML submission, and document closure reasons for non-escalated items.","Set a review date and flag the subject for enhanced monitoring where escalation was borderline."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Obliges reporting entities to file a suspicious transaction report with the FIU on suspicion of ML, TF, or proliferation financing without delay."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting to the FIU where there are reasonable grounds to suspect proceeds of crime or terrorist financing."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Sets out the implementing procedures for internal escalation, MLRO appointment, and STR obligations."}]}},{"id":"flags","label":"🚩 Red Flag Extractor","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Scans case material and transaction data to extract and categorise specific AML red flag indicators. The MLRO uses it to surface and classify suspicion drivers before deciding on escalation.","steps":["Feed the case file, KYC data, and transaction history into the extractor to identify candidate red flags.","Categorise each flag by typology such as structuring, layering, sanctions evasion, or trade-based laundering.","Rank flags by severity and corroboration, separating confirmed indicators from those needing further inquiry.","Cross-reference flags against the customer risk profile to assess deviation from expected behaviour.","Compile the categorised red flag list into the case record to feed the escalation and STR tools."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires entities to identify and report suspicious indicators and unusual transactions to the FIU."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Underpins detection and reporting of transactions suspected to relate to proceeds of crime or terrorist financing."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Details indicators and procedures supporting suspicion-based reporting obligations."}]}},{"id":"patterns","label":"📊 Case Patterns","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Analyses prior and concurrent cases to detect recurring behavioural and network patterns linking a subject to broader typologies. The MLRO uses it to spot serial or coordinated activity that a single alert would miss.","steps":["Aggregate the subject's historical alerts, STRs, and linked-party cases into a single pattern view.","Identify recurring counterparties, amounts, channels, and timing that indicate a sustained scheme.","Compare the pattern against known ML and TF typologies to label the likely method.","Quantify frequency and escalation over time to evidence persistence rather than a one-off event.","Document recurring-pattern findings for inclusion in the STR narrative and ongoing monitoring."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Mandates ongoing monitoring of the business relationship and scrutiny of transactions for consistency with known activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing due diligence and scrutiny of transactions throughout the relationship."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Supports reporting where transaction patterns give rise to suspicion."}]}},{"id":"brief","label":"📋 Subject Brief","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Assembles a concise, structured profile of an individual or entity subject, consolidating identity, KYC, risk, and case status into one brief. The MLRO uses it as the cover summary for a case or investigation.","steps":["Collate verified identity, nationality, residence, and occupation or business activity for the subject.","Summarise the customer risk rating, product use, and any PEP or sanctions status.","List linked parties, UBOs, and controllers relevant to the subject.","Capture the open alerts, prior STRs, and current case status in a status block.","Output the brief as the standardised cover sheet feeding the decision memo and STR."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires identification and verification of the customer and maintenance of accurate customer records."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Sets the customer due diligence elements that populate the subject profile."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 11","note":"Requires retention of CDD records that underpin the subject brief."}]}},{"id":"risk-profile-summary","label":"🛡 Risk Profile Summary","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Consolidates customer, product, channel, and geography risk factors into a single residual risk rating with rationale. The MLRO uses it to document and justify the assigned risk level of a relationship.","steps":["Gather the inherent risk factors across customer type, product, delivery channel, and jurisdiction.","Apply the risk-weighting model to derive an inherent rating and note the dominant drivers.","Factor in mitigating controls to calculate the residual risk rating.","Flag any factor that triggers mandatory enhanced due diligence such as PEP or high-risk jurisdiction exposure.","Record the rating, rationale, and next review date for the relationship file."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires a risk-based approach and assessment of ML and TF risk for each customer relationship."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires identifying, assessing, and mitigating ML and TF risks on a risk-based basis."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires applying CDD calibrated to assessed risk."}]}},{"id":"pep-network","label":"🕸 PEP Network","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Maps a subject's connections to politically exposed persons, including family members and close associates and the entities they control. The MLRO uses it to detect indirect PEP exposure that direct screening can miss.","steps":["Identify whether the subject is a domestic, foreign, or international organisation PEP.","Expand the network to family members and known close associates of any PEP found.","Map entities owned or controlled by PEPs or their associates that connect to the subject.","Assess source of wealth and source of funds for relationships with PEP exposure.","Trigger senior management approval and enhanced due diligence where PEP linkage is confirmed.","Record the network map and EDD outcome in the relationship file."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires enhanced measures, senior approval, and source of wealth checks for PEP relationships."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires PEP identification and enhanced due diligence covering family members and close associates."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Sets the procedures for PEP determination and applicable enhanced measures."}]}},{"id":"sanctions-nexus","label":"🔒 Sanctions Nexus","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Maps ownership and control links between a subject and designated persons or entities to detect indirect sanctions exposure. The MLRO uses it to test the fifty percent ownership and control rules beyond a direct name match.","steps":["Run the subject and all linked parties against the UAE Local Terrorist List and UN Consolidated List.","Trace ownership chains to test aggregate ownership at or above the fifty percent control threshold by designated parties.","Assess control indicators beyond shareholding such as board control, authority, or beneficial entitlement.","Where a nexus is found, escalate to immediately freeze without delay and notify the competent authority.","Document the ownership map, screening lists used, and the freeze or no-match decision with timestamps."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Prohibits dealing with designated persons and requires implementation of targeted financial sanctions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Requires implementation of targeted financial sanctions related to terrorism and terrorist financing."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020","note":"Sets the UAE framework for implementing UN Security Council resolutions and targeted financial sanctions including freezing without delay."}]}},{"id":"typology-match","label":"🎯 Typology Match","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Matches case facts against catalogued ML, TF, and proliferation financing typologies to name the suspected laundering method. The MLRO uses it to classify the scheme and align controls and reporting to the identified typology.","steps":["Summarise the observed behaviour, channels, and counterparties from the case.","Match the fact pattern against the typology library covering structuring, layering, trade-based laundering, and others.","Select the best-fit typology and note distinguishing indicators that support the match.","Identify residual indicators that do not fit, prompting consideration of alternative typologies.","Feed the named typology into the red flag, STR, and monitoring outputs."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Defines the ML, TF, and proliferation offences that typologies are mapped against."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires understanding ML and TF methods and typologies to assess and mitigate risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Supports reporting once a recognised typology gives rise to suspicion."}]}},{"id":"txn-narrative","label":"📝 Transaction Analyzer","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Examines individual and aggregated transactions to identify unusual flows and build a plain-language narrative of the money movement. The MLRO uses it to explain what happened with the funds for a case or STR.","steps":["Reconstruct the transaction sequence including dates, amounts, counterparties, and channels.","Compare the activity against the customer's expected profile and stated purpose of account.","Identify anomalies such as rapid pass-through, round amounts, or unexplained third parties.","Trace source and destination of funds across linked accounts where data permits.","Produce a chronological narrative of the flows suitable for the STR and decision memo."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires scrutiny of transactions to ensure consistency with the customer's profile and source of funds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing scrutiny of transactions throughout the business relationship."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Supports reporting of transactions that appear unusual or suspicious."}]}},{"id":"edd-questionnaire","label":"📑 EDD Generator","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Generates a tailored enhanced due diligence questionnaire and evidence checklist for high-risk relationships. The MLRO uses it to drive structured collection of source of wealth, source of funds, and corroborating documents.","steps":["Identify the EDD trigger such as PEP status, high-risk jurisdiction, or high residual risk rating.","Generate targeted questions on source of wealth, source of funds, and purpose of the relationship.","Specify the supporting documents required to corroborate each declared answer.","Require senior management approval to establish or continue the relationship.","Set an enhanced ongoing monitoring frequency and the next EDD review date.","File completed responses and evidence in the relationship record."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence and senior approval for high-risk relationships and PEPs."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires enhanced CDD measures commensurate with higher risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires source of wealth and source of funds checks for PEP relationships."}]}},{"id":"tbml","label":"🚢 TBML Analyzer","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Analyses trade documentation and goods flows to detect trade-based money laundering such as over or under invoicing and phantom shipments. The MLRO uses it on trade finance and trade-linked transactions.","steps":["Collect the invoice, bill of lading, packing list, and letter of credit for the trade transaction.","Compare declared unit prices against market value to detect over or under invoicing.","Check quantity, weight, and goods description consistency across all trade documents.","Verify shipping routes, vessels, and ports against the parties and stated trade rationale.","Screen all trade counterparties and intermediaries for sanctions and high-risk jurisdiction exposure.","Document discrepancies and feed confirmed TBML indicators to the STR and typology tools."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires scrutiny of transactions including trade flows to detect laundering of illicit proceeds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing scrutiny of complex and unusual trade transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Supports reporting of trade transactions giving rise to suspicion."}]}},{"id":"str-narrative","label":"✍️ STR Drafter","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Assembles a structured suspicious transaction report narrative formatted for submission through the FIU goAML portal. The MLRO uses it to convert a confirmed suspicion into a complete, citable filing.","steps":["Confirm the suspicion ground and the underlying predicate or TF concern before drafting.","Populate subject, account, and transaction details in the goAML data fields.","Write the narrative covering who, what, when, how much, and why it is suspicious in chronological order.","Attach supporting evidence and reference the red flags and typology identified.","Submit through the goAML portal without delay and record the submission reference.","Maintain confidentiality and avoid any tipping-off of the subject."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires filing of suspicious transaction reports to the FIU and prohibits tipping-off."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting to the FIU of suspicion of proceeds of crime or terrorist financing."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 21","note":"Provides protection for reporting and prohibits disclosure of the report to the customer."}]}},{"id":"wire-r16","label":"🔁 Wire R.16 Checker","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Checks wire transfers for complete and accurate originator and beneficiary information under the FATF travel rule. The MLRO uses it to detect missing or meaningless payment-chain data and decide on hold or reject.","steps":["Verify the wire contains required originator name, account, and address or equivalent identifier.","Verify the wire contains the required beneficiary name and account information.","Flag missing, incomplete, or meaningless originator or beneficiary data.","For intermediary roles, confirm all received information is transmitted onward in the chain.","Apply risk-based policy to hold, reject, or suspend wires lacking required information and consider an STR.","Record the completeness check outcome and any follow-up requests to the ordering institution."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires accompanying information on wire transfers to support traceability of funds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Requires complete originator and beneficiary information to accompany wire transfers along the payment chain."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Sets implementing procedures for wire transfer information requirements."}]}},{"id":"pf-screener","label":"☢️ Proliferation Finance Screener","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Screens parties, goods, and counterparties for proliferation financing exposure linked to weapons of mass destruction programmes and related UN designations. The MLRO uses it on dual-use goods and high-risk jurisdiction dealings.","steps":["Screen all parties against UN proliferation-related designation lists and the UAE Local Terrorist List.","Identify dual-use goods, controlled technology, or sensitive end uses in the transaction.","Assess exposure to high-risk jurisdictions of proliferation concern and front or intermediary entities.","Where a designated party or PF nexus is found, freeze without delay and notify the competent authority.","Document the screening lists, goods analysis, and the freeze or clearance decision."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Criminalises proliferation financing and requires implementation of related targeted financial sanctions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7","note":"Requires targeted financial sanctions related to proliferation and financing of weapons of mass destruction."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020","note":"Sets the framework for implementing UN proliferation-related sanctions and freezing without delay."}]}},{"id":"mlro-memo","label":"📂 MLRO Decision Memo","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Produces a formal MLRO decision memorandum recording the reasoning, evidence, and outcome for a case. The MLRO uses it to create the auditable record of an escalate, report, freeze, or close decision.","steps":["State the case background, subject, and the trigger that brought the matter to the MLRO.","Summarise the evidence, red flags, typology, and screening results considered.","Set out the decision with explicit reasoning whether to report, freeze, exit, or close.","Record any conditions such as enhanced monitoring, restrictions, or follow-up actions.","Sign and date the memo with the MLRO identity and retain it for the statutory record period."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires the MLRO to assess internal reports and decide on STR filing, and to retain related records."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 11","note":"Requires retention of records sufficient to reconstruct the basis of decisions."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Defines the MLRO role and the procedures for assessing and acting on internal reports."}]}},{"id":"tf-screener","label":"💣 Terrorist Financing Screener","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Screens subjects and transactions for terrorist financing indicators and matches against terrorism-related designation lists. The MLRO uses it to detect funding of terrorism even where amounts are small and funds are licit in origin.","steps":["Screen all parties against the UAE Local Terrorist List and the UN Consolidated List.","Assess TF indicators such as links to conflict zones, charities of concern, or unexplained small recurring transfers.","Evaluate funds destined for high-risk jurisdictions or known terrorist financing channels.","On any positive designation match, freeze without delay and notify the competent authority.","File an STR for suspicion of terrorist financing and preserve confidentiality.","Document the screening basis, match status, and actions taken."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Criminalises terrorist financing and requires reporting and targeted financial sanctions implementation."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Requires targeted financial sanctions related to terrorism and terrorist financing."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 5","note":"Requires criminalisation of terrorist financing consistent with the relevant convention."}]}},{"id":"shell-detector","label":"🏚 Shell Company Detector","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Assesses an entity for shell company characteristics such as no genuine operations, nominee structures, and opaque ownership. The MLRO uses it to test whether a corporate customer is a front for laundering or sanctions evasion.","steps":["Verify whether the entity has genuine physical presence, employees, and operating activity.","Identify nominee directors or shareholders and registered-agent-only addresses.","Trace the beneficial ownership chain to its natural-person owners and note any opacity.","Compare account turnover against declared business activity for mismatch.","Where shell indicators are confirmed, escalate for EDD, STR consideration, or relationship exit."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires identification and verification of beneficial owners and understanding of the customer's business."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires transparency of beneficial ownership of legal persons to prevent misuse."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires understanding the purpose and nature of the business relationship."}]}},{"id":"adverse-classify","label":"📰 Adverse Media Classifier","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Classifies adverse media hits by relevance, predicate offence category, and credibility for a screened subject. The MLRO uses it to filter negative-news noise into actionable risk findings.","steps":["Confirm the media hit refers to the actual subject and not a false-positive name match.","Classify the allegation by predicate category such as fraud, corruption, trafficking, or sanctions.","Assess the credibility and recency of the source and whether matters are alleged or adjudicated.","Rate the materiality of confirmed hits against the customer risk profile.","Feed material confirmed adverse media into EDD, risk rescoring, and STR consideration."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires ongoing due diligence and risk reassessment using available information about the customer."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing CDD and updating of customer information including negative information."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based approach informed by relevant risk information."}]}},{"id":"case-timeline","label":"📅 Case Timeline Builder","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Builds a chronological timeline of events, transactions, alerts, and actions for a case. The MLRO uses it to evidence sequence and causation in the STR and decision record.","steps":["Collect dated events including onboarding, alerts, transactions, communications, and prior reports.","Order the events chronologically and resolve any conflicting timestamps.","Mark the key inflection points where suspicion arose or activity changed.","Annotate each event with its source document for traceability.","Export the timeline to support the STR narrative and decision memo."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires retention of records sufficient to reconstruct transactions and the basis of suspicion."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 11","note":"Requires retention of transaction and CDD records for the prescribed period to permit reconstruction."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Supports the evidenced basis for reporting suspicion to the FIU."}]}},{"id":"ml-predicate","label":"⚖️ ML Predicate Analyser","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Analyses case facts to identify the likely predicate offence underlying suspected money laundering. The MLRO uses it to articulate the criminal source of funds in the STR.","steps":["Review the fact pattern and proceeds flow to infer the likely underlying criminal conduct.","Map the facts to predicate categories such as fraud, corruption, tax evasion, or trafficking.","Assess whether the conduct constitutes a predicate offence under UAE law including foreign predicates.","Document the basis linking proceeds to the suspected predicate for the report.","Feed the identified predicate into the STR narrative and typology classification."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Defines money laundering and the scope of predicate offences whose proceeds may be laundered."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 3","note":"Requires money laundering to apply to the widest range of predicate offences."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Supports reporting where proceeds are suspected to derive from a predicate offence."}]}},{"id":"client-risk","label":"👤 Client Risk Scorer","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Scores an individual or entity customer for ML and TF risk across standardised factors to produce a customer risk rating. The MLRO uses it at onboarding and periodic review to set the level of due diligence.","steps":["Capture customer-type, occupation or activity, nationality, and residence risk factors.","Score product, channel, and geographic exposure including any high-risk jurisdiction links.","Incorporate PEP, sanctions, and adverse media findings into the score.","Derive the overall customer risk rating and assign the corresponding CDD or EDD level.","Record the score, drivers, and the periodic review interval for the rating."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires a risk-based approach and assessment of each customer's ML and TF risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires risk assessment and application of measures proportionate to assessed risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires CDD calibrated to the customer's risk rating."}]}},{"id":"jurisdiction-intel","label":"🌍 Jurisdiction Intelligence","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Assesses the ML, TF, and sanctions risk of jurisdictions connected to a customer or transaction. The MLRO uses it to weight geographic risk and trigger enhanced measures for high-risk countries.","steps":["Identify all jurisdictions connected to the customer, counterparties, and fund flows.","Check each against FATF higher-risk and increased-monitoring jurisdiction lists.","Assess sanctions regimes, secrecy, and proliferation concerns for each jurisdiction.","Apply enhanced due diligence where a high-risk jurisdiction is involved.","Record the jurisdiction risk assessment and feed it into the customer risk score."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires enhanced measures for relationships and transactions involving high-risk countries."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 19","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence for business relationships and transactions with higher-risk countries."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires geographic risk to be assessed within the risk-based approach."}]}},{"id":"ubo-risk","label":"🏛 UBO Risk Assessment","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Identifies and risk-assesses the ultimate beneficial owners behind a legal person or arrangement. The MLRO uses it to pierce layered ownership and confirm the natural persons who own or control the customer.","steps":["Obtain the full ownership and control structure for the entity or arrangement.","Identify natural persons holding ownership at or above the applicable beneficial ownership threshold.","Identify control by other means where no qualifying ownership interest exists.","Screen each identified UBO for PEP, sanctions, and adverse media exposure.","Verify UBO identity and document the ownership map and residual risk.","Escalate unexplained complexity or undisclosed UBOs for EDD or STR consideration."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires identification and verification of beneficial owners of legal persons and arrangements."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires adequate, accurate, and current beneficial ownership information for legal persons."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 25","note":"Requires beneficial ownership transparency for legal arrangements such as trusts."}]}},{"id":"benford","label":"📐 Benford Forensics","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Applies Benford's Law leading-digit analysis to transaction and accounting datasets to detect fabricated or manipulated figures. The MLRO uses it as a forensic screen over large volumes of amounts.","steps":["Assemble a sufficiently large dataset of transaction or ledger amounts for analysis.","Compute the observed leading-digit distribution and compare against the Benford expected curve.","Identify significant deviations that suggest fabricated, capped, or split amounts.","Investigate the deviating digit clusters against underlying transactions and documents.","Document anomalies and feed confirmed manipulation indicators into structuring and STR analysis."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires scrutiny of transactions to detect indicators of laundering including manipulated activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing scrutiny of transactions for consistency and plausibility."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Supports reporting where forensic anomalies give rise to suspicion."}]}},{"id":"crypto-wallet","label":"₿ Crypto Wallet Analyser","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Analyses virtual asset wallet addresses and on-chain flows for exposure to illicit sources such as mixers, darknet markets, and sanctioned addresses. The MLRO uses it to risk-assess virtual asset activity and counterparties.","steps":["Identify the wallet addresses and virtual asset service providers involved in the activity.","Trace on-chain flows to and from the wallet across hops where data permits.","Screen counterparty addresses for links to mixers, darknet, ransomware, or sanctioned wallets.","Assess whether the originating or beneficiary VASP applied travel-rule information requirements.","Escalate exposure to illicit or sanctioned addresses for freeze, STR, or relationship action.","Document the trace, screening results, and risk conclusion."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Brings virtual assets and virtual asset service providers within the AML and CFT obligations."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Requires regulation and AML and CFT obligations for virtual assets and virtual asset service providers."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Extends the travel rule requiring originator and beneficiary information to virtual asset transfers."}]}},{"id":"onboarding-tier","label":"🎛 Onboarding Tier Selector","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Selects the appropriate onboarding due diligence tier, simplified, standard, or enhanced, based on the prospective customer's risk. The MLRO uses it to set the correct CDD level before account activation.","steps":["Capture the prospect's risk indicators including customer type, product, channel, and jurisdiction.","Run preliminary sanctions, PEP, and adverse media screening.","Map the risk profile to the simplified, standard, or enhanced due diligence tier.","Confirm that simplified measures are only applied where lower risk is justified and never for high-risk cases.","Require senior approval and EDD where the enhanced tier is selected.","Record the selected tier, rationale, and the required verification before activation."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires risk-based CDD with simplified or enhanced measures according to assessed risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires CDD measures calibrated to risk, including simplified and enhanced tiers."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires the risk-based allocation of due diligence resources."}]}},{"id":"prolif-finance","label":"☣️ Proliferation Finance","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Conducts a proliferation financing risk assessment of a relationship or transaction, covering end-use, end-user, and evasion typologies. The MLRO uses it to evaluate exposure to weapons proliferation networks and circumvention.","steps":["Assess the customer and transaction for proliferation financing risk indicators and evasion typologies.","Identify dual-use or controlled goods, suspect end-users, and concealment through intermediaries.","Screen all parties against UN proliferation-related and UAE designation lists.","On any nexus to a designated party, freeze without delay and notify the competent authority.","Document the PF risk assessment, screening basis, and reporting or freeze action taken."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Criminalises proliferation financing and requires implementing related targeted financial sanctions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7","note":"Requires targeted financial sanctions related to proliferation and its financing."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020","note":"Sets the UAE framework for proliferation-related sanctions and freezing without delay."}]}},{"id":"sar-triage","label":"🔍 SAR Triage","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Triages incoming internal suspicious activity reports to prioritise, deduplicate, and route them for MLRO decision. The MLRO uses it to manage report volume and ensure timely action on the highest-risk items.","steps":["Intake the internal reports and confirm completeness of the underlying information.","Deduplicate and link reports relating to the same subject or scheme.","Score each report for severity, urgency, and TF or sanctions sensitivity.","Prioritise the queue and route high-priority items for immediate MLRO assessment.","Record the triage outcome, assignment, and target decision time for each report."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires internal reporting to the MLRO and timely assessment leading to STR filing where warranted."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting to the FIU once suspicion is established."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Sets procedures for internal reporting channels and MLRO assessment."}]}},{"id":"doc-fraud","label":"🪪 Document Fraud Detector","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Examines identity and supporting documents for signs of forgery, alteration, or impersonation. The MLRO uses it during CDD and investigations to validate the authenticity of submitted documents.","steps":["Inspect identity documents for tampering, font inconsistencies, and security-feature anomalies.","Cross-check document data against independent and authoritative sources.","Detect signs of impersonation, photo substitution, or reused templates.","Verify supporting documents such as proof of address and source-of-funds evidence for authenticity.","Where fraud is indicated, halt onboarding, escalate, and consider an STR for identity fraud."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires verification of customer identity using reliable and independent source documents."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires identification and verification using reliable, independent source documents or data."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Supports reporting where document fraud gives rise to suspicion."}]}},{"id":"ctr-structuring","label":"💰 CTR / Structuring Analyser","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Analyses cash transactions for reporting thresholds and structuring designed to avoid them. The MLRO uses it to detect deliberate splitting of cash and to manage cash transaction reporting.","steps":["Aggregate cash transactions by customer over relevant periods and across linked accounts.","Identify amounts placed just below reporting thresholds and repeated near-threshold deposits.","Detect splitting across days, branches, or parties indicating deliberate structuring.","Apply the AED 55,000 cash threshold where dealing through DPMS or cash-intensive contexts.","Escalate confirmed structuring for STR filing and complete any required cash transaction report."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires monitoring and reporting of suspicious cash activity including attempts to evade thresholds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires scrutiny of cash transactions for consistency with the customer profile."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Applies CDD to DNFBPs including the AED 55,000 cash threshold for dealers in precious metals and stones."}]}},{"id":"dnfbp-obligations","label":"🏪 DNFBP Obligations","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Maps the specific AML and CFT obligations applicable to designated non-financial businesses and professions such as real estate agents, dealers in precious metals and stones, auditors, and corporate service providers. The MLRO uses it to confirm sector-specific compliance duties.","steps":["Identify the DNFBP category and the activities that bring the business in scope.","Confirm the applicable triggering thresholds including the AED 55,000 cash threshold for precious metals and stones dealers.","Map the required CDD, record-keeping, screening, and STR obligations for that sector.","Confirm registration with the supervisor and use of the goAML platform for reporting.","Verify appointment of a compliance officer and adoption of risk-based policies and training.","Document the obligations checklist and any gaps for remediation."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Extends AML and CFT obligations to DNFBPs including CDD, record-keeping, and reporting duties."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Applies customer due diligence requirements to designated non-financial businesses and professions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 23","note":"Applies reporting, record-keeping, and internal controls requirements to DNFBPs."}]}},{"id":"cdd-refresh","label":"🔄 CDD Refresh Planner","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Builds a risk-based schedule for periodically refreshing Customer Due Diligence on existing relationships. An MLRO uses it to decide which customers are overdue for review and what evidence must be re-collected.","steps":["Pull the customer book and segment each relationship by its assigned risk rating (high, medium, low).","Apply review cycles to each segment, for example high-risk reviewed at least annually and lower-risk on a longer cycle.","Flag customers whose last full CDD predates the next due date or whose risk rating has changed since onboarding.","List the specific data points to re-verify per customer: identity documents, beneficial ownership, source of funds, and expected activity.","Generate a remediation queue with owners and deadlines, and escalate any customer where refresh cannot be completed.","Record completion and document the rationale where a refresh is deferred or a relationship is exited."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires ongoing due diligence and keeping CDD information current throughout the business relationship."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Mandates ongoing monitoring and periodic updating of CDD records, scaled to risk."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (AML/CFT Implementing Regulation)","note":"Sets out the obligation to review and update due diligence on a risk-sensitive basis."}]}},{"id":"vasp-risk","label":"🔗 VASP Risk Scorer","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Scores the ML/TF risk of a Virtual Asset Service Provider counterparty or exposure, covering licensing, jurisdiction, and travel-rule compliance. An MLRO uses it before onboarding or transacting with a crypto exchange or wallet provider.","steps":["Confirm whether the VASP is licensed and regulated in its home jurisdiction and check it against the relevant UAE supervisory registers.","Assess jurisdictional risk, including whether the VASP operates from a high-risk or non-cooperative country.","Verify the VASP can send and receive originator and beneficiary information under the travel rule.","Review exposure to mixers, privacy coins, and unhosted wallets in the counterparty's flow.","Assign a composite risk score and set transaction limits or enhanced monitoring triggers accordingly.","Document the decision and escalate VASPs that lack regulation or travel-rule capability for rejection or EDD."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Brings virtual asset activities within the AML/CFT obligations and supervision framework."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Requires VASPs to be licensed, supervised, and to apply preventive measures including the travel rule."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Requires originator and beneficiary information to accompany virtual asset transfers."}]}},{"id":"goaml-validator","label":"📤 goAML Field Validator","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Checks a draft Suspicious Transaction or Activity Report against the UAE FIU goAML schema before submission. An MLRO uses it to catch missing or malformed fields that would cause the FIU portal to reject the filing.","steps":["Map each report field to the corresponding goAML XML element and confirm all mandatory fields are populated.","Validate party identifiers, dates, amounts, and currency codes against the goAML format rules.","Confirm the reason for suspicion narrative is present, specific, and linked to the identified transactions.","Check that all involved persons, accounts, and entities are cross-referenced consistently within the report.","Run the schema validation and resolve every error and warning before generating the final submission file.","Log the validated report and retain the submission receipt and supporting documentation."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious transactions to the Financial Intelligence Unit without delay."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting of suspicions to the FIU."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (AML/CFT Implementing Regulation)","note":"Specifies the obligation to file STRs through the FIU goAML system."}]}},{"id":"pep-edd","label":"🎖 PEP EDD Questionnaire","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Generates an Enhanced Due Diligence questionnaire tailored to Politically Exposed Persons, their family members, and close associates. An MLRO uses it to gather the heightened information required before approving a PEP relationship.","steps":["Classify the PEP type: foreign, domestic, or international organisation official, and identify any family members or known close associates.","Obtain senior management approval to establish or continue the relationship.","Collect detailed source of wealth and source of funds evidence proportionate to the PEP's position.","Document the nature of the public function and assess the corruption and TF risk it presents.","Set an enhanced ongoing monitoring regime with shortened review cycles for the relationship.","Escalate to the MLRO and board where adverse media or unexplained wealth is identified."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence measures for politically exposed persons."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires senior management approval, source of wealth checks, and enhanced monitoring for PEPs."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (AML/CFT Implementing Regulation)","note":"Defines PEP categories and mandates EDD and senior approval for such relationships."}]}},{"id":"sanctions-mapper","label":"🗺 Sanctions Mapper","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Maps a customer or counterparty to applicable sanctions regimes and traces ownership or control links to listed persons. An MLRO uses it to determine which lists apply and whether indirect exposure triggers a freeze obligation.","steps":["Identify all applicable sanctions regimes: UAE local terrorist lists, UN Security Council lists, and any other regimes the institution is exposed to.","Screen the named party and resolve potential matches against each list.","Trace beneficial ownership and control to detect indirect exposure to listed persons, applying the ownership and control test.","Distinguish exact, fuzzy, and false matches and document the basis for each disposition.","Where a true match is confirmed, trigger the freeze workflow and notify the Executive Office without delay.","Retain the mapping, screening evidence, and decision rationale for audit."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires implementation of targeted financial sanctions and freezing without delay."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Requires implementation of targeted financial sanctions related to terrorism and terrorist financing."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 (Terrorism Lists and TFS)","note":"Governs implementation of UN Security Council and UAE Local Terrorist List sanctions."}]}},{"id":"layering-detector","label":"🔀 Layering Detector","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Detects layering, the second stage of money laundering, by spotting rapid, complex movements of funds designed to obscure origin. An MLRO uses it to surface structuring chains and pass-through activity for investigation.","steps":["Reconstruct the chain of transactions across accounts, entities, and jurisdictions for the period under review.","Identify rapid in-and-out movements, round-tripping, and pass-through accounts with no economic purpose.","Flag the use of multiple intermediaries, shell entities, or unrelated third parties in the flow.","Compare the activity to the customer's expected profile and stated business rationale.","Score the layering indicators and document those that meet the suspicion threshold.","Escalate confirmed layering patterns to the MLRO for an STR decision."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Defines the laundering of proceeds, including concealment and disguise of their origin, as a predicate offence."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting where layering activity gives rise to suspicion of money laundering."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires scrutiny of transactions inconsistent with the customer's known profile."}]}},{"id":"real-estate-ml","label":"🏠 Real Estate ML Risk","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Assesses money-laundering risk in real estate transactions, a recognised high-risk sector in the UAE. An MLRO at a broker or developer uses it to screen deals for laundering red flags before completion.","steps":["Identify the buyer, seller, and beneficial owner and verify identity for all parties to the transaction.","Check whether payment is made in cash, by third party, or through complex or offshore structures.","Assess price-to-market mismatch, rapid resale, and below or above market valuations as red flags.","Verify and document the source of funds for the purchase.","Report cash transactions at or above the regulatory threshold and file an STR where suspicion arises.","Retain the transaction file and risk assessment for the required retention period."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Subjects designated non-financial businesses including real estate to AML/CFT obligations."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Applies CDD requirements to real estate agents in property transactions."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (AML/CFT Implementing Regulation)","note":"Sets DNFBP obligations and reporting thresholds for real estate dealings."}]}},{"id":"asset-tracer","label":"🔎 Asset Tracer","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Traces the flow and current location of funds or assets across accounts, entities, and jurisdictions. An MLRO uses it to support an investigation, an STR, or a response to a law-enforcement or freezing request.","steps":["Define the asset or fund flow to be traced and the start and end points of the period.","Follow transfers through intermediary accounts, entities, and jurisdictions to identify the ultimate destination.","Identify the beneficial owners and controllers at each node in the chain.","Flag points where the trail is obscured, for example cash withdrawals, virtual assets, or offshore vehicles.","Document the traced path with supporting transaction evidence for each step.","Provide the trace to support an STR, a freeze, or a response to a competent authority request."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Empowers authorities to trace, freeze, and confiscate proceeds of crime and requires institutions to assist."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 4","note":"Requires measures to enable confiscation of laundered property and proceeds of crime."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 31","note":"Requires the ability to identify, trace, and obtain financial records during investigations."}]}},{"id":"sow-calculator","label":"💼 Source of Wealth Calculator","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Reconstructs and tests the plausibility of a customer's overall source of wealth against the assets and activity observed. An MLRO uses it during EDD to confirm wealth is consistent with legitimate origins.","steps":["Itemise the declared sources of wealth: employment, business, inheritance, investments, and asset sales.","Collect documentary evidence for each component, such as financial statements, sale contracts, or tax records.","Quantify each source and compare the aggregate to the customer's observed assets and account activity.","Identify and challenge any material unexplained gap between declared wealth and observed wealth.","Assign a plausibility conclusion and record supporting evidence and any residual concerns.","Escalate unexplained or implausible wealth to the MLRO for EDD or STR consideration."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires understanding the source of funds and wealth as part of due diligence on higher-risk relationships."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires CDD measures including understanding the nature and source of customer funds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires source of wealth establishment for PEPs and other higher-risk customers."}]}},{"id":"insider-threat-screen","label":"🕵️ Insider Threat Screen","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Screens employees and insiders for indicators of collusion, tipping-off, or facilitation of money laundering. An MLRO uses it to detect internal compromise of the AML control framework.","steps":["Review staff access to customer accounts, overrides, and exception approvals against role expectations.","Look for indicators of tipping-off, suppressed alerts, or unexplained overrides of controls.","Cross-check employee connections to high-risk customers, PEPs, or flagged counterparties.","Assess unusual changes in employee lifestyle, behaviour, or undisclosed conflicts of interest.","Document findings confidentially and escalate to the MLRO and senior management or HR.","Preserve evidence and avoid alerting the subject where misconduct is suspected."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Prohibits tipping-off and requires internal controls and employee screening within the AML/CFT programme."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires internal controls and employee screening procedures to ensure high standards."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 21","note":"Addresses the prohibition on tipping-off and protection of reporting staff."}]}},{"id":"board-aml-report","label":"📊 Board AML Report","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Compiles the periodic MLRO report to the board and senior management on the state of the AML/CFT programme. An MLRO uses it to discharge the obligation to keep governance informed of compliance performance and risks.","steps":["Summarise STR volumes, screening alerts, and the disposition of escalated cases for the period.","Report the status of the institution-wide risk assessment and any material changes in risk exposure.","Highlight control deficiencies, audit findings, and the status of remediation actions.","Report regulatory developments, examinations, and any enforcement exposure or correspondence.","Present training completion, resourcing, and the adequacy of the AML programme.","Record board decisions and approvals and retain the report as governance evidence."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires senior management oversight of the AML/CFT compliance function and the MLRO role."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires internal programmes with governance oversight of AML/CFT controls."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based approach informed and overseen at the senior management level."}]}},{"id":"enforcement-exposure","label":"⚠️ Enforcement Exposure","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Estimates the institution's exposure to regulatory enforcement, penalties, and administrative sanctions arising from AML/CFT deficiencies. An MLRO uses it to prioritise remediation and brief management on liability.","steps":["Inventory known control gaps, breaches, late filings, and supervisory findings.","Map each gap to the specific AML/CFT obligation it breaches and the applicable sanction.","Assess severity, recurrence, and likely supervisory response for each exposure.","Estimate potential administrative penalties and reputational and licensing impact.","Prioritise remediation by exposure and assign owners and deadlines.","Brief senior management and document the exposure assessment and mitigation plan."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Establishes administrative penalties and sanctions for breaches of AML/CFT obligations."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 35","note":"Requires effective, proportionate, and dissuasive sanctions for AML/CFT failures."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (AML/CFT Implementing Regulation)","note":"Details supervisory powers and the framework for administrative penalties."}]}},{"id":"inter-agency-referral","label":"📨 Inter-Agency Referral","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Prepares a structured referral or information exchange to a competent authority such as the FIU, supervisor, or law enforcement. An MLRO uses it to route matters beyond an STR to the correct agency.","steps":["Identify the correct receiving authority based on the subject matter and legal basis for the referral.","Assemble the case facts, parties, and supporting evidence relevant to the receiving authority.","Confirm the legal gateway permitting the disclosure and apply tipping-off safeguards.","Draft the referral with a clear statement of the issue and the action requested.","Transmit through the approved channel and log the submission and any reference number.","Track the response and retain a confidential record of the referral."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides for cooperation with and reporting to competent authorities and the FIU."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 29","note":"Establishes the FIU as the central agency for receiving and analysing reports."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 31","note":"Supports access to information by competent authorities for investigations."}]}},{"id":"policy-reviewer","label":"📃 Policy Reviewer","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Reviews internal AML/CFT policies and procedures for completeness and alignment with current UAE law and FATF standards. An MLRO uses it during periodic policy refresh or after a regulatory change.","steps":["Map each required policy area to the current legal and regulatory requirement it must satisfy.","Identify gaps, outdated references, and provisions superseded by recent legal changes.","Check that CDD, EDD, screening, reporting, and record-keeping procedures are operationally specific.","Confirm roles, responsibilities, and escalation paths are clearly assigned.","Recommend revisions, route them for senior management approval, and version-control the policy.","Schedule the next review and communicate changes to relevant staff."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires documented internal policies, controls, and procedures proportionate to risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires institutions to maintain internal policies and procedures to combat ML/TF."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires policies to reflect a risk-based approach and be kept current."}]}},{"id":"compliance-test-planner","label":"🧪 Compliance Test Planner","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Designs a testing plan to independently verify that AML/CFT controls operate as intended. An MLRO or compliance testing function uses it to scope sample-based assurance over key controls.","steps":["Identify the controls to test, such as screening, CDD completeness, transaction monitoring, and STR timeliness.","Define test objectives, populations, and risk-based sample sizes for each control.","Set the testing methodology and pass or fail criteria for each test.","Execute the tests, record exceptions, and assess root cause and severity.","Document findings, assign remediation owners and deadlines, and rate residual risk.","Report results to the MLRO and senior management and schedule retesting."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires an independent audit function to test the AML/CFT programme."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires an independent audit function to test the AML/CFT system."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires controls to be tested and proportionate to assessed risk."}]}},{"id":"swift-lc-analyzer","label":"🏦 SWIFT / LC Analyser","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Analyses SWIFT messages and Letters of Credit for trade-based money laundering and sanctions red flags. An MLRO in trade finance uses it to scrutinise documentary credits and wire instructions.","steps":["Parse the SWIFT message or LC and identify all parties, including applicant, beneficiary, and intermediary banks.","Screen every party, vessel, port, and goods description against sanctions and high-risk lists.","Check for goods or price anomalies, vague descriptions, and dual-use or controlled items.","Identify routing through high-risk jurisdictions and stripped or incomplete payment information.","Verify the trade has a plausible commercial rationale consistent with the customer profile.","Escalate red flags to the MLRO and file an STR where suspicion is confirmed."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Applies AML/CFT and sanctions obligations to trade finance and cross-border payment activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Requires complete originator and beneficiary information on wire transfers."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Requires sanctions screening of all parties to a transaction."}]}},{"id":"regulatory-calendar","label":"📅 Regulatory Calendar","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Maintains a calendar of recurring AML/CFT regulatory deadlines, filings, and obligations. An MLRO uses it to ensure no statutory reporting or review date is missed.","steps":["List all recurring obligations: STR and goAML filings, risk assessment refresh, training, and supervisory returns.","Assign the due date, frequency, and responsible owner to each obligation.","Add ad hoc deadlines arising from new regulations, examinations, or remediation commitments.","Set advance reminders so preparation begins before each deadline.","Track completion status and flag any approaching or overdue items.","Review the calendar periodically and update it when obligations change."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Imposes time-bound reporting and compliance obligations on regulated entities."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 33","note":"Supports timely maintenance and reporting of compliance statistics and obligations."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (AML/CFT Implementing Regulation)","note":"Sets recurring obligations including reporting and record-keeping timelines."}]}},{"id":"ewra-generator","label":"📋 EWRA Generator","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Builds the Enterprise-Wide Risk Assessment documenting the institution's inherent ML/TF risk and the effectiveness of its controls. An MLRO uses it to produce the foundational risk document the risk-based approach depends on.","steps":["Identify inherent risk across the four pillars: customers, products and services, geographies, and delivery channels.","Score inherent risk for each pillar using defined likelihood and impact criteria.","Assess the design and operating effectiveness of mitigating controls.","Derive residual risk by applying control effectiveness to inherent risk.","Document conclusions, identify control gaps, and propose actions to address them.","Obtain senior management approval and schedule periodic reassessment."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires institutions to identify, assess, and document their ML/TF risks."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a documented risk assessment underpinning the risk-based approach."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (AML/CFT Implementing Regulation)","note":"Mandates a business risk assessment kept up to date and available to the supervisor."}]}},{"id":"aml-programme-gap","label":"🔍 AML Programme Gap","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Performs a gap analysis of the AML/CFT programme against legal and FATF requirements. An MLRO uses it to identify missing or weak components before an exam or as part of an annual health check.","steps":["Build a requirements checklist from UAE AML/CFT law and the FATF standards.","Map each requirement to the corresponding programme component and supporting evidence.","Identify components that are missing, partial, or not operating effectively.","Rate each gap by severity and regulatory significance.","Develop a remediation roadmap with owners, milestones, and target dates.","Report the gap analysis to senior management and track closure."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires a comprehensive AML/CFT programme covering controls, training, and audit."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires institutions to implement programmes against money laundering and terrorist financing."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires the programme to be commensurate with assessed risks."}]}},{"id":"trade-invoice-analyzer","label":"🧾 Trade Invoice Analyser","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Analyses commercial invoices for over- or under-invoicing and other trade-based money laundering indicators. An MLRO uses it to detect value manipulation used to move illicit value across borders.","steps":["Extract goods description, quantity, unit price, and total value from the invoice.","Benchmark the unit price against fair market value for the goods and route.","Check for over-invoicing, under-invoicing, and multiple invoicing of the same shipment.","Flag vague or generic goods descriptions and quantity-to-value mismatches.","Cross-check parties and shipment details against the related LC and shipping documents.","Escalate confirmed mispricing indicators to the MLRO for an STR decision."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Treats disguising the origin and value of proceeds, including via trade, as money laundering."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious trade activity to the FIU."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires scrutiny of transactions inconsistent with the customer's commercial profile."}]}},{"id":"network-mapper","label":"🕸 Network Mapper","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Maps relationships and links between customers, accounts, entities, and counterparties to expose hidden networks. An MLRO uses it to reveal connected parties, common beneficial owners, and collusive rings.","steps":["Define the seed entities and the relationship types to map, such as ownership, control, and transactions.","Build the network graph linking accounts, beneficial owners, addresses, and counterparties.","Identify shared attributes that indicate hidden connections, such as common addresses, directors, or devices.","Highlight clusters, central nodes, and conduits that warrant closer scrutiny.","Overlay risk indicators such as sanctions hits, PEP status, and prior STRs onto the network.","Document the network findings to support investigations, EDD, or an STR."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires identifying beneficial ownership and connected parties as part of due diligence."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires transparency of beneficial ownership of legal persons."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires identification of beneficial owners and the control structure of customers."}]}},{"id":"risk-appetite-builder","label":"🎯 Risk Appetite Builder","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Defines the institution's AML/CFT risk appetite, setting the boundaries of acceptable ML/TF risk. An MLRO uses it to translate board tolerance into actionable thresholds and prohibited categories.","steps":["Articulate the board's tolerance for ML/TF risk in qualitative and quantitative terms.","Define prohibited customer types, products, and jurisdictions that fall outside appetite.","Set thresholds and limits for higher-risk categories that require enhanced controls or approval.","Align the appetite statement with the enterprise-wide risk assessment results.","Obtain board approval and embed the appetite into onboarding and monitoring rules.","Monitor breaches of appetite and report them to senior management."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires a risk-based approach with controls proportionate to the institution's risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires institutions to apply a risk-based approach reflecting their risk tolerance."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires governance and internal controls that operationalise risk decisions."}]}},{"id":"regulatory-exam-prep","label":"📚 Regulatory Exam Prep","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Prepares the institution for a supervisory AML/CFT examination by assembling evidence and anticipating examiner focus. An MLRO uses it to organise documentation and readiness ahead of an inspection.","steps":["Assemble the document request pack: policies, risk assessment, STR logs, training records, and audit reports.","Anticipate examiner focus areas based on the institution's risk profile and prior findings.","Run a mock review of key files to confirm CDD, EDD, and STR quality.","Identify and remediate obvious gaps before the examination begins.","Brief and prepare staff likely to be interviewed by examiners.","Set up a process to track examiner requests and responses during the exam."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Subjects institutions to supervisory inspection and requires cooperation with the supervisor."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 27","note":"Provides supervisors with powers to inspect and require production of information."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 26","note":"Requires risk-based supervision of regulated institutions."}]}},{"id":"npo-risk","label":"🏛 NPO Risk","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Assesses the terrorist-financing risk of non-profit organisation customers and their activities. An MLRO uses it to apply proportionate scrutiny to charities and associations without disrupting legitimate work.","steps":["Identify the NPO's purpose, beneficiaries, funding sources, and areas of operation.","Assess exposure to conflict zones or high-risk jurisdictions in fund disbursement.","Verify governance, registration, and control over how funds are distributed.","Flag cash-intensive flows, opaque onward transfers, and links to high-risk parties.","Apply proportionate, risk-based monitoring rather than blanket de-risking.","Escalate TF indicators to the MLRO and file an STR where suspicion arises."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires measures to prevent the misuse of entities, including NPOs, for terrorist financing."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 8","note":"Requires a risk-based review of the non-profit sector to prevent TF abuse."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires proportionate, risk-based measures rather than indiscriminate de-risking."}]}},{"id":"correspondent-bank","label":"🏦 Correspondent Bank Risk","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Assesses the ML/TF risk of correspondent banking relationships and respondent institutions. An MLRO uses it to perform the enhanced due diligence required before opening or maintaining a correspondent account.","steps":["Gather information on the respondent's ownership, management, business, and AML/CFT controls.","Assess the respondent's home jurisdiction and supervisory regime for AML/CFT quality.","Confirm the respondent is not a shell bank and does not permit payable-through-account misuse.","Understand the nature of the respondent's customer base and the purpose of the relationship.","Obtain senior management approval and document the respective AML/CFT responsibilities.","Apply ongoing monitoring of correspondent activity and review the relationship periodically."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence and senior approval for correspondent banking relationships."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13","note":"Sets specific correspondent banking due diligence and prohibits relationships with shell banks."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Applies where respondent ownership or control involves PEPs."}]}},{"id":"mixed-funds","label":"🌀 Mixed Funds Analyser","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Analyses commingled accounts where legitimate and potentially illicit funds are mixed. An MLRO uses it to separate clean from suspect flows and assess whether the commingling itself is a red flag.","steps":["Map all inflows to the account and classify each by apparent source and legitimacy.","Identify illegitimate or unexplained inflows commingled with legitimate funds.","Trace outflows to determine whether suspect funds were moved onward or layered.","Assess whether the commingling pattern has a plausible business rationale.","Quantify the suspect proportion and document the analysis.","Escalate to the MLRO for an STR and consider account restrictions where warranted."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Treats concealing the illicit origin of funds, including by commingling, as money laundering."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires scrutiny of complex or unusual flows lacking apparent economic purpose."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting where commingled flows give rise to suspicion."}]}},{"id":"sanctions-breach","label":"🚨 Sanctions Breach Response","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Guides the response when a sanctions breach or near-miss is identified. An MLRO uses it to contain the breach, meet freeze and notification duties, and remediate the underlying control failure.","steps":["Confirm the match is a true positive and identify the assets, parties, and funds involved.","Freeze the relevant funds and accounts without delay and block further transactions.","Notify the Executive Office for Control and Non-Proliferation and relevant authorities within the required timeframe.","Determine the root cause of the breach and how the exposure occurred.","Preserve all evidence and document the breach timeline and actions taken.","Remediate the control failure and report to senior management."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires freezing without delay and reporting of sanctions matches to authorities."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Requires immediate implementation of targeted financial sanctions."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 (Terrorism Lists and TFS)","note":"Sets freeze and notification obligations and timelines for sanctions matches."}]}},{"id":"freeze-seizure","label":"❄️ Freeze / Seizure Guide","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Guides the lawful execution of a freeze or seizure order on customer assets. An MLRO uses it to apply, document, and maintain a freeze in response to a sanctions listing or a competent authority order.","steps":["Verify the legal basis for the freeze, whether a sanctions listing or an order from a competent authority.","Identify all in-scope accounts, assets, and funds held directly or indirectly for the subject.","Apply the freeze without delay and block debits, transfers, and dealing with the assets.","Notify the relevant authority and, where required, the customer in line with the rules.","Record the scope, timing, and value of the frozen assets and retain the order.","Maintain the freeze until lawfully lifted and handle any permitted-payment requests through the authority."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides for freezing and seizure of funds and the duty to comply with such orders."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Requires freezing of funds of designated persons without delay."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020 (Terrorism Lists and TFS)","note":"Governs the mechanics of implementing freezing measures in the UAE."}]}},{"id":"audit-response","label":"📋 Audit Response Builder","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Builds structured management responses to internal or external AML/CFT audit findings. An MLRO uses it to acknowledge findings, commit to remediation, and evidence closure.","steps":["Catalogue each audit finding with its rating, root cause, and the obligation affected.","Draft a management response stating acceptance or rationale for each finding.","Define remediation actions, owners, and target dates for every accepted finding.","Identify interim mitigating controls where full remediation will take time.","Track progress against milestones and collect evidence of closure.","Report remediation status to senior management and confirm closure with the auditors."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires an independent audit function and remediation of identified deficiencies."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires an independent audit function to test and improve the AML/CFT system."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires controls to be reviewed and strengthened in line with risk."}]}},{"id":"high-net-worth","label":"💎 HNW Client Profile","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Builds an enhanced risk profile for high-net-worth and private banking clients. An MLRO uses it to apply heightened due diligence proportionate to the wealth, complexity, and risk such clients present.","steps":["Establish the client's identity, beneficial ownership, and any complex holding structures.","Document and corroborate source of wealth and source of funds with evidence.","Assess PEP status, adverse media, and exposure to high-risk jurisdictions.","Define the expected activity profile and the products and services to be used.","Set an enhanced ongoing monitoring regime and a shortened review cycle.","Obtain senior management approval and document the basis for accepting the relationship."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence for higher-risk customers and relationships."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires CDD scaled to risk, including identification of beneficial owners."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires source of wealth and enhanced monitoring where the client is a PEP."}]}},{"id":"cash-intensive","label":"💵 Cash-Intensive Business","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Assesses the ML risk of cash-intensive business customers whose declared turnover should be tested against cash activity. An MLRO uses it to detect cash used to launder proceeds through legitimate-looking businesses.","steps":["Establish the business model and the level of cash turnover it should plausibly generate.","Compare observed cash deposits and withdrawals against the expected profile and declared revenue.","Flag deposits structured below reporting thresholds and rapid cash-to-transfer movements.","Assess whether cash activity is consistent with the sector, location, and customer base.","Apply DPMS and other applicable cash thresholds, including the AED 55,000 threshold for precious metals and stones dealers.","Escalate unexplained cash patterns to the MLRO and file an STR where suspicion arises."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Subjects cash-intensive and DNFBP activities to AML/CFT obligations and reporting."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Applies CDD to dealers in precious metals and stones above the cash threshold."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires scrutiny of cash activity inconsistent with the customer's profile."}]}},{"id":"trust-structures","label":"🔐 Trust Structures","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Maps and risk-rates trust and fiduciary arrangements (settlor, trustee, protector, beneficiaries) to expose layering and control concealment. An MLRO uses it when onboarding or reviewing a customer that sits behind a trust or similar legal arrangement.","steps":["Identify every party to the trust: settlor, trustee, protector, named and discretionary beneficiaries, and any letter of wishes.","Determine who exercises effective control and trace the natural person ultimate beneficial owner behind the arrangement.","Apply enhanced due diligence where the trust is in a high-risk jurisdiction, holds bearer instruments, or uses a professional nominee trustee.","Verify the source of funds settled into the trust and the source of wealth of the settlor.","Record the rationale, classify the relationship risk, and set a review cadence proportionate to that risk."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires identification and verification of beneficial owners of legal arrangements, including trusts, and ongoing due diligence."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 25","note":"Sets transparency and beneficial ownership obligations specific to trusts and other legal arrangements."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires CDD measures to identify the beneficial owner behind legal arrangements before and during the relationship."}]}},{"id":"cross-border-wire","label":"🌐 Cross-Border Wire Risk","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Scores international wire transfers for ML/TF risk by checking originator and beneficiary information, intermediary chains, and corridor exposure. An MLRO uses it to triage suspicious cross-border payments and confirm travel-rule completeness.","steps":["Confirm the wire carries complete and accurate originator and beneficiary information per the travel rule.","Assess the originating and destination corridors against high-risk and sanctioned jurisdiction lists.","Flag stripping, missing fields, or use of opaque intermediary banks in the payment chain.","Compare amount, frequency, and counterparties against the customer's expected transactional profile.","Escalate unexplained high-risk corridor activity to STR consideration and document the decision."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Mandates obtaining and transmitting originator and beneficiary information and monitoring of cross-border transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Wire transfer rule requiring accurate originator and beneficiary data to travel with the payment."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13","note":"Governs correspondent banking and intermediary risk in cross-border payment chains."}]}},{"id":"fiu-feedback","label":"📬 FIU Feedback Handler","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Processes feedback, requests, and instructions received from the UAE FIU via goAML and turns them into actioned tasks. An MLRO uses it to track FIU responses to filed reports and to manage information requests and freeze directives.","steps":["Log each FIU communication received through goAML with reference number, date, and required action.","Map the feedback back to the originating STR or SAR and update the case record.","Action any FIU request for additional information within the stated deadline and retain proof of submission.","Implement any no-consent or hold directive on the relevant account and notify relevant internal stakeholders.","Capture lessons learned to refine future report quality and monitoring rules."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires cooperation with the FIU, response to information requests, and compliance with FIU directives."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 29","note":"Defines the FIU's role and the obligation of reporting entities to provide requested information."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Frames the reporting relationship that generates FIU feedback and follow-up requests."}]}},{"id":"derisking-impact","label":"⚖️ De-Risking Impact","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Assesses whether exiting or refusing a customer or segment is proportionate or amounts to wholesale de-risking. An MLRO uses it before terminating relationships to balance financial inclusion against residual ML/TF risk.","steps":["Document the specific risk driving the proposed exit rather than relying on a blanket category judgement.","Test whether enhanced due diligence or risk mitigation could manage the relationship instead of termination.","Assess financial inclusion and access impact on the customer or sector being exited.","Confirm whether an STR is required before or alongside any account closure.","Record the proportionality analysis and obtain senior sign-off for the de-risking decision."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires a risk-based approach where mitigation is calibrated to actual risk rather than blanket exclusion."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Mandates a risk-based approach and warns that de-risking should not be used to avoid managing risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Permits relationship continuation with appropriate CDD where risk can be managed."}]}},{"id":"legal-privilege","label":"🔏 Legal Privilege Checker","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Determines whether material withheld or flagged is genuinely covered by legal professional privilege or must still be reported. An MLRO uses it when a lawyer or DNFBP client asserts privilege over information relevant to a suspicion.","steps":["Identify whether the communication is between a client and legal adviser for the purpose of legal advice.","Distinguish privileged legal advice from information passed to further a criminal purpose, which is not protected.","Confirm whether the asserting party falls within a profession entitled to claim privilege under UAE law.","Assess whether the reporting obligation overrides the privilege claim in the specific circumstances.","Document the privilege determination and the basis for reporting or withholding."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Sets reporting obligations and the limited scope within which legal professionals may rely on privilege."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 23","note":"Applies reporting duties to legal professionals subject to genuine legal professional privilege exceptions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Defines DNFBP customer due diligence including the legal profession's obligations."}]}},{"id":"ml-scenario","label":"🎭 ML Scenario Builder","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Constructs money laundering placement, layering, and integration scenarios to design and tune monitoring rules. An MLRO uses it to translate typologies into testable detection scenarios for the transaction monitoring system.","steps":["Select the predicate offence and laundering stage the scenario is intended to detect.","Define the behavioural indicators, thresholds, and time windows that characterise the scenario.","Map the scenario to relevant FATF typologies and known UAE risk exposures.","Back-test the scenario against historical data to estimate alert volume and false-positive rate.","Document scenario logic, calibration, and sign-off for inclusion in the monitoring framework."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires ongoing transaction monitoring capable of detecting suspicious patterns and typologies."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires identification and assessment of ML/TF risks that monitoring scenarios must address."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Underpins the suspicious-transaction detection that scenarios are built to trigger."}]}},{"id":"staff-alert","label":"🚨 Staff Alert Generator","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Generates targeted internal alerts and red-flag bulletins to frontline and operations staff on emerging risks. An MLRO uses it to push timely typology warnings and tipping-off cautions to relevant teams.","steps":["Define the emerging risk or red flag that staff must watch for and the affected business lines.","Draft the alert in plain operational language with concrete indicators and the required escalation route.","Include explicit tipping-off cautions so staff do not disclose suspicions to the customer.","Target distribution to the relevant roles and record acknowledgement of receipt.","Track resulting internal referrals to measure whether the alert improved detection."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires internal procedures and the prohibition on tipping off that staff alerts must reinforce."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires internal controls and employee awareness programmes across the group."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 21","note":"Establishes the tipping-off prohibition that alerts must caution staff about."}]}},{"id":"str-quality","label":"📝 STR Quality Checker","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Reviews draft Suspicious Transaction Reports for completeness, clarity of grounds for suspicion, and goAML field accuracy before filing. An MLRO uses it as a final quality gate prior to submission to the FIU.","steps":["Confirm the report clearly states the grounds for suspicion in factual, specific terms.","Verify all mandatory goAML fields, subjects, accounts, and transactions are complete and correctly coded.","Check that the narrative explains the what, who, when, and why of the suspicion without unsupported conclusions.","Ensure supporting documents and transaction evidence are attached and referenced.","Confirm timeliness against the reporting deadline and record MLRO approval before filing."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires prompt and complete reporting of suspicious transactions to the FIU."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious transactions promptly and with sufficient grounds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 29","note":"Frames the data quality the FIU needs to analyse reports effectively."}]}},{"id":"hawala-detector","label":"💱 Hawala Detector","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Detects indicators of informal value transfer and unregistered hawala activity in account behaviour. An MLRO uses it to identify customers acting as unlicensed money transmitters or settling hawala positions.","steps":["Look for many unrelated third-party cash deposits followed by rapid outbound transfers to common corridors.","Identify settlement patterns, round-sum offsets, and minimal apparent commercial purpose.","Cross-check whether the customer holds a licence to provide money transfer services.","Map counterparties to detect a hub-and-spoke structure typical of hawaladars.","Escalate confirmed indicators for STR filing and consider relationship exit."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Covers money or value transfer services and prohibits unlicensed provision of such services."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 14","note":"Requires licensing or registration and supervision of money or value transfer service providers."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious informal value transfer activity."}]}},{"id":"nominee-risk","label":"🎭 Nominee Risk","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Assesses the risk that nominee shareholders or directors are concealing the true controller of a customer. An MLRO uses it when ownership filings show professional or proxy nominees standing in for unnamed principals.","steps":["Identify any nominee shareholder, nominee director, or declaration of trust over shares.","Trace through the nominee to the natural person ultimate beneficial owner who actually benefits or controls.","Determine whether nominee arrangements are disclosed and whether the nominee is professionally regulated.","Apply enhanced due diligence where nominees obscure control or sit in opaque jurisdictions.","Document the verified controller and refuse or exit where true ownership cannot be established."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires identification of beneficial owners despite nominee or proxy arrangements."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires transparency of beneficial ownership of legal persons including nominee disclosure."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires looking through nominees to the natural person ultimate beneficial owner."}]}},{"id":"pep-corporate","label":"🏢 PEP Corporate Screen","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Screens corporate customers for politically exposed persons embedded as owners, controllers, or signatories. An MLRO uses it to surface PEP exposure that hides behind a corporate veil rather than appearing as a direct individual customer.","steps":["Decompose the corporate structure to extract all beneficial owners, directors, and authorised signatories.","Screen each natural person against PEP, close associate, and family member datasets.","Classify the entity as PEP-connected where a PEP holds ownership or control.","Apply enhanced due diligence including source of wealth and senior management approval for PEP-connected entities.","Set ongoing monitoring and periodic re-screening for the corporate relationship."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires PEP identification and enhanced measures including for beneficial owners of legal persons."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires PEP identification and enhanced due diligence including family members and close associates."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires beneficial ownership transparency enabling PEP detection behind corporates."}]}},{"id":"crypto-mixing","label":"🌀 Crypto Mixing Detector","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Detects use of mixers, tumblers, and coin-join services that break the on-chain trail of virtual assets. An MLRO uses it when analysing virtual asset deposits whose provenance has been deliberately obfuscated.","steps":["Trace incoming virtual asset flows back through prior hops to identify mixer or tumbler interaction.","Flag exposure to known mixing services, privacy coins, and chain-hopping patterns.","Assess the proportion of customer inflows linked to obfuscation services.","Request source of funds evidence and decline where provenance cannot be established.","Escalate confirmed mixing exposure for STR filing and document the on-chain analysis."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Extends AML/CFT obligations to virtual asset service providers and virtual asset transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Applies AML/CFT and the travel rule to virtual assets and VASPs, addressing obfuscation risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires source of funds verification where provenance is obscured by mixing."}]}},{"id":"ghost-company","label":"👻 Ghost Company Detector","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Identifies shell and ghost companies with no genuine economic activity used to move or layer funds. An MLRO uses it when a corporate customer shows signs of being a paper entity rather than an operating business.","steps":["Check for absence of physical premises, employees, website, or verifiable trading history.","Compare declared business activity against actual account turnover and counterparties.","Flag mass-registration addresses, recent incorporation paired with high-value flows, and dormant-then-active patterns.","Verify beneficial ownership and the genuine economic rationale for the structure.","Escalate confirmed ghost entities for STR consideration and relationship exit."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires understanding the purpose and nature of the business relationship and beneficial ownership."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Targets misuse of legal persons and shell companies for beneficial ownership concealment."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires CDD to understand the genuine nature and purpose of the customer's activity."}]}},{"id":"pkeyc-planner","label":"🔄 pKYC Planner","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Plans perpetual KYC by defining event triggers and data refresh cycles that replace fixed periodic reviews. An MLRO uses it to move from calendar-based reviews to continuous, risk-driven CDD refresh.","steps":["Define the trigger events such as ownership change, address change, or unusual activity that prompt a refresh.","Set risk-tiered refresh frequencies so high-risk customers are revalidated more often.","Map the data sources and screening feeds that continuously update the customer profile.","Establish remediation workflows for stale or missing CDD data surfaced by triggers.","Document the pKYC model and demonstrate it meets ongoing due diligence requirements."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires keeping CDD information current through ongoing due diligence on the relationship."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing due diligence and keeping documents and data up to date."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based approach to calibrate review frequency to customer risk."}]}},{"id":"whistleblower","label":"🔔 Whistleblower Assessment","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Triages internal whistleblower disclosures relating to suspected money laundering or compliance failures. An MLRO uses it to assess, protect, and escalate confidential reports from staff.","steps":["Record the disclosure confidentially and protect the identity of the whistleblower.","Assess whether the report discloses a suspicion requiring an STR or a control failing.","Investigate the underlying facts independently of the implicated parties.","Ensure no retaliation against the reporter and document the protection measures applied.","Escalate substantiated findings to senior management and the FIU where reporting is triggered."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires internal reporting channels and protection of those reporting suspicions in good faith."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires internal controls and confidential employee reporting mechanisms."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires escalation of disclosed suspicions to the FIU when grounds exist."}]}},{"id":"trade-finance-rf","label":"🚢 Trade Finance Red Flags","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Screens trade finance transactions for documentary and pricing red flags indicative of trade-based money laundering. An MLRO uses it when reviewing letters of credit, invoices, and shipping documents for manipulation.","steps":["Compare invoice values against fair market pricing to detect over- and under-invoicing.","Cross-check shipping documents, bills of lading, and goods descriptions for inconsistencies or phantom shipments.","Flag routing through high-risk jurisdictions and use of dual-use or sanctioned goods.","Verify the commercial logic between the parties and the financial flows.","Escalate confirmed TBML indicators for STR filing and document the documentary analysis."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires monitoring of trade transactions for suspicious patterns and reporting."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Addresses information accompanying trade-related payments and value transfer."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious trade finance activity to the FIU."}]}},{"id":"sanctions-exposure-calc","label":"💥 Sanctions Exposure Calculator","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Quantifies a customer's or transaction's exposure to designated parties, sanctioned jurisdictions, and ownership above control thresholds. An MLRO uses it to size and document sanctions risk before clearing or blocking activity.","steps":["Screen all parties against UN, UAE Local Terrorist List, and applicable sanctions regimes.","Apply ownership and control aggregation to detect indirect designation above the relevant threshold.","Quantify exposed value, counterparties, and jurisdictional nexus for the relationship.","Freeze without delay and report where a confirmed match is identified.","Document the screening logic, thresholds applied, and the freeze or clearance decision."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires implementation of targeted financial sanctions and freezing without delay."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 6","note":"Requires targeted financial sanctions related to terrorism and terrorist financing."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7","note":"Requires targeted financial sanctions related to proliferation financing."}]}},{"id":"customer-lifecycle","label":"🔁 Customer Lifecycle","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Tracks AML controls across the full customer journey from onboarding through monitoring to exit. An MLRO uses it to ensure CDD, EDD, review, and offboarding obligations are met at each stage.","steps":["Confirm onboarding CDD, risk rating, and screening are complete before activation.","Verify ongoing monitoring and triggered or periodic reviews occur at the right cadence.","Track risk-rating changes and ensure EDD is applied when risk escalates.","Manage offboarding so that records are retained and any STR is filed before closure.","Maintain a complete audit trail of decisions across every lifecycle stage."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires CDD at onboarding, ongoing due diligence, and record retention through the relationship."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires CDD at the start and ongoing due diligence throughout the relationship."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 11","note":"Requires retention of records for the prescribed period including after relationship end."}]}},{"id":"pep-screening-enhance","label":"🎖 PEP Enhanced Screening","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Applies enhanced screening and due diligence to identified politically exposed persons, their families, and close associates. An MLRO uses it to manage the heightened risk of foreign, domestic, and international organisation PEPs.","steps":["Classify the PEP type as foreign, domestic, or international organisation and identify relatives and close associates.","Obtain senior management approval to establish or continue the relationship.","Establish source of wealth and source of funds with documentary evidence.","Apply intensified ongoing monitoring proportionate to the PEP risk level.","Re-screen periodically and on trigger events and document the EDD performed."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence, senior approval, and source of wealth checks for PEPs."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires enhanced measures for PEPs, family members, and close associates."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires risk-based enhanced measures for higher-risk customers such as PEPs."}]}},{"id":"aml-training-gap","label":"🎓 AML Training Gap","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Identifies gaps in AML/CFT training coverage, completion, and role relevance across the workforce. An MLRO uses it to ensure staff are trained appropriately to their risk exposure and to evidence this to regulators.","steps":["Map required training content to each role's ML/TF risk exposure.","Identify staff with overdue, missing, or outdated training completions.","Assess whether training covers current typologies, sanctions, and reporting obligations.","Schedule remedial and refresher training and track completion to closure.","Maintain records of training delivery and outcomes for supervisory inspection."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires ongoing AML/CFT training and awareness programmes for relevant staff."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires employee training as part of internal controls and programmes."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires risk-based allocation of resources including training to higher-risk roles."}]}},{"id":"beneficial-owner-verify","label":"🔍 UBO Verification","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Verifies the identity of ultimate beneficial owners behind a legal person and confirms the control path. An MLRO uses it to satisfy beneficial ownership obligations before onboarding or during review of corporate customers.","steps":["Build the ownership chain and identify every natural person holding ownership above the control threshold.","Identify any person exercising control through other means where no ownership threshold is met.","Verify UBO identity with reliable independent source documents.","Reconcile declared UBOs against registry and beneficial ownership filings.","Refuse or exit where the UBO cannot be reliably identified and verified, and record the basis."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires identification and verification of the ultimate beneficial owner of legal persons."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires accurate and current beneficial ownership information for legal persons."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires identifying and verifying the beneficial owner as part of CDD."}]}},{"id":"aml-kpi-dashboard","label":"📊 AML KPI Dashboard","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Aggregates AML/CFT performance metrics such as alert volumes, STR rates, backlog ageing, and screening hits. An MLRO uses it to monitor programme effectiveness and report to senior management and the board.","steps":["Select KPIs that measure detection, investigation, and reporting effectiveness.","Track alert and case backlogs, ageing, and time to disposition.","Monitor STR filing volumes, quality, and FIU feedback trends.","Flag thresholds breached and produce management and board reporting.","Use the metrics to recalibrate monitoring rules and resource allocation."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires effective internal AML/CFT systems and oversight by senior management."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires internal controls and an effective compliance management function."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires monitoring and review to ensure the risk-based programme remains effective."}]}},{"id":"trade-finance-risk","label":"🚢 Trade Finance Risk","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Risk-rates trade finance relationships and facilities across goods, counterparties, and corridors. An MLRO uses it to assess overall portfolio and client-level trade finance exposure beyond individual transaction red flags.","steps":["Profile the customer's trade activity by commodity type, volume, and trading partners.","Assess counterparty and jurisdiction risk including sanctioned and high-risk corridors.","Evaluate exposure to dual-use goods and sectors prone to trade-based laundering.","Assign a risk rating that drives the depth of documentary review and monitoring.","Document the rating rationale and set review triggers for the relationship."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires risk assessment and proportionate measures for trade-based activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based approach to rating and managing higher-risk activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Addresses information requirements for trade-related payments and value transfer."}]}},{"id":"insider-threat","label":"👤 Insider Threat","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Detects indicators that an employee may be facilitating money laundering, sanctions evasion, or leaking suspicions. An MLRO uses it to assess internal collusion, override patterns, and tipping-off risk among staff.","steps":["Identify staff with anomalous override, exception, or alert-suppression activity.","Review access to high-risk accounts and unexplained relationships with customers.","Look for indicators of tipping off or unauthorised disclosure of suspicions.","Coordinate confidentially with HR and security while preserving evidence.","Escalate substantiated insider threats and file an STR where laundering is suspected."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires internal controls, employee screening, and the tipping-off prohibition."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires employee integrity screening and internal control programmes."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 21","note":"Establishes the tipping-off prohibition relevant to insider disclosure risk."}]}},{"id":"crypto-tracing","label":"🔗 Crypto Tracing","group":"Core Tools","play":{"intro":"Traces virtual asset flows across wallets and blockchains to attribute funds and identify counterparties. An MLRO uses it to follow on-chain trails from a subject wallet to exchanges, services, or illicit sources.","steps":["Establish the subject wallet addresses and the relevant blockchains involved.","Trace inbound and outbound hops to clustered services, exchanges, and known entities.","Attribute exposure to illicit sources such as scams, ransomware, or sanctioned addresses.","Identify the VASP off-ramp and seek attribution to a natural or legal person.","Document the trace evidence to support STR filing or an information request response."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Applies AML/CFT obligations to virtual assets and requires tracing and reporting capability."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Requires AML/CFT controls and the travel rule for virtual assets and VASPs."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Requires originator and beneficiary information to accompany virtual asset transfers."}]}},{"id":"investment-fraud","label":"📈 Investment Fraud / Ponzi Screener","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"Screens schemes for hallmarks of investment fraud and Ponzi structures funded by new investor inflows. An MLRO uses it when an entity offers implausible returns or shows payouts sourced from incoming deposits rather than genuine activity.","steps":["Test whether returns paid to investors are funded by new investor inflows rather than real revenue.","Flag guaranteed or implausibly high returns, unregistered offerings, and pressure to recruit.","Map the flow of funds between investor deposits and payouts and operator withdrawals.","Check the operator's licensing status and the existence of any genuine underlying assets.","Escalate confirmed indicators for STR filing and consider account restriction."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Treats proceeds of fraud as a predicate offence requiring suspicious transaction reporting."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of suspicions including proceeds of investment fraud."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 3","note":"Requires criminalisation of money laundering across predicate offences including fraud."}]}},{"id":"bec-fraud","label":"📧 BEC / CEO Fraud Detector","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"Detects business email compromise and CEO impersonation fraud where payment instructions are manipulated. An MLRO uses it when a payment shows signs of social-engineering, altered beneficiary details, or urgent executive instruction.","steps":["Identify last-minute changes to beneficiary account or invoice details before payment.","Flag urgent, confidential instructions purporting to come from senior executives.","Check the beneficiary account for mule indicators and rapid onward dispersal.","Coordinate recall or freeze on the receiving account where funds remain.","File an STR and document the social-engineering indicators identified."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious transactions including proceeds of BEC fraud."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting of suspicious fraud-related transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Requires accurate originator and beneficiary information that helps detect altered payment instructions."}]}},{"id":"cash-courier","label":"💼 Bulk Cash Courier Detector","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"Detects bulk cash smuggling and structured cash courier activity linked to undeclared cross-border movement. An MLRO uses it when cash deposits or withdrawals suggest physical transport of currency to evade declaration.","steps":["Identify large or structured cash deposits inconsistent with the customer profile.","Cross-check against any cross-border currency declaration data and travel patterns.","Flag structuring just below reporting or declaration thresholds.","Assess links to known courier networks or high-risk corridors.","Escalate for STR filing and document the cash movement analysis."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Addresses cross-border transport of currency and reporting of suspicious cash activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 32","note":"Requires measures to detect physical cross-border transportation of currency."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious cash courier and structuring activity."}]}},{"id":"nft-wash","label":"🖼 NFT Wash Trade Detector","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"Detects wash trading of non-fungible tokens used to fabricate value or launder funds. An MLRO uses it when NFT trades show self-dealing between related wallets to move value under the guise of art sales.","steps":["Identify trades between related or self-controlled wallets that move an NFT back and forth.","Flag rapid round-trip sales and prices disconnected from any genuine market.","Trace the funds settling the trades to their source and ultimate destination.","Assess whether the activity layers proceeds or fabricates a value narrative.","Escalate confirmed wash trading for STR filing and document the wallet linkage."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Extends AML/CFT obligations to virtual assets and requires reporting of suspicious activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Applies AML/CFT controls to virtual assets including NFTs functioning as such."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious wash-trading and layering activity."}]}},{"id":"carbon-fraud","label":"🌿 Carbon Credit Fraud Analyser","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"Analyses carbon credit trading for fraud typologies such as VAT carousel, double-counting, and phantom credits. An MLRO uses it when a customer trades emissions allowances with patterns suggesting fraud or laundering.","steps":["Check for missing-trader and carousel patterns in rapid cross-border credit trades.","Verify that credits are genuine, registered, and not double-counted or retired elsewhere.","Assess sudden high-volume trading by newly onboarded or unrelated counterparties.","Trace the financial settlement flows against the credit movements.","Escalate confirmed indicators for STR filing and document the analysis."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Treats proceeds of fraud and tax crimes as predicates requiring suspicious reporting."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of suspicions including carbon credit and VAT fraud proceeds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 3","note":"Requires money laundering to cover predicate offences such as fraud and tax evasion."}]}},{"id":"darknet-exposure","label":"🕶 Darknet Market Exposure","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"Assesses customer exposure to darknet marketplaces and illicit online vendors through virtual asset flows. An MLRO uses it when on-chain analysis links a customer's funds to darknet market addresses.","steps":["Trace virtual asset inflows and outflows for links to known darknet market clusters.","Quantify the proportion and value of activity exposed to illicit marketplaces.","Assess use of mixers or privacy tooling layered over the darknet exposure.","Request source of funds and decline where illicit provenance is indicated.","Escalate confirmed darknet exposure for STR filing and document the on-chain evidence."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Applies AML/CFT obligations to virtual assets and requires reporting of illicit-linked activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Requires AML/CFT controls for virtual assets and VASPs addressing illicit market exposure."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of suspicions linked to darknet and illicit marketplace activity."}]}},{"id":"drug-trafficking","label":"💊 Drug Trafficking Financial Indicators","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool screens customer and transaction data for the financial fingerprints of narcotics trafficking, such as structured cash deposits and funnel-account flows. An MLRO runs it when a case shows cash-intensive activity inconsistent with the customer's stated profile.","steps":["Map deposit patterns for rapid structuring just under cash reporting thresholds and clustered deposits across multiple branches or ATMs.","Identify funnel accounts where cash is deposited in one region and withdrawn or wired to a distant high-risk source or transit jurisdiction.","Cross-check counterparties and routes against known drug-source and transit geographies and any DNFBP cash purchases.","Trace layering through cash-intensive front businesses, money services, and trade payments to test for placement of narcotics proceeds.","Document the predicate-offence nexus and escalate a suspicious transaction report to the UAE FIU via goAML where indicators persist.","Apply enhanced due diligence or exit the relationship and preserve all evidence for law enforcement."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Establishes drug trafficking as a predicate offence and mandates STR filing and record keeping for suspected proceeds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting to the FIU of funds suspected to be proceeds of drug trafficking and related crime."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Sets out CDD, suspicious transaction reporting and recordkeeping obligations applied to narcotics-linked activity."}]}},{"id":"human-trafficking-fin","label":"🚨 Human Trafficking Financial Patterns","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool detects financial patterns consistent with human trafficking and forced labour, such as third-party controlled accounts and shared-address victim clusters. An MLRO uses it when payroll, remittance, or account-control anomalies suggest exploitation.","steps":["Flag accounts where one controller operates multiple accounts for different individuals sharing an address, device, or phone number.","Detect victim-pattern indicators such as deposits inconsistent with declared employment and recurring withdrawals near transport hubs or recruitment centres.","Review cross-border remittances to and from source jurisdictions for labour or sexual exploitation routes.","Examine payments to advertising platforms, hotels, and travel services that align with known trafficking typologies.","Escalate a suspicious transaction report to the UAE FIU and, where life is at risk, coordinate with competent authorities without tipping off.","Preserve KYC, device, and transaction evidence and apply enhanced monitoring on linked accounts."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Treats human trafficking as a predicate offence requiring detection, STR reporting and cooperation with authorities."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting to the FIU where funds are suspected to relate to human trafficking proceeds."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Imposes CDD, beneficial ownership and STR duties relevant to identifying trafficking-controlled accounts."}]}},{"id":"arms-trafficking","label":"⚔️ Arms / Weapons Trafficking Indicators","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool surfaces indicators of illicit arms and dual-use goods trafficking, including suspect trade finance and shipments to embargoed destinations. An MLRO runs it on trade and correspondent flows touching defence or dual-use sectors.","steps":["Screen counterparties, vessels, and end users against arms-embargo and proliferation-financing watchlists and UN sanctions lists.","Examine trade-finance documents for misdescribed goods, dual-use items, and routing through transshipment hubs that conceal end use.","Check for price and quantity anomalies, vague goods descriptions, and end-user certificates that do not match the buyer profile.","Trace payment structures using front companies, brokers, or third-party intermediaries to obscure the true end user.","Escalate a suspicious transaction report and, where sanctions or proliferation links exist, freeze and notify competent authorities.","Retain shipping, settlement, and screening records for the statutory retention period."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Covers illicit arms trafficking and proliferation financing as predicate concerns requiring reporting and freezing."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7","note":"Requires targeted financial sanctions to prevent proliferation financing of weapons of mass destruction."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020","note":"Implements UN Security Council resolutions on targeted financial sanctions and proliferation financing in the UAE."}]}},{"id":"corruption-bribery","label":"🤝 Corruption & Bribery Analyser","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool analyses relationships and flows for corruption and bribery indicators, including PEP-linked payments and consultancy or facilitation fees. An MLRO uses it where a customer or counterparty has government links or unexplained advisory payments.","steps":["Screen all parties for PEP status, close associates, and family members, and apply enhanced due diligence to identified PEPs.","Examine consultancy, success-fee, and facilitation payments lacking commercial substance or supporting deliverables.","Trace funds to or from public procurement, state-owned entities, or government contracts for kickback patterns.","Test source of wealth and source of funds for PEPs against declared income and asset profiles.","Escalate a suspicious transaction report to the UAE FIU where bribery indicators are unresolved.","Document the PEP risk rating, EDD outcome, and senior management approval to continue the relationship."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Defines corruption and bribery as predicate offences and underpins PEP and STR obligations."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence, source of wealth checks and senior approval for politically exposed persons."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Sets the PEP definition and EDD measures applied to corruption and bribery risk."}]}},{"id":"tax-evasion-fin","label":"💸 Tax Evasion Financial Indicators","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool identifies financial indicators of tax evasion, such as undisclosed offshore structures and invoice manipulation. An MLRO uses it when a customer's flows or structures suggest concealment of taxable income or assets.","steps":["Map ownership through offshore vehicles, nominee shareholders, and opaque trusts that mask beneficial ownership.","Detect under- or over-invoicing, round-tripping, and circular payments designed to shift or hide profits.","Compare declared income and tax residency against actual cash flows and lifestyle indicators.","Review unexplained transfers to low-tax or secrecy jurisdictions and inconsistent CRS or FATCA self-certifications.","Escalate a suspicious transaction report to the UAE FIU where tax-evasion proceeds are suspected.","Preserve beneficial ownership and structuring records to support any cross-border information exchange."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Recognises tax crimes as predicate offences for money laundering and requires reporting of suspected proceeds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 3","note":"Requires that tax crimes be designated predicate offences for the money laundering offence."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires transparency of beneficial ownership of legal persons to counter concealment of taxable assets."}]}},{"id":"cybercrime-proceeds","label":"💻 Cybercrime Proceeds Analyser","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool traces the laundering of cybercrime proceeds, including business email compromise, phishing, and account takeover funds. An MLRO uses it when inbound funds or rapid pass-through activity matches fraud-victim patterns.","steps":["Identify mule-account behaviour such as rapid inbound credits followed by immediate fan-out to multiple beneficiaries or crypto exchanges.","Match beneficiary details against business email compromise and invoice-redirection fraud indicators.","Trace funds across newly opened accounts, prepaid instruments, and virtual asset off-ramps.","Coordinate with the originating institution on recall or hold requests where victim funds are identified.","Escalate a suspicious transaction report to the UAE FIU and freeze suspected proceeds pending review.","Preserve device, IP, and transaction logs to support law enforcement and recovery efforts."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Establishes cybercrime as a predicate offence and requires STR filing and freezing of suspected proceeds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt FIU reporting where funds are suspected to be proceeds of cyber-enabled crime."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Requires originator and beneficiary information on wire transfers to trace mule and fraud flows."}]}},{"id":"market-manipulation","label":"📊 Market Manipulation Detector","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool detects market manipulation and insider dealing patterns such as wash trading, spoofing, and pre-announcement trading. An MLRO uses it for securities and trading clients showing abnormal order or settlement behaviour.","steps":["Detect wash trades, matched orders, and circular trading between related accounts that create artificial volume.","Flag spoofing and layering where large orders are placed and cancelled to move prices.","Identify trading clustered ahead of price-sensitive announcements indicating possible insider information.","Correlate beneficial ownership and connected accounts to expose coordinated manipulation rings.","Escalate a suspicious transaction report to the UAE FIU and notify the relevant securities regulator.","Retain order, execution, and communication records to support market abuse investigation."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Treats market manipulation and insider dealing as predicate offences requiring suspicious activity reporting."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting to the FIU where funds are suspected proceeds of market abuse."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Applies CDD, monitoring and STR duties to securities and capital-market activity."}]}},{"id":"insurance-fraud","label":"🛡 Insurance Fraud Detector","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool flags insurance fraud and policy-based laundering, including early surrender, inflated claims, and overpayment refunds. An MLRO at an insurer or broker uses it across policy lifecycle and claims data.","steps":["Detect early policy surrender shortly after large premium payments that returns laundered funds as clean refunds.","Flag overpayment of premiums followed by refund requests to third parties or different jurisdictions.","Review staged or inflated claims, duplicate claims, and beneficiaries unrelated to the policyholder.","Check single-premium and investment-linked policies funded by cash or unclear source of funds.","Escalate a suspicious transaction report to the UAE FIU where laundering through the policy is suspected.","Document underwriting, claims, and source-of-funds evidence for the retention period."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Covers fraud as a predicate offence and applies AML obligations to insurance providers."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires FIU reporting of funds suspected to be proceeds of insurance fraud or laundering."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Defines CDD and reporting obligations for life insurance and investment-linked products."}]}},{"id":"mortgage-fraud","label":"🏠 Mortgage Fraud Analyser","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool analyses real estate financing for mortgage fraud and property-based laundering, such as inflated valuations and straw buyers. An MLRO uses it on lending and real-estate flows in the UAE property sector.","steps":["Compare declared income, employment, and source of funds against loan affordability and supporting documents.","Detect inflated or under-stated valuations and rapid resale flips that disguise value transfer.","Identify straw buyers, nominee purchasers, and undisclosed beneficial owners behind property transactions.","Trace deposits and settlement funds for cash injections or third-party payments inconsistent with the buyer profile.","Escalate a suspicious transaction report to the UAE FIU where mortgage fraud or laundering is suspected.","Retain valuation, title, and source-of-funds records and coordinate with real estate DNFBP reporting."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Establishes fraud as a predicate offence and brings real estate financing within AML scope."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Extends CDD obligations to real estate agents involved in property transactions."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Applies CDD, beneficial ownership and STR requirements to real estate dealings."}]}},{"id":"identity-theft","label":"🪪 Identity Theft Indicators","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool surfaces identity theft and synthetic identity indicators at onboarding and during account use, such as reused documents and mismatched biometrics. An MLRO uses it when KYC integrity or account-takeover risk is in question.","steps":["Detect reused or shared identity documents, addresses, phones, and devices across multiple customer profiles.","Flag synthetic identities combining real and fabricated data and mismatches between document, selfie, and biometric checks.","Identify sudden changes to contact details, beneficiaries, or payees consistent with account takeover.","Cross-check submitted documents against issuing-authority validation and known fraud document templates.","Escalate a suspicious transaction report and suspend or re-verify accounts where identity compromise is confirmed.","Preserve onboarding evidence and notify affected customers and authorities as required."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires reliable customer identification and treats identity-related fraud as a predicate offence."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires customer due diligence and verification using reliable, independent source documents and data."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Specifies identity verification and recordkeeping standards for customer onboarding."}]}},{"id":"elder-fraud","label":"👴 Elder Financial Abuse","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool detects financial exploitation of elderly or vulnerable customers, including undue third-party control and scam-driven transfers. An MLRO uses it when an elderly customer's account shows out-of-pattern or coerced activity.","steps":["Flag sudden changes in beneficiaries, power of attorney, or new account signatories on elderly customers' accounts.","Detect unusual large withdrawals, transfers to new payees, or activity inconsistent with established behaviour.","Identify romance, lottery, and tech-support scam patterns and repeated transfers to high-risk destinations.","Review for third-party control where another person directs transactions or accompanies the customer in branch.","Escalate a suspicious transaction report and, where coercion is suspected, pause transactions and engage safeguarding contacts.","Document interactions, source of instruction, and evidence supporting the suspicion."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Treats fraud and exploitation proceeds as reportable and requires ongoing transaction monitoring."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of transactions suspected to involve proceeds of fraud against vulnerable persons."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Requires ongoing monitoring to detect transactions inconsistent with the customer profile."}]}},{"id":"ransomware-response","label":"🔒 Ransomware Payment Response","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool guides the AML response when a ransomware extortion payment is suspected, including sanctions screening of wallets and reporting. An MLRO uses it where a customer payment may fund a ransomware actor.","steps":["Screen the destination virtual asset wallet and any intermediaries against sanctions lists and known ransomware addresses.","Assess whether facilitating the payment risks breaching targeted financial sanctions before any transfer settles.","Trace the payment path across exchanges and mixers and document the extortion context and victim details.","Escalate a suspicious transaction report to the UAE FIU and notify cyber and sanctions authorities promptly.","Freeze or block where a sanctioned party is involved and do not facilitate prohibited payments.","Preserve blockchain, communication, and decision records for regulators and law enforcement."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires reporting and freezing where payments may constitute or fund predicate crime or sanctioned activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Applies AML and sanctions controls to virtual assets and virtual asset service providers."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 74 of 2020","note":"Implements targeted financial sanctions obligations triggered when a sanctioned party is involved."}]}},{"id":"fraud-network","label":"🕸 Fraud Network Mapper","group":"Financial Crime Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool maps connected accounts, devices, and counterparties to expose organised fraud and money-mule networks. An MLRO uses it to link individual alerts into a coordinated network for a consolidated report.","steps":["Build a link graph connecting shared addresses, devices, IPs, phone numbers, and common beneficiaries.","Identify mule clusters, controllers, and hub accounts that aggregate or redistribute illicit funds.","Overlay transaction flows to reveal layering paths and central nodes within the network.","Prioritise nodes by exposure value and connection density to focus investigation.","Escalate a consolidated suspicious transaction report to the UAE FIU covering all linked entities.","Apply coordinated account restrictions and retain the network analysis as evidence."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires identification and reporting of linked suspicious activity and supports network-level STRs."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires FIU reporting of suspicious transactions, including coordinated network activity."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Requires ongoing monitoring and analysis to detect connected suspicious patterns."}]}},{"id":"fatf-evaluation-prep","label":"🎯 FATF Mutual Evaluation Prep","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool prepares an institution for a FATF mutual evaluation by assessing technical compliance and effectiveness against the immediate outcomes. An MLRO uses it ahead of an assessment or national review cycle.","steps":["Map the institution's controls against the 40 FATF Recommendations to identify technical compliance gaps.","Assess effectiveness against the immediate outcomes, focusing on risk understanding, supervision, and reporting quality.","Compile evidence of risk assessments, STR statistics, training records, and remediation actions for assessors.","Run mock interviews for senior management and the MLRO on the institution's risk-based approach.","Prioritise and remediate identified gaps before the on-site visit and track to closure.","Maintain a defensible evidence file aligned to each immediate outcome."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Forms the national legal basis assessed for technical compliance during the evaluation."},{"ref":"FATF 40 Recommendations and Methodology","note":"Provides the technical compliance and effectiveness criteria against which the assessment is scored."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based approach and national and institutional risk assessments central to effectiveness review."}]}},{"id":"vara-compliance","label":"🔐 VARA AML Compliance Checker","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool checks a virtual asset service provider's AML controls against Dubai's VARA regime. An MLRO at a licensed VASP uses it to confirm compliance with VARA's rulebooks and travel rule duties.","steps":["Confirm VARA licensing scope and that AML controls match the activities authorised under the rulebooks.","Verify travel rule implementation for originator and beneficiary data on virtual asset transfers.","Test wallet screening, blockchain analytics, and sanctions controls for inbound and outbound transactions.","Review VASP-specific CDD, including self-hosted wallet handling and risk scoring of counterparties.","Confirm STR reporting to the UAE FIU and regulatory reporting to VARA are operational.","Document findings against each VARA rulebook requirement and remediate gaps."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides the federal AML basis that VARA-licensed VASPs must satisfy alongside VARA rules."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Sets the AML and travel rule standard for virtual assets and VASPs implemented by VARA."},{"ref":"VARA Compliance and Risk Management Rulebook","note":"Sets Dubai-specific AML, CDD and reporting obligations for licensed virtual asset service providers."}]}},{"id":"cbuae-exam","label":"🏦 CBUAE Examination Readiness","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses readiness for a Central Bank of the UAE AML examination across governance, monitoring, and reporting. An MLRO at a CBUAE-licensed institution uses it before or after notification of an on-site review.","steps":["Verify the enterprise AML risk assessment is current and approved and that policies reflect CBUAE guidance.","Confirm goAML registration, STR and SAR timeliness, and quality of filed reports.","Test transaction monitoring tuning, alert backlogs, and case disposition documentation.","Review sanctions screening coverage, PEP handling, and the MLRO's independence and reporting line to the board.","Prepare evidence packs and walkthroughs for examiners and rehearse responses to likely findings.","Log gaps in a remediation tracker with owners and deadlines."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Sets the AML obligations CBUAE examiners test, including reporting, CDD and recordkeeping."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 26","note":"Requires risk-based supervision of financial institutions, the basis for CBUAE examinations."},{"ref":"CBUAE AML/CFT Guidance for Licensed Financial Institutions","note":"Sets supervisory expectations on governance, monitoring and reporting tested during examinations."}]}},{"id":"eu-amla","label":"🇪🇺 EU AMLA Requirements Analyser","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool analyses obligations under the EU's single AML rulebook and the AML Authority (AMLA) for institutions with EU exposure. An MLRO uses it to assess cross-border alignment between UAE and EU requirements.","steps":["Identify EU nexus through branches, subsidiaries, or EU customers that bring AMLA and the AML Regulation into scope.","Map group AML policies against the EU single rulebook's harmonised CDD and beneficial ownership rules.","Assess whether the entity may fall under AMLA direct supervision based on cross-border risk and size.","Reconcile UAE and EU requirements and apply the stricter standard where they diverge.","Document the gap analysis and update group policy and reporting lines accordingly.","Track AMLA technical standards and guidance for ongoing changes."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Anchors the UAE obligations reconciled against EU requirements for group consistency."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires group-wide AML programmes and information sharing across cross-border entities."},{"ref":"EU AML Regulation and AMLA Regulation","note":"Establish the single rulebook and the AML Authority's supervisory powers over in-scope entities."}]}},{"id":"mica-compliance","label":"💎 MiCA Crypto Asset Compliance","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool checks crypto-asset service provider obligations under the EU Markets in Crypto-Assets framework alongside AML duties. An MLRO uses it where a virtual asset business serves or operates into the EU.","steps":["Determine whether activities require CASP authorisation and which MiCA token categories apply.","Verify whitepaper, governance, and conduct obligations are met for issued or serviced tokens.","Confirm AML controls and the EU travel rule apply to crypto-asset transfers in parallel with MiCA.","Assess stablecoin and asset-referenced token reserve and disclosure requirements where relevant.","Reconcile MiCA duties with UAE and VARA obligations and apply the stricter control.","Document the compliance mapping and remediate gaps before EU market access."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides the UAE AML baseline applied alongside MiCA for crypto-asset businesses."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Sets the global AML and travel rule standard for virtual assets that complements MiCA."},{"ref":"EU Markets in Crypto-Assets Regulation (MiCA)","note":"Sets EU authorisation, conduct and disclosure rules for crypto-asset service providers and token issuers."}]}},{"id":"dora-resilience","label":"🖥 DORA Operational Resilience","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses digital operational resilience for AML-critical systems under the EU DORA framework. An MLRO uses it to ensure screening, monitoring, and reporting platforms remain resilient to ICT disruption.","steps":["Inventory ICT systems supporting screening, monitoring, and STR filing and rate their criticality.","Assess ICT risk management, incident classification, and reporting timelines for major incidents.","Review third-party and cloud provider dependencies and contractual resilience requirements.","Confirm resilience testing, including threat-led penetration testing where applicable, is scheduled.","Ensure AML reporting can continue or recover within tolerance during an ICT outage.","Document the resilience assessment and remediate concentration and continuity gaps."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires AML systems and recordkeeping to remain available and reliable, supported by resilience controls."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Requires assessment and mitigation of risks from new technologies underpinning AML operations."},{"ref":"EU Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA)","note":"Sets ICT risk management, incident reporting and resilience testing duties for financial entities."}]}},{"id":"aml-framework-gap","label":"🔍 AML Framework Gap Analyser","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool performs a structured gap analysis of an institution's AML/CFT framework against legal and FATF requirements. An MLRO uses it during program reviews or before audits to find and prioritise weaknesses.","steps":["Inventory current policies, procedures, and controls across CDD, monitoring, screening, and reporting.","Map each control to the corresponding UAE legal obligation and FATF Recommendation.","Identify missing, outdated, or ineffective controls and rate residual risk for each gap.","Benchmark governance, MLRO resourcing, and training against regulatory expectations.","Produce a prioritised gap register with owners, severity, and target dates.","Feed material gaps into the remediation plan and report to senior management."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Defines the AML obligations the framework is measured against during gap analysis."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based AML programme with controls proportionate to assessed risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires internal controls, compliance management and audit covered by the gap review."}]}},{"id":"regulatory-breach-notice","label":"📢 Regulatory Breach Notification","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool guides the assessment and notification of an AML compliance breach to the relevant UAE regulator. An MLRO uses it when a control failure or reporting lapse may be notifiable.","steps":["Determine whether the incident meets the materiality and notification threshold for the supervisor.","Establish the breach timeline, root cause, scope of customers or transactions affected, and any STR impact.","Assess immediate containment, including freezing affected accounts and stopping ongoing harm.","Draft the notification with required facts and submit to the regulator within the prescribed deadline.","Record the breach, decision rationale, and remediation actions in the compliance register.","Track corrective actions to closure and report progress to the supervisor."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Imposes duties to comply with AML obligations and supports supervisory notification of failures."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 35","note":"Requires effective, proportionate sanctions for AML breaches, underscoring timely notification."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Sets compliance and reporting obligations whose breach triggers supervisory notification."}]}},{"id":"remediation-planner","label":"🔧 Regulatory Remediation Planner","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool builds a structured remediation plan to close AML deficiencies identified by audits, examinations, or gap analyses. An MLRO uses it to convert findings into tracked, accountable actions.","steps":["Consolidate findings from audits, examinations, and gap analyses into a single remediation backlog.","Prioritise each finding by risk severity, regulatory exposure, and customer impact.","Assign owners, define corrective actions, and set realistic milestones for each item.","Define validation criteria and evidence required to confirm each remediation is effective.","Track progress, escalate slippage, and report status to senior management and the board.","Maintain an auditable record of closure evidence for supervisors."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires institutions to maintain effective AML controls, which remediation restores."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires controls proportionate to risk, which remediation actions reinforce."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires internal controls and independent audit whose findings feed the remediation plan."}]}},{"id":"mou-treaty","label":"🤝 MOU / Treaty Obligation Checker","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool checks obligations arising from memoranda of understanding and mutual legal assistance treaties relevant to AML cooperation. An MLRO uses it when handling cross-border information requests or sharing.","steps":["Identify the applicable MOU or treaty governing the requesting authority and jurisdiction.","Confirm the legal gateway, permitted scope, and confidentiality conditions for sharing information.","Verify the request's authenticity and that it routes through the proper FIU or competent authority channel.","Assess data protection and tipping-off constraints before disclosing customer information.","Document the legal basis, approval, and information shared for audit.","Coordinate the response with legal counsel and the FIU where required."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Authorises international cooperation and information exchange on AML matters."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 40","note":"Requires the widest range of international cooperation between competent authorities."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 36","note":"Requires implementation of relevant international conventions and mutual legal assistance instruments."}]}},{"id":"finma-compliance","label":"🇨🇭 FINMA AML Compliance (Swiss)","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool checks AML obligations under the Swiss FINMA regime for institutions with Swiss operations or counterparties. An MLRO uses it to align group controls with Swiss requirements.","steps":["Identify the Swiss nexus and whether FINMA-supervised activity or an SRO membership applies.","Map controls to the Swiss AML Act and FINMA Anti-Money Laundering Ordinance requirements.","Verify beneficial ownership identification and the documentation standards Swiss rules require.","Confirm reporting to the Swiss Money Laundering Reporting Office where obligations arise.","Reconcile Swiss and UAE duties and apply the stricter standard across the group.","Document the compliance mapping and remediate divergences."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides the UAE baseline reconciled against Swiss obligations for group consistency."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Sets the customer due diligence standard underlying Swiss CDD requirements."},{"ref":"Swiss Anti-Money Laundering Act and FINMA AML Ordinance","note":"Set Switzerland-specific CDD, beneficial ownership and reporting obligations for financial intermediaries."}]}},{"id":"mas-compliance","label":"🇸🇬 MAS AML Notice Compliance","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool checks compliance with the Monetary Authority of Singapore AML/CFT Notices for institutions operating into Singapore. An MLRO uses it to align controls with the relevant MAS Notice.","steps":["Identify the applicable MAS Notice for the institution's licence class and activities.","Map CDD, enhanced due diligence, and ongoing monitoring controls to the Notice requirements.","Verify sanctions and PEP screening meet MAS expectations and timelines.","Confirm suspicious transaction reporting to the Singapore FIU where obligations arise.","Reconcile MAS and UAE requirements and apply the stricter standard across the group.","Document the mapping and remediate identified gaps."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides the UAE baseline reconciled against MAS Notice obligations for group consistency."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Sets the CDD standard underpinning the MAS Notice customer due diligence requirements."},{"ref":"MAS AML/CFT Notices","note":"Set Singapore-specific CDD, screening and reporting obligations for licensed financial institutions."}]}},{"id":"fca-compliance","label":"🇬🇧 FCA ML Regs Compliance","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool checks AML compliance under the UK Money Laundering Regulations and FCA expectations for institutions with UK exposure. An MLRO uses it to align group controls with UK requirements.","steps":["Identify the UK nexus and confirm whether the entity is within scope of the Money Laundering Regulations.","Map risk assessment, CDD, and EDD controls to the Regulations and FCA financial crime guidance.","Verify SAR reporting to the UK FIU and the nominated officer arrangements are in place.","Confirm sanctions and PEP controls meet UK requirements and OFSI expectations.","Reconcile UK and UAE duties and apply the stricter standard across the group.","Document the mapping and track remediation of gaps."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides the UAE baseline reconciled against UK obligations for group consistency."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Sets the CDD standard underpinning the UK Money Laundering Regulations."},{"ref":"UK Money Laundering Regulations 2017","note":"Set UK-specific risk assessment, CDD and reporting obligations supervised by the FCA."}]}},{"id":"bafin-compliance","label":"🇩🇪 BaFin AML Compliance","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool checks AML obligations under the German Money Laundering Act and BaFin supervision for institutions with German exposure. An MLRO uses it to align group controls with German requirements.","steps":["Identify the German nexus and BaFin-supervised activity within scope of the Geldwaschegesetz.","Map CDD, beneficial ownership, and transparency register obligations to the Act.","Verify suspicious activity reporting to the German FIU and the AML officer appointment.","Confirm sanctions and PEP screening meet BaFin expectations.","Reconcile German and UAE duties and apply the stricter standard across the group.","Document the mapping and remediate divergences."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides the UAE baseline reconciled against German obligations for group consistency."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires beneficial ownership transparency reflected in Germany's transparency register."},{"ref":"German Money Laundering Act (Geldwaschegesetz)","note":"Sets Germany-specific CDD, transparency and reporting obligations supervised by BaFin."}]}},{"id":"basel-aml-index","label":"🌍 Basel AML Index Risk Assessor","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses country money-laundering risk using the Basel AML Index and related indicators. An MLRO uses it to set jurisdiction risk ratings feeding the customer and country risk model.","steps":["Retrieve the current Basel AML Index scores for relevant jurisdictions.","Combine the score with FATF listings, sanctions exposure, and corruption indices for a composite rating.","Map each jurisdiction to the institution's country-risk tiers and CDD intensity.","Trigger enhanced due diligence and senior review for customers tied to high-risk jurisdictions.","Document the methodology and refresh ratings on the Index's annual cycle.","Feed updated country risk into the enterprise risk assessment."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires a risk-based approach in which country risk informs CDD intensity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 19","note":"Requires enhanced measures for higher-risk countries identified by risk indicators."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires risk assessment incorporating jurisdiction risk into the risk-based approach."}]}},{"id":"egmont-fiu","label":"📬 Egmont FIU Information Request","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool supports handling Egmont Group FIU-to-FIU information requests and the institution's role in responding. An MLRO uses it when the UAE FIU seeks information for or from a foreign FIU.","steps":["Confirm the request originates through the UAE FIU and the Egmont secure channel.","Verify the legal basis, scope, and the Egmont Principles on use and confidentiality of shared information.","Gather the requested customer and transaction information accurately and completely.","Apply tipping-off and data protection safeguards before any disclosure.","Submit the response to the FIU within the requested timeframe and record the exchange.","Retain the request, approval, and response for audit."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Authorises the FIU to exchange information internationally and obligates institutions to support requests."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 40","note":"Requires effective FIU-to-FIU cooperation through channels such as the Egmont Group."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 29","note":"Defines FIU functions including international information exchange underpinning Egmont requests."}]}},{"id":"wolfsberg-principles","label":"📋 Wolfsberg Principles Checker","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool benchmarks an institution's correspondent banking and AML controls against the Wolfsberg Group principles and questionnaire. An MLRO uses it for correspondent due diligence and program benchmarking.","steps":["Complete or review the Wolfsberg Correspondent Banking Due Diligence Questionnaire for the relationship.","Benchmark CDD, KYC, and monitoring controls against the relevant Wolfsberg principles.","Assess respondent banks for nesting, downstream clearing, and shell-bank exposure.","Identify gaps between current practice and Wolfsberg expectations and rate residual risk.","Apply enhanced due diligence and senior approval for higher-risk correspondent relationships.","Document the assessment and refresh on the relationship review cycle."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires correspondent and customer due diligence consistent with Wolfsberg benchmarks."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13","note":"Requires correspondent banking due diligence and prohibits relationships with shell banks."},{"ref":"Wolfsberg Group Principles and CBDDQ","note":"Provide the industry benchmark for correspondent banking and AML due diligence standards."}]}},{"id":"palermo-convention","label":"⚖️ Palermo Convention Analysis","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool analyses obligations under the UN Convention against Transnational Organized Crime (Palermo) as they apply to AML controls. An MLRO uses it to link organised-crime predicate offences to reporting duties.","steps":["Identify which Palermo predicate offences, including organised crime and trafficking, apply to the case.","Confirm the UAE's implementation of Palermo obligations through its AML legal framework.","Assess whether the activity supports mutual legal assistance or extradition cooperation.","Link the predicate analysis to the money laundering offence and STR reporting decision.","Escalate a suspicious transaction report to the UAE FIU where organised-crime proceeds are suspected.","Document the legal analysis and evidence for cross-border cooperation."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Implements predicate offences and cooperation duties consistent with the Palermo Convention."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 36","note":"Requires ratification and implementation of the Palermo Convention."},{"ref":"UN Convention against Transnational Organized Crime (Palermo)","note":"Defines transnational organised-crime offences and mutual legal assistance obligations."}]}},{"id":"vienna-convention","label":"🌐 Vienna Convention Analysis","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool maps a drug-trafficking or proceeds-of-crime fact pattern against the 1988 Vienna Convention predicate offences and confiscation obligations. An MLRO uses it to confirm that suspected narcotics-linked funds qualify as a money laundering predicate before escalating.","steps":["Identify the underlying conduct and test whether it falls within the Vienna Convention's narcotics production, trafficking, or proceeds offences.","Confirm the predicate is recognised under UAE law and that laundering its proceeds is criminalised.","Document the cross-border element and any jurisdictions involved to support mutual legal assistance considerations.","Assess whether confiscation, freezing, or seizure triggers apply to the identified funds or assets.","Record the analysis in the case file and escalate to STR/SAR filing where laundering of narcotics proceeds is suspected."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Establishes that laundering proceeds of predicate offences, including drug trafficking, is criminalised and reportable in the UAE."},{"ref":"1988 UN Vienna Convention (illicit drug trafficking)","note":"Defines narcotics trafficking predicate offences and confiscation duties this tool screens against."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 3","note":"Requires criminalising money laundering on the widest range of predicate offences, including drug-related crime."}]}},{"id":"fatf-grey-impact","label":"⚠️ FATF Grey/Black List Impact","group":"Regulatory & Compliance","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses the compliance and exposure impact when a counterparty, transaction, or correspondent is linked to a FATF grey-listed (increased monitoring) or black-listed (call for action) jurisdiction. The MLRO uses it to calibrate enhanced due diligence and possible countermeasures.","steps":["Confirm the jurisdiction's current FATF status against the latest grey and black list statements.","Apply enhanced due diligence proportionate to the listing and document the rationale.","For black-listed (call for action) jurisdictions, evaluate mandated countermeasures or restrictions on the relationship.","Review exposure across the portfolio to that jurisdiction and flag aggregate risk concentration.","Record listing-driven controls in the customer risk assessment and update on each FATF plenary cycle."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires applying enhanced measures to higher-risk countries identified by competent authorities."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 19","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence and, where called for, countermeasures against higher-risk jurisdictions."},{"ref":"FATF High-Risk Jurisdictions statements","note":"Source of grey and black list designations this tool screens against."}]}},{"id":"digital-identity","label":"🔐 Digital Identity Verifier","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool validates a customer's digital identity credentials, such as UAE Pass or government eID, against authoritative sources during onboarding or remote verification. The MLRO uses it to confirm identity assurance levels meet CDD standards.","steps":["Capture the digital identity credential and confirm it derives from a trusted government or accredited provider source.","Verify the credential is live, unexpired, and bound to the presenting customer.","Match the asserted identity attributes against independent and reliable data.","Record the identity assurance level achieved and any residual verification gaps.","Escalate to manual or enhanced verification where the credential cannot be authenticated."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires verifying customer identity using reliable, independent source documents, data, or information."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Sets the customer due diligence and identity verification standard underpinning digital identity checks."},{"ref":"FATF Guidance on Digital Identity","note":"Frames assurance-level expectations for using digital ID systems in CDD."}]}},{"id":"synthetic-identity","label":"🤖 Synthetic Identity Detector","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool detects fabricated or blended identities that combine real and fictitious data to create a fraudulent customer. The MLRO uses it to stop synthetic identities being used to open accounts and launder funds.","steps":["Cross-check identity attributes (name, date of birth, ID number, address) for inconsistent or impossible combinations.","Test whether the identity has a credible verifiable history rather than a thin or recently fabricated footprint.","Flag reused identifiers, mismatched issuance dates, and links to known fraud rings.","Apply enhanced verification before account activation where synthetic indicators are present.","Document findings and file a suspicious transaction report where laundering or fraud is suspected."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires verifying identity from reliable independent sources and reporting suspicion of laundering or fraud."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Mandates robust customer identification and verification that synthetic identities aim to defeat."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting of suspicious transactions, including those using fabricated identities."}]}},{"id":"deepfake-kyc","label":"🎭 Deepfake / AI Doc Detector","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool screens KYC submissions for AI-generated or manipulated documents, photos, and liveness videos used to defeat remote onboarding. The MLRO uses it to validate the authenticity of evidence presented during eKYC.","steps":["Run document images and selfies through manipulation and deepfake detection checks.","Verify liveness and that biometric capture corresponds to a genuine live person.","Compare submitted documents against issuer templates and security features for tampering.","Quarantine onboarding where synthetic or altered media is detected and require alternative verification.","Record the authenticity assessment and escalate suspected fraud for STR consideration."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires identity verification from reliable, independent and genuine source documents."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires authentic identification evidence, which deepfake and altered documents undermine."},{"ref":"FATF Guidance on Digital Identity","note":"Addresses anti-spoofing and liveness assurance for remote identity proofing."}]}},{"id":"adverse-media-deep","label":"📰 Deep Adverse Media Analysis","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool performs structured negative-news screening across media sources to surface allegations of financial crime, corruption, or sanctions evasion tied to a customer. The MLRO uses it to inform risk rating and EDD decisions.","steps":["Search reputable media and structured negative-news sources for the subject and close associates.","Confirm true matches by reconciling identifiers and discarding false positives.","Categorise findings by predicate relevance, recency, and credibility of the source.","Reflect material adverse media in the customer risk assessment and EDD scope.","Document the disposition and escalate to STR where credible criminal allegations exist."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires ongoing CDD and risk assessment informed by all relevant information about the customer."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires understanding customer risk, supported by adverse information gathering."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Adverse media is a key input to identifying PEPs and corruption-related higher risk."}]}},{"id":"sow-substantiator","label":"💼 Source of Wealth Substantiator","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool tests whether a customer's stated source of wealth is plausible and corroborated by independent evidence. The MLRO uses it during EDD for higher-risk and PEP relationships to satisfy the SoW obligation.","steps":["Record the customer's stated origin of overall wealth and the activities that generated it.","Collect corroborating documents such as business ownership, sale proceeds, inheritance, or remuneration records.","Assess whether documented wealth is consistent with the customer's profile and account activity.","Flag unexplained gaps between declared wealth and observed funds for further enquiry.","Document the SoW conclusion and escalate where wealth cannot be plausibly substantiated."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence including establishing source of wealth and funds for higher-risk customers."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires establishing source of wealth and funds for PEP relationships."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires understanding the nature and purpose of the relationship to assess fund plausibility."}]}},{"id":"corporate-registry","label":"🏢 Corporate Registry Crosscheck","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool reconciles a corporate customer's declared particulars against official company registry records onshore and in free zones. The MLRO uses it to verify legal existence, status, and registered officers.","steps":["Retrieve the entity's registry record using its trade licence or registration number.","Confirm legal name, status, incorporation date, and registered address match what the customer declared.","Verify listed directors, managers, and shareholders against the CDD file.","Flag struck-off, dormant, or suspended status and any recent rapid ownership changes.","Record discrepancies and require resolution before proceeding with the relationship."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires verifying the legal status and identity of legal-person customers from reliable independent sources."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires identifying and verifying legal-person customers and their control structure."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires accurate and current basic and beneficial ownership information for legal persons."}]}},{"id":"entity-resolution","label":"🔍 Entity Resolution Engine","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool consolidates fragmented records to determine whether multiple identifiers refer to the same underlying person or entity. The MLRO uses it to avoid screening gaps and to build a single accurate customer view.","steps":["Aggregate candidate records across systems using names, identifiers, and addresses.","Resolve matches and aliases to a single entity while separating genuinely distinct parties.","Confirm resolved entities are screened as one for sanctions, PEP, and adverse media.","Detect hidden links between accounts that indicate structuring or layering networks.","Document the resolved entity map and feed it into ongoing monitoring and risk rating."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires accurate identification and ongoing monitoring across the whole customer relationship."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires reliable customer identification, which depends on resolving fragmented records."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Supports a risk-based approach that requires a complete and accurate view of customers."}]}},{"id":"politically-sensitive","label":"🎖 Politically Sensitive Person Mapper","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool identifies and maps politically exposed persons, their family members, and close associates connected to a customer. The MLRO uses it to apply senior-management approval and PEP-level enhanced due diligence.","steps":["Screen the customer and connected parties against foreign, domestic, and international organisation PEP categories.","Map family members and known close associates linked to the identified PEP.","Obtain senior management approval to establish or continue the relationship.","Apply enhanced ongoing monitoring and establish source of wealth and funds.","Document PEP classification and review periodically as roles change."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires PEP identification, senior-management approval, and enhanced due diligence."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Sets PEP measures including family and close associates and source-of-wealth checks."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Extends PEP requirements to relevant designated non-financial businesses and professions."}]}},{"id":"dual-nationality","label":"🌐 Dual Nationality Risk Analyser","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses the risk arising where a customer holds multiple nationalities, including exposure to sanctioned or high-risk jurisdictions and citizenship-by-investment routes. The MLRO uses it to refine geographic risk and screening scope.","steps":["Capture all nationalities, passports, and residences the customer holds.","Screen each nationality and travel document against sanctions and high-risk jurisdiction lists.","Assess citizenship-by-investment or recently acquired nationalities for elevated risk.","Ensure screening uses every identity document, not only the primary nationality.","Adjust the customer risk rating and document the multi-nationality exposure."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires identifying geographic and jurisdictional risk factors in customer due diligence."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires identifying and verifying identity, including all nationalities held."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 19","note":"Requires attention to exposure to higher-risk jurisdictions a nationality may signal."}]}},{"id":"high-risk-profession","label":"💼 High-Risk Profession Screener","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool flags customers whose occupation carries elevated money-laundering risk, such as cash-intensive businesses, arms dealers, or precious-metals traders. The MLRO uses it to scale due diligence to occupational risk.","steps":["Capture the customer's occupation or business activity and map it to a risk tier.","Flag cash-intensive, dual-use goods, gatekeeper, and high-value-asset professions.","Apply enhanced due diligence and tighter monitoring thresholds to higher-risk occupations.","Confirm expected activity is consistent with the stated profession.","Document the occupational risk assessment in the customer risk rating."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires assessing customer risk including occupation and business activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based approach that accounts for customer and occupational risk factors."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Identifies designated non-financial businesses and professions carrying inherent ML risk."}]}},{"id":"local-kyc-requirements","label":"📋 UAE/Local KYC Requirements Guide","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool sets out the specific KYC and CDD evidence the UAE framework requires for natural and legal persons. The MLRO uses it to confirm an onboarding file is complete against local obligations.","steps":["Determine whether the customer is a natural person, legal person, or legal arrangement.","Compile the required identity, address, and beneficial-ownership evidence for that customer type.","Confirm verification used reliable, independent UAE-recognised sources.","Apply the correct CDD tier based on the customer risk rating.","Document completeness of the file before activating the relationship."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Sets the UAE customer due diligence and record-keeping obligations this guide operationalises."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019 (Implementing Regulation)","note":"Specifies detailed CDD, identification, and verification requirements for UAE entities."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Provides the international CDD baseline reflected in UAE requirements."}]}},{"id":"ekyc-risk","label":"📱 eKYC Risk Assessment","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool evaluates the residual risk of onboarding a customer remotely through electronic KYC channels. The MLRO uses it to decide whether non-face-to-face verification meets the required assurance.","steps":["Identify the eKYC channel used and the identity assurance level it provides.","Assess non-face-to-face risk factors and whether liveness and document authentication were applied.","Determine whether additional or step-up verification is needed for higher-risk profiles.","Confirm screening and beneficial-ownership checks were completed within the eKYC flow.","Document residual risk and approve, escalate, or decline accordingly."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires identity verification from reliable sources, including for non-face-to-face customers."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Treats non-face-to-face onboarding as a potential higher-risk scenario requiring controls."},{"ref":"FATF Guidance on Digital Identity","note":"Sets assurance and risk expectations for remote electronic onboarding."}]}},{"id":"perpetual-kyc","label":"♻️ Perpetual KYC Trigger Analyser","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool identifies events that should trigger a refresh of a customer's CDD in a perpetual KYC model rather than on fixed periodic cycles. The MLRO uses it to keep customer information current and risk-aligned.","steps":["Define the trigger events such as risk-rating change, ownership change, or unusual activity.","Detect when a trigger fires and queue the affected customer for review.","Re-verify the elements affected by the trigger and update the risk rating.","Confirm screening and beneficial-ownership data remain current after the event.","Document the refreshed CDD and the trigger that prompted it."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires keeping CDD information up to date and conducting ongoing due diligence."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing due diligence and keeping documents and data current."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Supports a risk-based approach to the timing and intensity of CDD refreshes."}]}},{"id":"beneficial-ownership-calc","label":"🏛 Beneficial Ownership Calculator","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool traces ownership and control through layered structures to identify ultimate beneficial owners above the applicable threshold. The MLRO uses it to satisfy UBO identification for legal persons and arrangements.","steps":["Map the full ownership chain from the customer up to natural persons.","Calculate aggregate direct and indirect ownership percentages at each tier.","Identify natural persons meeting the controlling ownership threshold or exercising control by other means.","Apply the senior managing official fallback where no UBO can be identified through ownership.","Verify and document each identified UBO and screen them accordingly."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires identifying and verifying the beneficial owners of legal persons and arrangements."},{"ref":"Cabinet Decision No. 58 of 2020 (Beneficial Owner Procedures)","note":"Sets UAE beneficial ownership identification, threshold, and registry requirements."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires accurate, current beneficial ownership information for legal persons."}]}},{"id":"corporate-complexity","label":"🔀 Corporate Complexity Scorer","group":"Advanced KYC/CDD","play":{"intro":"This tool scores how opaque a corporate structure is based on layering, cross-border tiers, nominee use, and bearer arrangements. The MLRO uses it to detect structures designed to obscure beneficial ownership.","steps":["Map the number of ownership layers and the jurisdictions in each tier.","Flag nominee directors or shareholders, bearer instruments, and shell-company indicators.","Score complexity and assess whether it has a legitimate commercial rationale.","Apply enhanced due diligence to structures with high opacity scores.","Document the complexity assessment and any unexplained structuring concerns."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires understanding ownership and control structure and applying EDD to higher risk."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Targets misuse of legal persons and opaque ownership structures."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires understanding the purpose and structure of legal-person customers."}]}},{"id":"velocity-analyzer","label":"⚡ Transaction Velocity Analyser","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool measures the speed and frequency of transactions over short windows to detect rapid movement of funds inconsistent with a customer's profile. The MLRO uses it to spot layering and bust-out behaviour.","steps":["Establish the customer's expected transaction frequency and volume baseline.","Measure transaction counts and values over short rolling windows.","Flag sudden spikes in velocity that exceed the expected profile.","Correlate high-velocity bursts with rapid in-and-out fund movement.","Investigate flagged activity and file an STR where layering is suspected."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires ongoing monitoring and reporting of transactions inconsistent with the customer profile."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing scrutiny of transactions against expected customer activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting of suspicious transactions identified through monitoring."}]}},{"id":"peer-group-anomaly","label":"👥 Peer Group Anomaly Detector","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool compares a customer's activity against peers in the same segment to surface behaviour that deviates from the group norm. The MLRO uses it to detect outliers that single-account rules miss.","steps":["Assign the customer to a peer group by profile, product, and business type.","Compare the customer's volumes and patterns against peer-group benchmarks.","Flag statistically significant deviations from peer behaviour.","Determine whether the deviation has a legitimate explanation or indicates laundering.","Document the analysis and escalate confirmed anomalies for STR review."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires monitoring transactions for consistency with the customer's expected activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing scrutiny to ensure transactions match the customer's risk profile."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Supports a risk-based approach using peer benchmarking to allocate scrutiny."}]}},{"id":"round-trip-detector","label":"🔄 Round-Trip Transaction Detector","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool detects funds that leave an account and return through intermediaries with no genuine economic purpose. The MLRO uses it to identify layering and sham trading designed to disguise origin.","steps":["Trace outbound flows and match them to inbound returns across linked parties.","Test whether the circular flow lacks a genuine commercial or economic rationale.","Identify intermediaries used to obscure the round-trip path.","Assess whether the pattern indicates layering, tax abuse, or sham trade.","Document the round-trip findings and file an STR where laundering is suspected."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires reporting transactions suspected of laundering or lacking economic purpose."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires examining the background and purpose of unusual and complex transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting suspicious transactions identified through monitoring."}]}},{"id":"funnel-account","label":"🌀 Funnel Account Detector","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool identifies accounts that receive many deposits, often from dispersed locations, that are quickly withdrawn or moved elsewhere. The MLRO uses it to detect funnel-account schemes used to aggregate and forward illicit funds.","steps":["Identify accounts with numerous inbound deposits from multiple parties or geographies.","Measure how quickly aggregated funds are withdrawn or transferred out.","Flag geographic dispersion between deposit and withdrawal points.","Assess whether activity matches the customer's stated business purpose.","Investigate and file an STR where funnel-account laundering is indicated."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires monitoring and reporting transactions inconsistent with the customer profile."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires scrutiny of complex and unusual fund-aggregation patterns."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt suspicious transaction reporting for detected schemes."}]}},{"id":"dormant-reactivation","label":"😴 Dormant Account Reactivation Alert","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool alerts when a long-inactive account suddenly resumes activity, a common indicator of account takeover or money-mule use. The MLRO uses it to scrutinise unexpected reactivations.","steps":["Detect accounts moving from dormant status to renewed transactional activity.","Compare the new activity against the account's historical baseline.","Verify the account holder and confirm the legitimacy of the reactivation.","Flag immediate large inflows or outflows following reactivation.","Investigate and escalate to STR where takeover or mule activity is suspected."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires ongoing monitoring and reporting of unusual account activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing scrutiny of transactions throughout the relationship."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting suspicious activity such as anomalous reactivations."}]}},{"id":"currency-mismatch","label":"💱 Currency Mismatch Analyser","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool flags transactions whose currencies are inconsistent with the customer's stated business, geography, or counterparties. The MLRO uses it to detect trade-based laundering and disguised cross-border flows.","steps":["Identify the currencies used and compare them to the customer's expected operating currencies.","Flag unexplained use of unrelated or high-risk-jurisdiction currencies.","Assess whether currency conversions add unnecessary layers without rationale.","Correlate mismatches with trade documents and counterparty locations.","Document findings and escalate suspected trade-based laundering for STR review."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires scrutiny and reporting of transactions inconsistent with the customer profile."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires examining unusual transactions lacking apparent economic purpose."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting suspicious transactions, including disguised cross-border flows."}]}},{"id":"ach-fraud","label":"🏦 ACH / Direct Debit Fraud Detector","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool detects unauthorised or fraudulent ACH and direct-debit activity such as unauthorised pulls, return abuse, and mule collection. The MLRO uses it to identify fraud-linked proceeds entering the account.","steps":["Monitor direct-debit and ACH flows for unauthorised or unusual mandates.","Flag elevated return and reversal rates indicating fraud or testing.","Identify accounts collecting funds from multiple unrelated payers.","Confirm whether mandates and authorisations are genuine.","Investigate and file an STR where fraud proceeds or mule activity are suspected."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires reporting proceeds-of-crime activity, including fraud-linked flows."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing monitoring of payment activity against expected behaviour."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting suspicious transactions involving fraud proceeds."}]}},{"id":"wire-transfer-risk","label":"📡 Wire Transfer Risk Scorer","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool scores wire transfers using amount, jurisdiction, counterparty, and originator and beneficiary information completeness. The MLRO uses it to prioritise high-risk wires and enforce the travel rule.","steps":["Confirm required originator and beneficiary information is present and complete.","Score the wire against amount, jurisdiction, and counterparty risk factors.","Flag missing or meaningless payment-chain information for follow-up.","Hold or escalate high-scoring wires for enhanced review before processing.","Document the scoring decision and report suspicious wires."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires controls over wire transfers and reporting of suspicious cross-border flows."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Requires complete originator and beneficiary information to accompany wire transfers (travel rule)."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing scrutiny of transactions including wires against profile."}]}},{"id":"high-freq-trading","label":"📈 High-Frequency Trading Abuse","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool detects abusive high-frequency trading patterns such as layering, spoofing, and wash trades used to manipulate markets or launder funds. The MLRO uses it to identify market-abuse-linked proceeds.","steps":["Monitor rapid order placement and cancellation patterns indicative of spoofing or layering.","Identify wash trades and self-matching between related accounts.","Assess whether trading has genuine economic purpose or is manipulative.","Correlate suspicious trading with fund movements suggesting laundering.","Document findings and escalate to STR and market-abuse reporting as required."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Treats market manipulation as a predicate offence whose proceeds must be reported."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 3","note":"Requires criminalising laundering of proceeds from predicate offences such as market abuse."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting suspicious transactions including manipulative trading."}]}},{"id":"payment-routing","label":"🌐 Unusual Payment Routing Analyser","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool analyses payment paths for unnecessary intermediaries, transit through high-risk jurisdictions, and circuitous routing. The MLRO uses it to detect layering designed to obscure fund origin or destination.","steps":["Reconstruct the full payment path including all intermediary institutions.","Flag unnecessary hops or routing through high-risk or unrelated jurisdictions.","Assess whether the routing has a legitimate commercial rationale.","Correlate routing anomalies with the customer's expected payment corridors.","Document findings and escalate suspected layering for STR review."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires scrutiny and reporting of transactions lacking economic purpose."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Requires transparency of information across the payment chain."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires examining complex and unusual routing patterns."}]}},{"id":"refund-arbitrage","label":"🔁 Refund / Chargeback Arbitrage","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool detects abuse of refund and chargeback mechanisms to extract or launder funds, such as paying in then reclaiming via refunds. The MLRO uses it to identify proceeds laundered through merchant or payment channels.","steps":["Monitor refund and chargeback rates relative to genuine purchase activity.","Flag patterns where deposits are systematically recovered through refunds or disputes.","Identify collusion between payers and merchants used to move funds.","Assess whether refunds correspond to genuine returned goods or services.","Investigate and file an STR where refund or chargeback laundering is suspected."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires reporting transactions suspected of laundering proceeds of crime."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires scrutiny of unusual transactions lacking apparent purpose."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting of suspicious refund and chargeback abuse."}]}},{"id":"correspondent-chain","label":"🏦 Correspondent Banking Chain Analyser","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool analyses correspondent banking relationships and nested or downstream chains to surface hidden exposure and shell-bank risk. The MLRO uses it to apply correspondent-banking due diligence and detect nesting.","steps":["Map the correspondent relationship and any nested or downstream respondent banks.","Confirm respondents are not shell banks and assess their AML controls.","Identify payable-through and nested access that bypasses due diligence.","Apply enhanced due diligence and obtain senior-management approval for the relationship.","Document exposure and report suspicious correspondent flows."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence for cross-border correspondent banking relationships."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 13","note":"Sets correspondent banking due diligence requirements and prohibits shell-bank relationships."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting suspicious correspondent banking transactions."}]}},{"id":"remittance-risk","label":"💸 Remittance Risk Analyser","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses money-remittance activity for structuring, high-risk corridors, and unlicensed value transfer. The MLRO uses it to monitor exchange-house and remittance flows common in the UAE market.","steps":["Profile remittance corridors and flag high-risk or sanctioned destinations.","Detect structuring across multiple remittances below reporting thresholds.","Identify potential hawala or unlicensed money or value transfer activity.","Confirm sender, beneficiary, and purpose information is complete.","Investigate and report suspicious remittance patterns."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires monitoring and reporting of suspicious money or value transfer activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 14","note":"Requires licensing and supervision of money or value transfer services."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Requires complete originator and beneficiary information for remittances."}]}},{"id":"atm-pattern","label":"🏧 ATM Pattern Analyser","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool analyses ATM withdrawal and deposit patterns for structuring, cross-border cash-out, and mule cash collection. The MLRO uses it to detect cash-based layering and placement through ATM networks.","steps":["Aggregate ATM activity by card, account, location, and time.","Flag repeated withdrawals just below cash reporting thresholds (structuring).","Detect rapid cross-border or out-of-pattern cash-out activity.","Identify shared devices or locations linking multiple mule accounts.","Investigate and file an STR where cash structuring or mule activity is suspected."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires monitoring and reporting of suspicious cash activity and structuring."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires ongoing scrutiny of cash transactions against the customer profile."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting suspicious cash structuring and withdrawal patterns."}]}},{"id":"casino-chip","label":"🎰 Casino Chip Washing Detector","group":"Transaction Monitoring","play":{"intro":"This tool flags chip-washing patterns where a customer buys casino chips, plays minimally or not at all, then redeems for a clean cheque or wire to disguise dirty cash. An MLRO uses it when reviewing gaming-floor cash-in versus cash-out records for suspected layering.","steps":["Pull the customer's chip purchase, table buy-in, and redemption records and compute the ratio of chips redeemed to chips actually wagered.","Flag low play-through where large cash buy-ins are converted back to cheques, wires, or markers with little genuine gambling activity.","Identify structuring where chip purchases are split below cash reporting thresholds or spread across multiple cages and sessions.","Verify the source of the incoming cash and the identity of the person collecting the cash-out, including any third-party redemptions.","Apply enhanced due diligence and aggregate linked sessions, then escalate to the MLRO for a suspicious transaction report decision.","Preserve cage logs, surveillance references, and chip serial data to support the STR and any FIU follow-up."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Establishes the AML/CFT obligations and the duty to report suspicious transactions to the UAE FIU, applicable to casino and gaming operators."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Brings casinos within designated non-financial businesses and professions and requires customer due diligence on relevant gaming transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires prompt reporting of suspicious transactions, including suspected layering through chip purchase and redemption."}]}},{"id":"luxury-goods","label":"💎 Luxury Goods AML Risk","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses money laundering risk in high-value goods dealing such as watches, jewellery, designer items, and luxury vehicles, focusing on cash purchases and trade-based value transfer. An MLRO uses it to risk-rate dealers and individual high-value sales.","steps":["Identify whether the sale meets the high-value cash threshold and trigger customer due diligence on the buyer and beneficial owner.","Screen the buyer and any third-party payer against sanctions and PEP lists before completing the sale.","Examine payment method for cash, split payments, or third-party settlement that obscures the true purchaser.","Check for resale or export patterns consistent with trade-based laundering or value transfer.","Record source-of-funds evidence and escalate anomalous high-value transactions to the MLRO for STR consideration."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Defines AML/CFT duties for dealers in precious metals and high-value goods, including CDD and suspicious transaction reporting."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Applies CDD requirements to dealers in precious metals and stones and other high-value goods when cash thresholds are met."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Implements AML/CFT controls for designated non-financial businesses including high-value goods dealers, requiring registration and reporting."}]}},{"id":"art-provenance","label":"🎨 Art Market Provenance Checker","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool evaluates art and antiquities transactions for laundering risk by testing provenance, ownership chains, and pricing against market value. An MLRO uses it when a gallery, auction house, or freeport handles high-value or anonymous art sales.","steps":["Reconstruct the provenance chain and verify export, import, and prior ownership documentation for gaps or forgery.","Identify the ultimate buyer and seller behind any intermediary, advisor, or shell entity and screen them for sanctions and PEP exposure.","Compare the transaction price against independent valuation to detect over- or under-valuation used to move value.","Flag freeport storage, rapid resale, or anonymous private sales that obscure ownership.","Document source-of-funds and provenance findings and escalate suspicious lots to the MLRO for STR filing."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Imposes AML/CFT obligations including CDD and reporting on dealers and intermediaries handling high-value art transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Extends CDD obligations to high-value dealers and intermediaries, relevant to art and antiquities markets."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based approach, supporting enhanced scrutiny of high-risk provenance and anonymous art sales."}]}},{"id":"superyacht-jet","label":"✈️ Superyacht / Private Jet Risk","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses laundering and sanctions-evasion risk in superyacht and private jet acquisition, chartering, and ownership through offshore structures. An MLRO uses it when financing, brokering, or servicing high-value mobile assets.","steps":["Trace the ownership structure through any special purpose vehicles, flags of convenience, or registries to the ultimate beneficial owner.","Screen the beneficial owner, charterer, and financiers against sanctions lists given the high sanctions-evasion exposure of these assets.","Verify source of wealth and funds for the purchase, deposit, and ongoing operating costs.","Check registration jurisdiction, flag state, and any rapid title transfers or nominee arrangements that obscure control.","Document the beneficial ownership and source-of-funds analysis and escalate concealment or sanctions matches to the MLRO."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires identification of beneficial owners, sanctions compliance, and STR reporting for high-value asset transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires transparency of beneficial ownership of legal persons used to hold high-value assets."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 7","note":"Requires implementation of targeted financial sanctions relevant to yacht and jet sanctions-evasion risk."}]}},{"id":"agri-commodities","label":"🌾 Agricultural Commodities Risk","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool screens agricultural commodity trades for trade-based money laundering through mis-invoicing, phantom shipments, and circular trading. An MLRO uses it when reviewing commodity trade finance and physical shipment documentation.","steps":["Reconcile invoice value, quantity, and quality against market price and the bill of lading to detect over- or under-invoicing.","Verify that the shipment is genuine by cross-checking shipping documents, ports, and vessel movements for phantom or duplicate trades.","Map the buyer, seller, and intermediaries to detect circular or round-trip trading among related parties.","Screen counterparties and routing jurisdictions for sanctions and high-risk country exposure.","Document the trade-based laundering indicators and escalate to the MLRO for STR consideration and a hold on trade finance disbursement."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Imposes AML/CFT CDD and reporting duties relevant to commodity trade finance and suspicious trade patterns."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based approach to detect trade-based laundering in commodity flows."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Requires complete originator and beneficiary information on cross-border wire transfers funding commodity trades."}]}},{"id":"precious-stones","label":"💠 Precious Stones / Diamonds Risk","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool risk-rates dealers in precious stones and diamonds for laundering and conflict-stone risk, focusing on cash sales, Kimberley Process compliance, and value concealment. An MLRO uses it for DPMS onboarding and transaction review.","steps":["Confirm the dealer is registered as a DPMS and apply CDD where a single or linked cash transaction reaches AED 55,000.","Verify Kimberley Process certification and origin documentation for rough diamonds to exclude conflict stones.","Screen buyers, sellers, and third-party payers against sanctions and PEP lists.","Examine valuations and payment methods for under- or over-valuation and cash structuring below thresholds.","Record source-of-funds evidence and escalate suspicious cash or origin anomalies to the MLRO for STR filing."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Establishes AML/CFT obligations for dealers in precious metals and stones, including CDD and reporting."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Applies CDD to dealers in precious metals and stones for cash transactions at or above the designated threshold."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 10 of 2019","note":"Sets the AED 55,000 cash CDD threshold and reporting framework for precious metals and stones dealers."}]}},{"id":"gaming-sector","label":"🎮 Gaming / iGaming AML","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses AML risk in online and interactive gaming, including deposits, in-game currency, and withdrawals used for layering. An MLRO uses it when monitoring iGaming player accounts and payment flows.","steps":["Analyse deposit and withdrawal patterns for low play-through where funds are deposited and quickly cashed out as clean balances.","Verify player identity and screen against sanctions and PEP lists at onboarding and at withdrawal.","Detect account sharing, mule accounts, and peer-to-peer in-game transfers used to move value between players.","Examine payment instruments for use of multiple cards, prepaid accounts, or third-party wallets that obscure source of funds.","Set funding limits and escalate anomalous deposit-to-withdrawal behaviour to the MLRO for STR consideration."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires CDD, monitoring, and suspicious transaction reporting applicable to gaming and gambling operators."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 22","note":"Brings casinos and gaming operators within DNFBP obligations including CDD on relevant transactions."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires customer due diligence and verification of player identity and funds."}]}},{"id":"fintech-risk","label":"📱 FinTech / Payment Institution Risk","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool evaluates AML/CFT risk in fintechs, payment institutions, and e-money issuers covering onboarding, wallet flows, and wire transfer information. An MLRO uses it when assessing a payment platform's controls and transaction monitoring.","steps":["Map the product flow including wallet top-up, peer-to-peer transfer, and payout to identify layering and mule-account risk.","Verify that originator and beneficiary information accompanies all wire and transfer messages per the travel rule.","Test onboarding KYC, device and IP risk signals, and sanctions and PEP screening coverage.","Review transaction monitoring rules for velocity, structuring, and rapid pass-through funds.","Document control gaps and escalate suspicious account behaviour to the MLRO for STR filing."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Imposes AML/CFT obligations on financial institutions including payment and e-money providers."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Requires complete originator and beneficiary information on electronic transfers, the travel rule."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Requires risk assessment and mitigation for new technologies and payment products."}]}},{"id":"fund-administration","label":"📊 Fund Administration AML Risk","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses AML risk faced by fund administrators handling investor onboarding, subscriptions, and redemptions on behalf of funds. An MLRO uses it to verify reliance arrangements and investor due diligence quality.","steps":["Confirm investor CDD and beneficial ownership identification for each subscription, including nominee and feeder fund layers.","Verify source of funds for subscriptions and screen investors against sanctions and PEP lists.","Test reliance and outsourcing arrangements with the fund manager to confirm responsibility for CDD records.","Monitor subscription and redemption timing for rapid in-and-out flows inconsistent with genuine investment.","Document due diligence gaps and escalate suspicious investor activity to the MLRO for STR consideration."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires CDD, beneficial owner identification, and reporting by entities administering investment funds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires customer due diligence including verification of investors and source of funds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 17","note":"Governs reliance on third parties for CDD, relevant to fund administrator and manager arrangements."}]}},{"id":"private-equity","label":"💰 Private Equity AML Risk","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool evaluates laundering risk in private equity covering limited partner onboarding, source of committed capital, and portfolio acquisition targets. An MLRO uses it across fundraising and deal due diligence.","steps":["Identify each limited partner and look through feeder structures and nominees to the ultimate beneficial owner.","Verify source of committed capital and screen LPs and key portfolio counterparties for sanctions and PEP exposure.","Apply AML due diligence to acquisition targets to detect proceeds of crime entering the fund through portfolio investments.","Review co-investors and capital call flows for opaque or rapidly changing ownership.","Document beneficial ownership and source-of-funds findings and escalate concerns to the MLRO for STR consideration."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Imposes CDD, beneficial ownership, and reporting obligations relevant to private equity fund operators."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires beneficial ownership transparency for legal persons used in PE structures."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires CDD and source of funds verification for investors."}]}},{"id":"hedge-fund","label":"🏦 Hedge Fund Risk Profile","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool builds an AML risk profile for hedge funds covering investor source of funds, prime brokerage flows, and complex trading used to obscure value. An MLRO uses it during fund onboarding and ongoing monitoring.","steps":["Identify investors and look through feeder and master fund layers to verify beneficial ownership.","Verify source of funds and wealth and screen investors and counterparties for sanctions and PEP exposure.","Review prime brokerage and custodian flows for unusual cross-border movements and rapid redemptions.","Assess trading strategy for instruments or jurisdictions that facilitate value concealment.","Document risk rating and escalate suspicious subscription or redemption activity to the MLRO."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Establishes AML/CFT CDD and reporting duties for collective investment operators including hedge funds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires CDD and verification of investor identity and source of funds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based approach to profile and mitigate investor and strategy risk."}]}},{"id":"family-office","label":"🏠 Family Office Risk Assessment","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses AML risk for single and multi-family offices that manage wealth through complex trusts, holding companies, and cross-border structures. An MLRO uses it when onboarding or reviewing high-net-worth family clients.","steps":["Map the family wealth structure across trusts, foundations, and holding companies to identify all beneficial owners and controllers.","Verify source of wealth and funds and apply enhanced due diligence given high-net-worth and cross-border complexity.","Screen family members, controllers, and connected parties for PEP status and sanctions exposure.","Assess use of nominees, intermediaries, and offshore jurisdictions that obscure control.","Document the structure analysis and escalate unexplained wealth or suspicious flows to the MLRO."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires beneficial ownership identification, enhanced due diligence, and reporting for complex client structures."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires transparency of beneficial ownership of legal persons in family wealth structures."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 12","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence for politically exposed persons connected to family clients."}]}},{"id":"free-zone","label":"🏙 UAE Free Zone Entity Risk","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool risk-rates UAE free zone entities for misuse as shells or trade-based laundering vehicles, accounting for free zone registries and substance requirements. An MLRO uses it when onboarding or reviewing free zone clients.","steps":["Identify the free zone authority of incorporation and verify the entity's licence, activity, and beneficial owners against the registry.","Assess economic substance and physical presence to detect shell entities with no genuine operations.","Screen owners, managers, and connected counterparties for sanctions and PEP exposure.","Review trade and banking flows for trade-based laundering, pass-through transactions, or activity inconsistent with the licensed activity.","Document substance and ownership findings and escalate red flags to the MLRO for STR consideration."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Applies AML/CFT obligations including CDD and beneficial ownership to entities operating in UAE free zones."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 24","note":"Requires accurate and current beneficial ownership information for legal persons including free zone entities."},{"ref":"UAE Cabinet Decision No. 58 of 2020","note":"Regulates beneficial owner procedures and registry obligations for UAE companies including those in free zones."}]}},{"id":"foundation-risk","label":"🏛 Foundation / Endowment Risk","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses AML/CFT risk in foundations and endowments that can obscure beneficial ownership or be misused for terrorist financing. An MLRO uses it when onboarding or reviewing foundation and not-for-profit clients.","steps":["Obtain the foundation charter and identify founders, council members, beneficiaries, and ultimate controllers.","Verify the source of endowed funds and the legitimate purpose of the foundation.","Screen controllers, donors, and beneficiaries for sanctions, PEP, and terrorist financing exposure.","Assess disbursement patterns and recipient jurisdictions for diversion or terrorist financing risk.","Document control and purpose findings and escalate suspicious flows to the MLRO for STR consideration."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Imposes AML/CFT obligations including beneficial ownership and reporting on legal arrangements such as foundations."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 8","note":"Addresses terrorist financing abuse of non-profit organisations and requires proportionate oversight."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 25","note":"Requires transparency of beneficial ownership and control of legal arrangements."}]}},{"id":"crowdfunding","label":"🌐 Crowdfunding / ICO Risk","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool evaluates laundering and terrorist financing risk on crowdfunding platforms and initial coin offerings, where many small contributions can disguise illicit value. An MLRO uses it when assessing platform controls and token issuances.","steps":["Verify contributor and issuer identity and apply CDD scaled to contribution size and aggregated activity.","Screen issuers, project promoters, and large contributors for sanctions, PEP, and fraud exposure.","Assess token economics and wallet flows for ICOs, tracing on-chain movement of raised funds.","Detect structuring through many small contributions or anonymous wallets used to obscure source of funds.","Document red flags and escalate suspicious campaigns or token sales to the MLRO for STR consideration."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Applies AML/CFT CDD and reporting duties to platforms raising and transmitting funds including ICOs."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Requires AML/CFT controls over virtual assets and virtual asset service providers including token offerings."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 16","note":"Requires originator and beneficiary information for transfers of funds and virtual assets."}]}},{"id":"p2p-lending","label":"💳 P2P Lending Platform Risk","group":"Sector-Specific","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses AML risk on peer-to-peer lending platforms where lenders and borrowers can be used as conduits for layering and self-funding schemes. An MLRO uses it when reviewing platform onboarding and loan flows.","steps":["Verify lender and borrower identity and beneficial ownership and apply CDD on both sides of each loan.","Detect self-lending or circular arrangements where the same beneficial owner funds and repays loans to move value.","Verify source of lender funds and the legitimate purpose and repayment source of borrowers.","Screen participants and connected parties for sanctions and PEP exposure.","Monitor early repayments and rapid fund movement and escalate suspicious patterns to the MLRO."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Imposes AML/CFT CDD and reporting obligations on lending and fund transmission platforms."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires CDD and source of funds verification for lenders and borrowers."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based approach to identify layering through lending arrangements."}]}},{"id":"geopolitical-risk","label":"🌍 Geopolitical Risk Overlay","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool overlays country, jurisdiction, and geopolitical risk factors onto customers and transactions to flag exposure to high-risk and sanctioned regions. An MLRO uses it to calibrate enhanced due diligence and monitoring.","steps":["Map the customer's nationality, residence, and transaction counterparties to country risk ratings and FATF high-risk lists.","Apply enhanced due diligence and increased monitoring for exposure to high-risk and conflict jurisdictions.","Cross-check routing and intermediary jurisdictions against sanctions regimes and capital controls.","Re-score risk when geopolitical events, new sanctions, or list designations occur.","Document the jurisdiction risk rationale and escalate high exposure to the MLRO for review."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires risk-based controls and enhanced measures for higher-risk countries and counterparties."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 19","note":"Requires enhanced due diligence for customers and transactions from higher-risk countries."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires identification and mitigation of geographic and country risk under a risk-based approach."}]}},{"id":"network-centrality","label":"🕸 Network Centrality Analyser","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool measures the structural importance of entities in a transaction or relationship network using centrality metrics to surface hubs, brokers, and mule clusters. An MLRO uses it to prioritise investigations and identify hidden coordinators.","steps":["Build the entity graph from accounts, parties, and transactions and compute degree, betweenness, and centrality scores.","Identify high-centrality nodes acting as hubs or brokers between otherwise separate clusters.","Examine high-betweenness intermediaries for mule, gatekeeper, or layering roles.","Cross-reference flagged nodes against sanctions, PEP, and prior STR records.","Document the network findings and escalate central suspicious entities to the MLRO for investigation."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires monitoring and investigation of suspicious activity, supported by network analysis of linked parties."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting of suspicious transactions identified through analysis of connected activity."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Supports a risk-based prioritisation of investigations using network risk indicators."}]}},{"id":"follow-the-money","label":"💵 Follow the Money Tracer","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool traces funds across accounts, intermediaries, and jurisdictions to reconstruct the flow of suspected proceeds from source to destination. An MLRO uses it to build the financial trail for an STR or asset tracing.","steps":["Establish the starting point of the suspected funds and chart each onward debit and credit through the chain.","Follow funds across accounts, institutions, and borders, noting conversions, splits, and consolidation points.","Identify placement, layering, and integration stages and the final beneficiaries.","Cross-reference each hop against sanctions, PEP, and high-risk jurisdiction data.","Document the end-to-end flow with dates and amounts and escalate to the MLRO for STR and asset tracing action."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Underpins tracing of criminal proceeds and the duty to report and support confiscation efforts."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 4","note":"Requires measures to identify, trace, and confiscate laundered property and proceeds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 29","note":"Establishes the FIU function that receives and analyses fund-flow intelligence."}]}},{"id":"causal-chain","label":"🔗 Causal Chain Builder","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool constructs the cause-and-effect sequence linking a predicate offence to subsequent transactions and parties to evidence how proceeds were laundered. An MLRO uses it to articulate the laundering narrative in a case file.","steps":["Identify the predicate offence and the originating illicit proceeds as the start of the chain.","Sequence each subsequent event, transaction, and party showing how one step caused or enabled the next.","Link supporting evidence to each node so every causal step is documented and verifiable.","Test alternative explanations to confirm the chain withstands scrutiny.","Document the validated causal narrative and escalate to the MLRO for the STR and any law enforcement referral."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Defines money laundering predicate offences and the linkage between proceeds and laundering conduct."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 3","note":"Requires criminalisation of money laundering covering the link from predicate offence to laundered proceeds."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 30","note":"Supports investigative reconstruction of laundering by competent authorities."}]}},{"id":"evidence-assessment","label":"⚖️ Evidence Strength Assessor","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool grades the strength, reliability, and admissibility of evidence supporting a suspicion before an STR or referral decision. An MLRO uses it to ensure case files meet the threshold for reporting and prosecution support.","steps":["Catalogue each item of evidence and classify it by type, source, and reliability.","Assess corroboration, chain of custody, and whether each item is direct or circumstantial.","Identify evidentiary gaps and conflicting information that weaken the suspicion.","Weigh the overall body of evidence against the reasonable-grounds-to-suspect threshold for reporting.","Document the assessment and recommend STR filing or further investigation to the MLRO."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Sets the suspicion threshold and obligation to report suspicious transactions to the FIU."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 20","note":"Requires reporting where there are reasonable grounds to suspect funds are proceeds of crime."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 33","note":"Supports maintaining quality statistics and records underpinning evidence-based reporting."}]}},{"id":"witness-statement","label":"📜 Witness Statement Analyser","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool analyses witness and interview statements for consistency, corroboration, and red flags relevant to a money laundering investigation. An MLRO uses it when assessing staff, customer, or third-party accounts within a case.","steps":["Extract the key factual assertions from each statement and place them on a timeline.","Cross-check assertions against transaction records and documentary evidence for corroboration or contradiction.","Flag inconsistencies, omissions, and signs of coaching or coordination between witnesses.","Identify follow-up questions and additional evidence needed to resolve gaps.","Document the reliability assessment and feed conclusions into the MLRO's case decision."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Supports investigation and evidence gathering for suspicious activity and reporting obligations."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 30","note":"Requires competent authorities to investigate and gather evidence including witness accounts."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 31","note":"Requires investigative powers to compel and obtain relevant evidence and information."}]}},{"id":"open-source-intel","label":"🔍 OSINT Investigation Guide","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool guides structured open-source intelligence gathering on subjects, entities, and assets to support due diligence and investigations. An MLRO uses it to corroborate identity, beneficial ownership, and adverse media.","steps":["Define the intelligence requirement and list the subjects, entities, and assets to research.","Collect from corporate registries, sanctions and PEP sources, adverse media, and public records.","Corroborate findings across at least two independent sources and record provenance for each item.","Identify undisclosed connections, adverse media, and beneficial ownership leads.","Document sourced findings with citations and escalate material risk to the MLRO."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires CDD and ongoing monitoring that OSINT supports through identity and adverse-media verification."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 10","note":"Requires verification of customer identity and beneficial ownership using reliable independent sources."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Supports a risk-based approach informed by open-source risk intelligence."}]}},{"id":"court-order-drafter","label":"⚖️ Court Order / Production Order Guide","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool guides the preparation and support of court orders and production orders to obtain records, freeze assets, or compel disclosure. An MLRO uses it when supporting law enforcement requests or seeking judicial measures.","steps":["Identify the specific records, accounts, or assets sought and the legal basis for the order.","Assemble the supporting evidence and factual grounds demonstrating relevance and necessity.","Coordinate with legal counsel and the relevant authority to frame the order correctly.","Ensure scope and timing protect the investigation and avoid tipping off the subject.","Document the request package and route it through the MLRO and competent authority channels."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides for production, freezing, and disclosure measures in support of AML/CFT investigations."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 31","note":"Requires powers to compel production of records and obtain access for investigations."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 4","note":"Requires provisional measures and orders to freeze and confiscate property."}]}},{"id":"law-enforcement-liaison","label":"🚔 Law Enforcement Liaison Guide","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool guides structured engagement with law enforcement and the FIU on suspicious cases, information requests, and tipping-off controls. An MLRO uses it to manage referrals and respond to authority requests lawfully.","steps":["Identify the correct authority and channel for the matter, whether FIU, police, or prosecutor.","Prepare a concise case summary with the financial trail and supporting evidence.","Apply tipping-off controls so engagement does not alert the subject.","Track requests, responses, and any required account actions such as holds or freezes.","Document all communications and route decisions through the MLRO for authorisation."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Establishes reporting to the FIU and prohibits tipping off the subject of a report."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 29","note":"Defines the FIU as the central agency for receiving and disseminating reports."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 21","note":"Provides protection for reporting and prohibits tipping off."}]}},{"id":"mutual-legal-assistance","label":"🌐 Mutual Legal Assistance (MLA) Guide","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool guides cross-border evidence and asset cooperation through mutual legal assistance and informal channels. An MLRO uses it when a case requires foreign records, freezing, or witness cooperation.","steps":["Identify the foreign jurisdiction holding the evidence or assets and the applicable treaty or channel.","Determine whether informal FIU-to-FIU cooperation or a formal MLA request is appropriate.","Prepare the request specifying the offence, evidence sought, and dual criminality basis.","Coordinate with the central authority and legal counsel and track the request through to response.","Document the cooperation trail and feed results into the MLRO's case and any asset recovery action."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides for international cooperation, information exchange, and asset measures in AML/CFT cases."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 37","note":"Requires the ability to provide mutual legal assistance in money laundering investigations."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 38","note":"Requires assistance with foreign freezing, seizure, and confiscation requests."}]}},{"id":"asset-recovery","label":"💰 Asset Recovery Strategy","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool develops a strategy to identify, trace, freeze, and recover criminal proceeds and instrumentalities. An MLRO uses it to plan recovery actions alongside an STR or investigation.","steps":["Identify and value the assets and trace them to the suspected proceeds and current holders.","Select the recovery route, whether criminal confiscation, civil recovery, or cross-border action.","Seek provisional measures such as freezing or seizure to prevent dissipation.","Coordinate with authorities and, where assets are abroad, with foreign counterparts.","Document the recovery plan and progress and report through the MLRO and competent authority."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides for freezing, seizure, and confiscation of proceeds and instrumentalities of crime."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 4","note":"Requires powers to identify, trace, freeze, and confiscate laundered property."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 38","note":"Requires cooperation on foreign-based asset freezing and confiscation."}]}},{"id":"forfeiture-analysis","label":"⚖️ Civil / Criminal Forfeiture Analysis","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool analyses whether to pursue civil or criminal forfeiture of suspected proceeds and the evidentiary thresholds for each. An MLRO uses it to advise on the optimal forfeiture pathway.","steps":["Characterise the asset as proceeds, instrumentality, or substitute property eligible for forfeiture.","Compare the criminal conviction-based route against non-conviction-based civil forfeiture for the case.","Assess the evidence against each route's burden of proof and any third-party interests.","Identify provisional measures needed to preserve the asset pending forfeiture.","Document the recommended pathway and route it through the MLRO and competent authority."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Provides for confiscation of proceeds and instrumentalities including value-based and equivalent measures."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 4","note":"Requires confiscation measures including, where appropriate, non-conviction-based forfeiture."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 30","note":"Requires authorities to pursue parallel financial investigation for confiscation."}]}},{"id":"whistleblower-protect","label":"🔔 Whistleblower Protection Assessment","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool assesses the protection, confidentiality, and handling of whistleblower and internal AML disclosures. An MLRO uses it to ensure reporters are protected and disclosures are acted on without retaliation.","steps":["Verify the disclosure channel protects the reporter's identity and is accessible to staff.","Assess that confidentiality and anti-retaliation safeguards are in place and observed.","Triage the disclosure for AML/CFT relevance and route it for investigation while protecting the source.","Track outcomes and ensure no detriment is suffered by a good-faith reporter.","Document the protection assessment and escalate substantive disclosures to the MLRO."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Protects persons reporting suspicions in good faith and supports internal reporting frameworks."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 21","note":"Protects reporting persons from liability and prohibits tipping off."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 18","note":"Requires internal controls and procedures including confidential employee reporting."}]}},{"id":"regtech-assessment","label":"🤖 AML RegTech Assessment","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool evaluates AML RegTech solutions for screening, monitoring, and reporting against control requirements and model governance. An MLRO uses it when selecting, validating, or auditing compliance technology.","steps":["Define the control objective the tool must meet, whether screening, monitoring, or reporting.","Test detection coverage, false-positive rates, and tuning against the institution's risk profile.","Assess data quality, model governance, explainability, and auditability of outputs.","Validate that screening lists and rules are current and that alerts feed the case workflow.","Document the assessment and recommend approval, tuning, or remediation to the MLRO."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires effective systems for monitoring, screening, and reporting that RegTech tools must support."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Requires assessment and mitigation of risks from new technologies used for AML/CFT."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires controls commensurate with risk, supporting risk-based RegTech validation."}]}},{"id":"aml-innovation","label":"🚀 AML Innovation / Technology Roadmap","group":"Advanced Analytics & Investigation","play":{"intro":"This tool helps plan a phased roadmap for adopting AML technology and innovation aligned to risk and regulatory expectations. An MLRO uses it to prioritise investment in detection, automation, and analytics.","steps":["Assess current control maturity and identify gaps in screening, monitoring, and reporting.","Map candidate innovations such as analytics, network detection, and automation to those gaps.","Prioritise initiatives by risk reduction, regulatory expectation, and feasibility.","Embed model governance, data quality, and validation checkpoints into the roadmap.","Document the phased plan with owners and milestones and present it to the MLRO and board."],"refs":[{"ref":"UAE Federal Decree-Law No. 10 of 2025","note":"Requires effective and proportionate AML/CFT systems that a technology roadmap should strengthen."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 15","note":"Requires proactive assessment of new technologies and associated risks."},{"ref":"FATF Recommendation 1","note":"Requires a risk-based approach guiding prioritisation of AML technology investment."}]}}];
